      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-07-22_WidnesExaminer_art0009" n="art0009">
        <head>WOMEN'S CHAT.</head>
        <p>WOMEN'S CHAT.</p>
        <p>The Royal Children.---With the birth of a fifth son to 'the Prince and Princess of Wales, there is little cause for fear concerning the succession, and with a less wise, and far-seeing mother, the little Princess Mary would appear to be in a fair way to becoming spoiled. But no favouritism is allowed in the Royal nurseries, and unquestioning obedience is a rule that must never be broken. The result is, a group of the most charming children whose winsome,unaffected manners are delight- ful to behold? Very many Society mothers would do 'well to follow in the footsteps of the Princess of Wale, who personally supeAntends every detail 'of her nurseries, in which every hour of her leisure time is spent. A Great Heiress.—lt was expected that an interesting announcement w ould have synchronised with the Hamil- ton ball, which Mary Duchess of Hamilton was to have given in London, but to the great disappointment of many, the ball did not take place. After the death of the twelfth Duke, in 1895' the Duchess of Hamilton married again, her second husband being Mr. Carnaby -Foster, and her daughter Lady Mary Hamilton is one of the greatest heiresses in the Kingdom. She it is who ' owns the beautiful isle of Arran, a seaport domain, which is perhaps unrivalled in its attractions of moun- tain moor and stream. The Duchess is a daughter of the Duchess of Devonshire by her first husband, the seventh Duke of Manchester. She is DOG very fond of London society,and usually spends theautunan withher daughter in Scotland. Both are keen sportswomen, and Lady Mary Hamilton holds the" Mastership "of the Hamil- ton Harriers. The Prime Minister and his Sister.—Mr. A. J. Balfour, who celebrates his fifty-seventh birthday the 25th of this month, is an excellent musician, and when at Whittinghame, spends almost as much time at the piano as at his books. For mach of the leisure he enjoys be is indebted to his sister Miss Alice Balfour, -who looks after his estates and tenantary, and practi- cally manages the whole of his private affairs. This is a work of no small magnitude, seeing that the Prime Minister owns some 90,000 acres in Scotland. and in addition Miss Balfour has to look after the enter- taining and a great deal of routine work at the official residence in Downing-street. If Mr. Balfour is fre- quently the victim of colds and attacks of influenza, it is not for want of looking after by his sister in every- thing appertaining to his daily life. It has been said that Mr. Balfour would be a younger man if he were .not a bachelor, but certainly he lacks nothing as regards watchful care over everything connected with his comfort and advantage. The Social Side of Parliament.—The terrace of the Houses of Parliament has not been so crowded this season, and it almost looks as if it were losing some of its popularity as a fashionable rendezvous for afternoon tea. This perhaps has encouraged the suggestion that a band should play on the terrace in the afternoons. It is not the first time that such a suggestion has been made, and dismissed, by our legislators as frivolous and insulted to the occasion—yet perhaps it is not so inappropriate, even if we consider the purely business its apart from the social view of Parliament. Music is very often an effective stimulant to flagging energies, as witness the fact that it is now provided with this inten- tion in many of the American factories, and of all the institutions in the country, the Houses of Parliament are certainly not theleast to suffer from chronic inertia. Lady Cricketers—Cricket appears to be increasing in popularity with the fair sex, and the match between Lady Kathleen Pilkington's team and the Wakefield Girls' High School, showed how excellently the game can be played by women and girls who .hove had any traiaing in the • cricket field. In Australia the game is played a great deal by girls, and in the matches which are frequently played between the various clubs and colleges, some really first class cricket is often seen. There is talk in Australia of sending over a lady's team during the next season or two, and, if that is arranged there will probably be no ditliculty in getting together a home -team of profesional cricketers to meet them. If it were not for the inconvenience of women's dress for cricket as also for most other outdoor games, there is no doubt that the game would be much more played by women and girls than it is. The world of cricket however owes something to the awkwardness of skirts, as it is believed that it was a girl who first invented round arm bowling, owing to the trouble she saperienced in bowling underhand like her brothers. Shoes and Petticoats.—Footgear, and petticoats are two items to which the girl or woman who intends spending her holidays at a fashionable seaside resort, should pay special attention. To commence with the former, none taking a larger size than a three should indulge in white shoes. Even the daintiest foot is not improved by white footgear, and when a large four or a five is in question the effect is disastrous in the extreme. Dark brown shoes and hoots should even be avoided by girls end women who take anything larger than a small fonr,—black footgear being the safest and best wear for such individuals. With regard to petticoats, lawn speci- mens, trimmed with medallions of embroidery and tucks, are particularly suitable. Lace trimmed petticoats save in their first freshness are undesirable, moreover the lace, to look well, must he of excellent quality. A silk slip, with flounces of various descriptions is how- ever the most valuable of all possessions in the under- skirt department, as one can always, at the shortest possible notice button the flounce best suited to the weather, and the occasion. Risks in Paddliug.—A large percentage of mothers appear to be under the impression that sea water can do their little ones no harm, and that however wet the small people's clothes may become when paddling, there is no need of precattion of any kind. This is a mistake and waterproof knickers to draw on over petticoats and skirts, or little suits, should always be provided in the outfit for the seaside. Another mistake is that of allowing children to wade and paddle in the blazing sun, with only a cap upon the head. Shady headgear should be donned always under such circum- stances, and fewer rashes, sick headaches, and other ailments would result, if childien were kept somewhat quiet and as much as possible out of the sun, during the hottest portion of the day. Concerning Fans.—Whether the popularity of the fan is due to the visit of the King of Spain, or the hot weather is an open question, but the Sect remains that at no period of the day is the fashionable woman seen without her fan. Quite small specimens are preferred and these though so light in the hand can be made to produce quite a pleasant little breeze when briskly used. Glove fans too, which can be carried in the palm of the hand are modish, but it rant be confessed that this particular variety is very much more ornamental than useful. At many of the fashionable restaurants a fan is presented to every lady—the merest trifle it is true, and inexpensive, but wonderfully pretty and artistic. Maidservants in Liverv.--Housemaids and domestic servants are only too ready as a rule to put aside their distinctive form of dress, and it may be doubted whether service will have any more attraction for them, if put into gorgeous liveries. This innovation has been adopted at some of the great houses in London, though with what object is not very apparent, except it is the one very common among the newly rich, of showing their friends that money is no object. By some unfortunate process, housework has come to be regarded as menial and derocatory,andhovvever gorgeous her uniform might be, the housemaid would never be proud of it, as her young man the soldier is supposed to he proud of his.</p>
        <p>MA DOE.</p>
        <p>LIFEBOAT DEMONSTRATION AT EARLESTOWN.</p>
        <p>On Saturday afternoon a brilliant demonstration was held at Earlestown and Newton-le-Willows in aid of the National Lifeboat Fund. The procession proved to be the most varied, lengthy, and imposing seen in the neighbourhood. The procession in which were the Earlestown Prize Band and Liverpool Farm School Band went to afield adjoining Newton Lake. There the life- boat was launched, and an exhibition of life-saving given by the crew, followed by a display of fire-fighting by the four brigades, spectacular dancing and dramatic performances in a large marquee by members of the Earlestown Dramatic Society, and tugs-of-war by the &amp;amen.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-07-22_WidnesExaminer_art0010" n="art0010">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, July 22, 1905.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, July 22, 1905.</p>
        <p>iL ASMON, larrindon Se., London.</p>
        <p>To say 'that one ,possesses a smart muslin frock does not always mean that one has spent a ruinous sum on these dainty but flimsy addi- tions to one's wardrobe. In fact, it is an economy to wear them during the summer, for they are rinexponsive if made up at home, and can so easily be freshened up for some special occasion by a visit to the laundress. Our sketch shows a white mercerised muslin dotted with</p>
        <p>pale blue spots. The bodice is absolutely plain, and the pointed collar or berthe is gathered on beneath a shaped band of pale blue mercerised muslin, edged with frillings of Valenciennes lace. The short puff sleeves are caught in above the elbow with a corresponding band. The belt may be a perfectly plain Swiss belt, lacing up the back, or a draped one; while a broad belt in blue suede would look very smart. The full skirt is plain, save for two vandyked bands of the blue muslin.</p>
        <p>A COMPARISON.</p>
        <p>There are a good many French boners engaged in London just now, writes "Hebe" in the Gentle- woman. I see them when I take my walks abroad in the Park, and view them with admiration and horror—admiration for their broad shoulders, their healthy appearance and strength; and horror when I compare them with Englishwomen of their class —the weak, ansemic, narrow - chested English- woman. A lady, a philanthropist, who has the fate of the working classes on the brain, tells me that malnutrition in early years is the source of all the woe of the young servants and shop-girls, &amp;c., of the day. This love of finery, this abominable "picturesque" dressing, and gadding about of the mothers are the reasons why they have no money left to provide nourishing food for their little ones. And the teapot! It is doing a dreadful I work in replacing porridge, milk and potatoes, and cheese in Great Britain from Land's End to John o' Groats. It rejoices me to learn that women who have studied the subject are to have 11 official power to look into the matter of national feeding,, and "help stave off the decadence of the Empire.'</p>
        <p>LL'S Pate SELF—RAISING FLOUR ONCE TRIM) ALWAYS USED. WOMAN'S REALM. THE BLACK DRESS—FASHION—HOME. MOTOR CAR. • i By J. B. HARRIS BURLAND. AT THE SUMMER SALES. A wise woman never joins the panting, 'pushing CHAPTER XXV.—(Continned., mob on the early days of a summer sale, writes "A Society Butterfly." But now, in mid fluty, she can Half a mile away from the parting of -the spend her money to the best advantage. Pretty roads they were so close to Jordison's car that parasols can be picked up for a few shillings; and they could hear the hissing of the wheels and some of the best feather boas, once priced at many the sound of voices. A second later the white pounds, are to be bought for a mere trifle. Gloves strip of road vanished, as suddenly as though are an important adjunct; and white kid gloves, it had been blotted off the map, and a veil of sixteen-button length, must be worn with our short-sleeved summer gowns. These are expensive, darkness descended Over the whole scene. but can be procured at a cheaper rate at sale times. Nothing was visible but the fan of yellow light Stockings also belong to our stern necessities; and that swept on before them. But they cduld this is a good moment for laying in a store of black still hear the sound of wheejs on the soft road. and brown silk, said a few of the ever useful Lisle It iwould, however, be impossible to tell thread description. A word on what to avoid may whether Jordison had taken the right or left ant be out of place. Deticate,.pale-coloured fabrics turning until they had shot well past the sign sllould be passed by: no one wants Soiled hats and ES Bles, crumpled chiffons and mangled muslins. s a wrong ,DO And turning at this point Aad ready-made gowns are seldom a wise purchase; would mean the loss of at ,least a mile in the on the other hand, ready-made coats are often chase. excellently made, and commend themselves even to VI Lord Harry .Ouy peered ahead, but could a well-dressed woman. see nothing. Their own lamps illuminated 1 .lusrm DRESS. the roadfor nearly fifty yards in front of them, but beyond that all was 'darkness. " Which is .it to be, he said. quickly, "the right or left ? " For answer • Holme took a •coin from his pocket and held it between his onen palms. ' Heads right, taits left," he S-ald. • " Very well," I.ord Henry replied, it is . . . just as good a way as, any' otheiY Hohne eSc- posed the coin, mid held it down to one of the side lamps. "Heads' he said, and replaced the coin in his pocke:. Lord Parry took the car to the right of the road, an, a few seconds later the white sign post flasaed up like a ghost on their left and vanished. It was an anxious moment. Both men listened attentively. Then Lord Harry burst out with a " Thank God." The faint noise in' front of them continued. Fortune had been with them in tiii3 ore thing,: at any rate. " 'We shall overta:;.e it now," said Lord Harry ; " there' is not another break in the road for ten miles, and then he dare not take it. He would have to ,slow down to take a right angle turn. For thirty miles he \vitt have to go straight ahead on the north road." " And what do we do when we overtake it," pstr-d TT: '</p>
        <p>'alongside and talk." rin afraid Jordison won't waste on •us," he said drily, " and think that it. would be advisable to get too close to him. We had better hang on his heels till daylight and give the alarm. The news will be all over England in an hour, and he cannot possibly 'escape."</p>
        <p>" 'You are probably right". Lord Harry answered, " but I intend to have a go al. him myself. I wish to meet tllo gentleman _ .</p>
        <p>Few that have Met him wish to do so again," Holme replied, and the two Men relapsed into silence. The two cars tore on through the night. The hedge rows ran past in streaming linos ot brown, and the road spun toward:: them like a strip of paper from a tape machine. - jordison re-lit his lamps and the of them ran before the pursuers like a Cloud of fire. Up gentle slopes and down long gradients and aver wide stretches of level ground, they rushed at the same headlong speed. sleeping villages burst into sight and vanished, like scenes that are disclosed from darkness by a flash of lightning. And inch by inch the grey car crept up to the object of its pursuit, till at last a black Mass of metal crept out of the darkness into the edge of the broad fan of light that streamed from the Bleriot larnzs._</p>
        <p>-" It's a long road," said Lord Harry Quy, but the end of it is in sight." _</p>
        <p>Further and still further the black car crept into the light. The two young men could now see the backs of the occ.upants. A 'tall. man had his hands on the steering gear and a- small squat figure looked back at them. They could se .his face, white in the glare of the lamps, coarse feat wed, cad evil. , " Lipp," said Holrne, as though in answer to a silent query on the part ot his companion; "we've a tough couple to deal with, Quy. I wish I had my revolver. It's the, devil's own luck that I left it in Up Withing, when we went off in such a huary.' "You've two hands," tcr;ed ,Lord Harry, " and I wouldn't care to have my throat in your fingers." Holme smiled. Lord Harry had not been in the clutches of William Jordi- Eon.</p>
        <p>Then suddenly the squat figure turned right round and flung his arm over the back of the car. A second later there was a flash and a report, and a bullet sang past Lord Harry's 'ear.</p>
        <p>.He laughed. He was strung up to so high a pitch of excitement that he would have faced a Maxim gun without fear._</p>
        <p>There was another flash and yet another, and two bullets in quick succession struck the ground in front of the car. Lord Harry laughed again. The aim was so palpably ridiculous. He chuckled to see his opponents ding away their ammunition on an inyukner- able road.</p>
        <p>There was another flash and a metallic ring as the bullet struck the honeycombing in front of the bonnet. The cars were now a bare twenty yards apart. "Keep back," cried Holme, "we can hang on their heels all right till daylight." in" Keep back, be damned " Aaid Lord Harry; "they can't shbot straight. I will run their cursed oar down in two minutes."</p>
        <p>Holme watched Lipp refill his revolver and take careful aim over the back of the car, and he noticed that the barrel of the revolver pointed downwards. Then there came four flashes one after the other as quick as a man can count. Two struck the road, one hit the hub ot the right hand wheel, and the fourth found its mark. It struck the tyre with a dull thud, and hiss of air. The car sank slowly over, and wrenched the wheel out of Lord Harry's hand. The next instant the tyre was ripped off the wheel like a piece of paper. The great car swerved to the right, and a ton of weal came full tilt into a tree.</p>
        <p>Holme was flung from his seat in a crumpled ball and shot like a bullet into the hedge. Lord Harry Quy, still strapped to his seat, was crushed against the tree like a fly on a wall. In a second of time he had travelsed a longer road than any marked on the map of England. and had found the end of it.</p>
        <p>Then he cursed himself for a coward, an rose to his feet: He shook off the cloud that the darkness had woven about his brain, and his mind once more became ' keen and vigorous, the mind of a man who is in a tight place, and who has to exert all his energies to get out of it. He stepped• cautiously forward, for he knew not what lay before him, It was possible that a precipice might suddenly fall away beneath his feet. He moved several yards in one direction and encountered nothing, Then he turned to the right, and a few steps brought him up against a dead wall, He turned sharply round and retraced his steps, counting them as he walked, When he had taken eight aces he encountered another walld Keep- ing one hand on the stone, he moved along it, expecting -every minute to again meet some further obstacle to his progress. But when he had walked for half an hour he came to the conclusion that he was in a long passage, He quickly obtained confirmation of this, by leaving the wall once more and crossing over to the ,other .side, now some ten paces distant 4 He resolved to explore the passage from end to end, and continued in the same direction; In an. hour's time he stepped into some water. He thought at first that it might be a mere puddle, but when he tried to skirt it he found that. it reached to the opposite wall, Ile placed his • hand against the rock and splashed cautiously forward, The cold water rose to his ankles, and as he proceeded grew , deeper and deeper till it reached his knees, Then he stopped, felt once more in hie pockets for the matches which he always carried with him and searched every inch of the lining carefully for a single match, which is often to be found about the clothes of a con- firmed smoker. But he , was unsuccessfuti 'Matches are Cheap enough in England, but at that moment he would have given half of all his worldly goods for a single box of them, , The darkness was becoming heavy, and suffo- cating like the folds of a velvet pall, He was more hopelessly lost in it than a sailor who , tries to pick his way through an unknown and unlighted channel in a fog. Only the stone of the passage and the cold water were realities, He moved forward another few yards .and the water reached his coat. ,It had begun to rieepen more rapidly. His feet no longer touched the hard rock, but sank into a soft bed of mud or sand. An idea suddenly seized him. He gathered up some of the water in his hands and lapped a few drops with his tongue. The water was salt. He turned round,' and retracing his steps, came back to the dry ground. Then he sat down in his dripping clothes and reasoned things out, it was clear that this unknown tunnel was connected in some way or other with one of the creeks, or even with the sea, It was possible that the pool was merely, an accumu- lation of salt wateriwhich had filterechthrough the rock and earth above, but on the other hand, it was conceivable that the tunnel emerged in the sea. If so, there was a chance that the opening was uncovered at low tided Holme had no means of ascertaining the state of the tide at that particular moment, for he had no idea of the time and did not even know whether it was day or night. It would, however, be easy to find out whether the water was tidal, and if so, whether the tide was rising or falling. He went down to the pool, and placing a shilling on the very edge of the water, waited patiently for the information he required. In a few minutes' time he found that the water had receded from the coins It was clear that the pool was tidal, and that the tide was running out. But it was just possible that the tide had neared its lowest point, and that it would only fall a few inches more, In which case there was no hope of an exit into the open air. He carefully felt the ground running front the water's edge into the tunnel behind him For three yards it was wet, and then his fingers encountered dry rock. The distance ropresented, as far as he could judge, a fall of about six inches in the water. It was, there- fore, evident that the tide had only jest turned. With twelve or fifteen feet to fall it was possible that the mouth of the tunnel might be completely uncovered. He stooped down and peering across the water into the darkness, waited for some glimmer of light, the first indication of escape from this tomb of rock. When he had waited an hour, shivering, hun•gry, and tormented with thirst, it sud- denly occurred to him that there might be a bend in the tunnel, which would absorb the iirst weak rays of the sun, filtering through a mass of green water, He moved several yards forward to the water's edge, but still there was darkness. Then it occurred to him that it might possibly he night, and that there would be no light to guide him. He debated with himself whether after all it would not be better to take off his clothes, plunge boldly into the water, and swim as far as the roof at the tunnel would permit. When another hoar had passed, and still no glimmer of light came from the black wall of darkness, he decided to take this somewhat desperate course. He took off all his clothes and</p>
        <p>DOttOgt and stand with his head above water 4 His naked and shivering body was now almost lifeless with the cold, and he set to work to warm himself. At first he splashed and kicked vigorously in the Water. Then, as the tide receded still farther, he swung his antis to and fro across his chest as cabmen do on a , wintry day in London, and danced up and down on the sandy bottom. He knew that he must keep moving, and although he was al- ' most 'exhausted with hunger and thirst and fatigue, he never rested for sa minute in his exertions to keep ,the blood circulating in his half-frozen body. At last the tide ran out from the tunnel, , and the arch of sky began to fade into a Lighter shade of grey. Then a faint tinge of yellow appeared at the base of it, rising and - rising till _it became an arch of gold. Then the burning rim of the sun appeared, and a ftw minutes later the glory of it poured into the narrow tunnel, and turned the dark walls into a splendour of wet and sparkling rock, blazing with a myriad points of light At that moment Holme realised that the Sim worshippers had a-very solid truth at the root of their superstitious reverence. The rising sun, seen large through the morning mists and almost filling the mouth of the ' tunnel with its glowing sphere, seemed indeed a thing to be worshipped and adored. It was, however, no time for regarding the dawn in any spirit of sentiment. Holme first turned his attention to the great iron gate now standing out firm. and definite against the. light. He examined it quickly and care- fully, his eyes searching keenly for some weak spot in chain or bar. But it was as strong as the gate .of .a medixval. castle. The bars had apparently been fastened together, With steel -screws, and the heads of the screws had been cut off. He shook the gate with , all his strength, till his whole body glowed with the exertion. The chain that secured it to the wall,gave two or three inches each way, and the tunnel resounded with the clash and rattle of the steel links. But Holme saw at a glance 'that it would be almost 'impossible to loosen or break anything. The chain would, have held a hundred ton boat to her moorings. Irre padlock was enormous, of solid steel, and ,fitted with a patents lock. The staples and the hinges were cemented into the rock on either side, and were as firm as' the rock itself. Then his eyes travelled to the top of the gate. It had been impossible to run it quite flush with the uneven surface of the rock, but at no part was there a gap of more than six inches in depth. The bottom of the frame- work was covered with half an inch of sands Holme sank on his knees and scraped a hole under the lowest bar. At the depth of about four inches he came down to the solid rock: He rose to his feet and realised that it would be impossible for him to break' through the gate unless he could obtain help from the other side.- He stared out at the broad expanse of gold across the sea, and tried to find out where- abouts he was on the coast. He knew that he was looking almost due east, for he had seen the sun rise. Eight or nine miles away, on the horizon, two ships, one a brig and the , other a small steamer, were moving north- wards. There was no land in sight, and his keen eyes, trained to observe details that escape the eye of a landsman, could not detect a single boat on the whole expanse of smooth waters. But he noticed that the tide was running out with incredible swiftness, and that already the sea was nearly a quarter of a mile from the mouth of the tunnel, while even farther out bare ridges and islands of sand were rising from the surrounding water. Holme ransacked his memory for some clue to the locality of the scene. He was thorough- ly acquainted with the eoast, as viewed from the deck of a boat, and the turns and depths of every creek and channel were completely charted out in his mind. But he guessed that these shallow fiats lay far inside safe water, even for so smS.ll a vessel as the " Rover," and that probably he had passed no nearer to them than the vessels he saw on the horizon. He knew, however, that he looked east, and that the traffic went north and south. It was also quite clear that his back was to the land, and that he was, therefore, on the east coast. Then he suddenly realised where he was, and cursed himself for a fool for not having grasped the fact before. The rock that sur- rounded him afforded the clue. .Rock was scarce on that coast of marsh land and sand banks. But a few pieces of it were scattered about in one particular district, mere islands of stone that some great upheaval had thrust up from the earth and sand around them. The Red House had been built on one of these, and all the others were within a radius of to miles. This was probably one of the Triplets, three small patches of firm land, a quarter of a mile from a large tract of marsh, and set right•on the edge of the Great East Ray Flats. His heart sank within him as he realised his position. No big vessels ever crossed these shallow and dangerous waters, and no fisher- men ever set their nets or lines on the barren ground. At low tide no man ever set loot on the sands, for between the island and the shore Lay a line of quicksands, . broad and deep enough to have engulfed an army corps. He shuddered, and turning away from the sand, retraced his steps up the tunnel to find his clothes and cover shivering body. As he receded from the entrance, the day passed into twilight, and his own dark shadow blended more and more with the surrounding greyness. When at last he came to the place where he had left his clothes he found, to his surprise, that they had vanished. The shilling still lay on the rock, where he had placed and for- gotten it. He moved a few yards further up the pas- sage, searching every inch of the ground for some trace of his garments. Then suddenly a long white oblong shimmered before him in the half light. He stooped down, and touched some soft material. Then he grasped it and drew it up to him, foot by foot. It was a large white sheet, thick and warm to the touch. Underneath it lay a long black box. He kicked it with his foot, and it rang out hollow. Then he examined it closely, and gave a cry of horror as he realised the shape of it. It was a roughly-made coffin, and the square piece of material which he held in his hands was doubtless intended as a winding sheet. Then he burst into a peal of laughter. The keen wits of a sane man ought surely to prevail over the maniac mind that had cOnceived so gruesome a jest as this.</p>
        <p>_ . walked into the icy water till it reached his waist. Then he struck out boldly. Yet, as he started, the thought came toh im that Jiirdison had brought him to this place, that Jordison had left him unfettered and free to roam where he pleased, and that it was hardly likely that Jordison had left him any loophole for escape. Holine was e powerful swimmer, and he had' no fears for his own safety so long as nothing out of the way interrupted his progress., But it was strange work swimming there in the darkness, hearing the echoing splash of his steady strokes, and bumping against the wet rocks that closed him in on either side. When he had gone a hundred yards he tried to touch the bottom, but found that he was out of his I der h. • Raising his hand above his head, he I touched . the roof, and the impact sent him under for a few seconds. After another hun- dred yards he again lit ted his arm, but found that the roof was no closer to him. At the end of another hundred yards he could not touch it, and sinking suddenly down, reached the sand at the bottom. . Then all at once, out of the darkness came a far off gleani of light, no more than a patch of dark bluish grey against an inky back- ground, but still the first ray from the outer world. - Re swam more quickly and power- fully, and the water bubbled against his head as it bubbles at the bow of a boats In a few minutes' time the patch became lighter and more definite in shape. In ' the joy of his discovery he did not notice the fine network of • lines that crossed it. His head was half buried in the water, and he drove his body forward with long powerful strokes. Then as he lifted his arm out of the water to make a stroke his fingers rapped hard against someelline, slid past it to his elbow, and his whole body came crash against a framework of iron. He raised ids other hand, and catching hold of a stout bar, examined the obstacle which had brought his progress to such an abrupt termination. As far as he could see in the darkness, which could • only have been re- I garded as 'twilight by one who had grown I : accustomed to the absolute ;blackness of the . tunnel, he was confronted by a great screen, made of thick bars of iron, crossed at right angles, and resembling in some measure an ancient portctilF. He went over it carefully with his numbed fingers and lound that the horizontal and transverse bars were firmly rivet ted together. He also discovered that it had hinges on one side, and that on the other side it was fastened to the rock by a rnantivs chain secured with a iadlock. It was, in short, a gate, and its. presence seemed to imply that the passage , was used as a means of egress and ingress, and that for some reason or other th.! who used it were anxivirs to make their underground residence intol a fortress, ite realised .now that he had found , Jordifion's bidi"ng place, but he could not as yet !•:,oe how this exit could be of any possible use to a motor-Car. Fifty yarit:i away from him an arch of grey showed aganist the blackness of the tunnsl. ' tee• Ur, roof awl the water, and fax off he could see the moving lights of a ship. He resolved to wait till day- And as he waited be realised Jordison's cruelty to the full. The man had purposely kit him unfettered in the darkness so that he could make this effort to escape, and find after all his sufferings and exertions this last Lepregns.bie barrier between him and liberty. . i.hali an hour's time les could touch the</p>
        <p>iTo be continuedi</p>
