      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-07-22_WidnesExaminer_art0011" n="art0011">
        <head>SEASIDE COSTUME.</head>
        <p>SEASIDE COSTUME.</p>
        <p>A useful 'seaside or walking costume is shewn in our illustration. The skirt shews the new two-piece skirt, which is a simple way of making up wide-width material. The front seam is cut with three small tabs towards the bottom, and a stitched band gives weight to the hem. The Eton coat is trimmed with a narrow strapping of the material, the little waistcoat and collar being of white pique or cloth, and adorned with</p>
        <p>CHAPTER XXVI. THE TOMB. WHEN 1-101111 e came to his senses and opened his eyes, he was in i darkness. He sat up and tried to collect his thoughts. , At first he could not remember what had happened, then he recollected the long 'chase Hough the night, the swerve of the car, the crash, and small bottons. The sleeves are a modified kg- after that nothing more. of-mutton shape, finished at the wrist with a He had a confused idea in his brain that he ..arrow strap of the material. We would sug- was still lying where he had fallen, and that gest a light grey tweed, with waistcoat and the blackness around him was that of night, collar of white pique; and to wear with it a hat but, as his mind grew clearer, he realised that trimmed with scarlet ribbon and poppies. Of course the cravat and belt should be en suite. no night was ever so dark as this. Then the awful thought burst upon him MODERN Mammas. I that he had been blinded by the accident. He In our childhood our garrulity was checked by : felt in his pocket for some matches, but could the words: "There are some things which ladies i not find the bdx. Then he touched the ground and gentlemen don't talk about." But now, says . by his side. It did not feel to him like the Lady Helen Forbes, it would appear that there are 1 road. It was hard, smooth, and cold like no longer any subjects which we don't talk about. solid rocki it was inconceivable that he We seem to have brought frankness to a fine art : should have fallen on such a place and escaped and with this general relaxing of conversational with his life. He could not imagine where he . principles, we nowadays disregard all the older , had been taken to, but he guessed that Jordi- conventions.of address. I son had some hand in the matter. TO-DAY'S RECIPE. I With the idea of blindness uppermost in his ' KIDNEYS SAUTES.—ChOOSC two large kidneys, ' mind, he unlaced one of his heavy nailed skin, and cut them the round way into thin slices. Have ready a tablespoonful of flour, well boots, and taking it in his hand struck the rocky floor several times with all his strength. seasoned wth pepper and salt, dip each piece of r kidney in it. Cut some neat thin squares of Two o three sparks flashed out from the streaky bacon, fry them very slowly in a little impact, and never had such small sparks of butter. When done, put them in the dish for , light meant iso much to a man. He shouted serving, and keep hot whilst you saute the I with joy, and the noise reverberated like kidneys, which put into the fat the bacon was• . distant thunder till it died away in a whisper. cooked in. Take care the kidneys cook gently; , He thrust' his foot back into the boot and if done fast they will be hard and indigestible. I In about a minute the gravy will begin to rise i laced it up carefully. He realised now that he was in some sub- on the upper side, then turn the kidneys and let i them finish cooking slowly—they will be done terranean cavern or passage, and a new terror in four or five minutes. Put them on the dish seized him. Was it possible that Jordison ,ith tha hallow_ s.e,l ..nour over them a XDOOnft . had thrown him into some underground or two of beef gravy, or water thickened wit 1 a little flour, boiled and mixed with the fat and Prison, which was destined to be his tomb. , gravy from the kidneys in the. frying-pan. Serve ,He pictured to himself a lingering death from tit A het-water +dish. - _ thirst and starvation, with never a glimmer of . _ . ' light to cheer his last moments, and with never a sound to break the silence but the dying gasps of his own breath. ..</p>
