      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-07-22_WidnesExaminer_art0012" n="art0012">
        <head>Warrington Coll Club.</head>
        <p>Warrington Coll Club.</p>
        <p>By the kindness of Mr. T. H. Lyon the members of the Warrington Golf Club have been able to extend their links to the extent 0f.9 holes. This makes the tumid one of eighteen holes. Mr. T. H. Lee and the Pey Father Swarbreck, the finalists for the prize pre- senta by Mr. 11. Greenall, have not yet decidedwhen they will meet. When the event is arranged it will be followed with considerable interest. The qualifying round for the captain's prize will be continued to-day.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-07-22_WidnesExaminer_art0013" n="art0013">
        <p>L</p>
        <p>SCIENCE NOTES AND</p>
        <p>GLEANINGS.</p>
        <p>ENGINEERS Judge of the condition of their machinery by the tone it gives out while running. Every engine, whether stationary or locomotive, has a particular tone of its own; the engineer becomes accustomed to that, and any departure from it at once excites a suspicion that all is not right.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-07-22_WidnesExaminer_art0014" n="art0014">
        <head>ALUMINIUM-COATED PAPER.</head>
        <p>ALUMINIUM-COATED PAPER.</p>
        <p>Aluminium-coated paper, made in Germany, for wrapping food substances, is prepared by applying a thin coat of an alcoholic solution of resin to artificial parchment, then sprinkling aluminium .powder over the surface, and finally submitting to pressure. The artificial parchment is paper that has been treated with sulphuric acid. The aluminium paper is not attacked by the air oe by fats, is much cheaper than tin-foil, and is not poisonous.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-07-22_WidnesExaminer_art0015" n="art0015">
        <head>INCREASE OF A GRAIN *OF WFWAT.</head>
        <p>INCREASE OF A GRAIN *OF WFWAT.</p>
        <p>It is calculated by a German philosopher that a single grain of wheat produces fifty grains, and that these fifty will each produce fifty grains more, and so on. Thus ho finds that the grain would develop In the following way: In the second year ... 2,500 grs. „ third year ... 125,000 grs. „ sixth year ... 15. 625, 000, 000 gra. ~ twelfth year ... 244,140,625,000,000 grs. The third year's crop would give 300 men one meal, leaving enough bran to feed eight pigs for one day. The produce of a single grain in the twelfth year would suffice to supply all the inhabitants of the earth with food during their lifetime.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-07-22_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <head>HUMAN TSETSE FLY DISEASE.</head>
        <p>A HUMAN TSETSE FLY DISEASE.</p>
        <p>Coloner David Bruce, who has been inquiring into the cause, effect, and distribution of "sleeping sickness," states that in certain parts of Uganda, where the disease has broken out, it has reduced a populous and richly cultivated country to a de- populated wilderness. Sleeping sickness is really a disturbance of the functions a the brain. A patient's friends might not notice for years that there was anything the matter. But gradually a slight change becomes evident; he is less inclined to work, and begins to lie about more. In the end the first symptom of the absolutely fatal disease is apparent. The disease, it would seem, was in- troduced from the Conga, and was caused by the entrance into the blood of a protozoal parasite, and the infection was carried by a tsetse fly. The distribution df the fly corresponds with the dis- tribution of the disease. Where there is no fly there is no sleeping sickness. In other words, we are dealing wlth a human tsetse By disease.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-07-22_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>PUZZLE OF THE SUN'S HEAT.</head>
        <p>PUZZLE OF THE SUN'S HEAT.</p>
        <p>We can measure the heat emitted by the sun, says an, American contemporary, because we can measure the amount received by our earth, which litercepts about the 2,300,000,000 th part of all the light and heat of the sun. We thus find that in every second the sun emits as much heat as would result from the combustion of 11,600,000,000,000 tons of coal. Each portion of the sun's surface as large as our earth sends out as much heat per second as would result from the combustion of 1,000,000,000 tons of coal—a simple and easily- remembered relation. If the sun's whole mass con- sisted of coal, and could burn right out to the last ton, maintaining till then the present rate of emission, the supply would not last more than 5,000 years. As the sun has most certainly been emitting light and heat for a far longer period than this, the idea that the solar lire is maintained in any such way is untenable. There are, however, many other reasons for rejecting the idea that the sun is composed of burning matter, using the word "burning" in its proper sense, according to which a piece of coal in a fire is burning, whereas a piece of red-hot iron is not burning, though burning hot.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-07-22_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>MATTER AND FORCE THE SAME.</head>
        <p>MATTER AND FORCE THE SAME.</p>
        <p>- ----- As explained by Sir Oliver Lodge, the new school of chemists hold that matter and force are forms of the same thing. Every chemical atom is com- posed of thousands and thousands of "electrons," positive and negative, in a state of violent move- ment. In the case of most "elements "—iron and steel, for example—the electrons composing the atom are sufficiently stable so that no change is discoverable in the substance, even when /observed through a long period of time. The electrons that compose an atom of radium, however, are con- stantly being ejected at an amazing speed (100,000 miles a second) and they rapidly rearrange them- selves into other atoms' no longer atoms of radium, but atoms of a totally different substance, helium. This operation is not affected by temperature, proceeding with the same rapidity at red heat as at the temperature of liquid air. An enormous amount of heat is dissipated by radium in this process of dissolution.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-07-22_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>LIGHTNING TELEGRAPHY.</head>
        <p>LIGHTNING TELEGRAPHY.</p>
        <p>Since the laying of the cable which joins San 'Francisco to Manila completed the telegraphic cir- cuit of the earth it has become possible to measure by the ordinary method of telegraphic longitude the arc across the Pacific, and hence to complete I the entire circle of the globe. The interest lies in noting how nearly the sum of the links which go to make up the whole approximate to the total 1 of 360 deg., and thus obtaining some indication of the degree of accuracy which attaches to such operations. There are several routes across the Eastern Hemisphere by which the ends of the Pacific cable might be joined, and taking one direct route, 'rid Madras, it appears that the "closing error" is less than a fifth of a second—that is to say, the sum of the various links exceeds twenty- four hours by only 0.1745ec. Another interesting fact is that this Pacific cable consists of four sections divided by transmitting stations at Honolulu, Midway Island, and Guam, the total length of cable being 7,846 nautical miles. Th• time that a signal takes to traverse this length— excluding that occupied by reading and passing on the messages at the intermediate stations —is 0.6485ec., not quite three-quarters of a second.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-07-22_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>MAGIC MIRROR OF INK.</head>
        <p>THE MAGIC MIRROR OF INK.</p>
        <p>The use of the ink-mirror, says Science Siftings, Is not confined to the Arabs, but extends to the natives of India, who employ it chiefly to discover theft. In the South the ink is poured upon the green leaf of the plantain (banana), where it stands in globular form, like a large dewdrop. A young child is brought in to act as medium or seer. In one of the cases that came to the notice of a correspondent, a trinket had been stolen from I the lady of the house. The staff of servants, numbering nineteen persons, were assembled. The seer was secured and placed in front of the pre- pared pool of isik. Presently the little one described a room in which were a table and a looking-glass% both draped with white muslin. The servants exchanged glances as they recognised the dressing- room of their mistress. Yet the gazer belonged to the street, and had never seen the interior of an. English lady's room. With growing excitement the meni urn related how a young man, a chokra, entered and advanced towards the table, standing I before the glass as he looked at his own reflection. Be examined the various Wiles lying on the table, and took up one which sparkled. At this point the seer was interrupted. The dressing-boy, trembling in every limb, began to whimper, and on being questioned admitted that he was the thief.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-07-22_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <head>PLANT AND INSECT MIMICRY.</head>
        <p>PLANT AND INSECT MIMICRY.</p>
        <p>A kind of reciprocal mimicry between plants and insects has sometimes been established, the insect imitating the leaf or the flower in order to escape injury, and the flower imitating the insect in order to attract fertilising agents. But evidently different laws of mimicry must apply .to seeds, which, like insects, have to seek to avoid injury; and Professor Beal points out in the P4pular Science Monthly that seeds are frequently met with of a mottled or striped or inconspicuous colour, which brings to them a similarity with the soil or pebbles where they are dropped. On the coast of the Philippine Islands a coarse briar produces Leans more or less approaching a sphere in shape. They are about the size of the finger tips of a man, and sonic of them, like peas crowded in the pod, have two fiat surfaces. The colour varies from moderately dark to light drab, seine giving a faint greenish tinge, while the lustre of many is exactly that of chat pebbles. Nearly all the specimens sliew a series of approximately parallel darker lines passing round them, which are very suggestive of stratification. All are quite hard, cut only with difficulty with a knife, and when shaken together in the hand give the clinking sound, only somewhat duller, which is characteristic of pebbles. The mimicry then is that of mixed quartz pebbles, and covers shape,size, lustre, hardness, and stratification. It is so com- plete and perfect that it cannot be regarded as a mere coincidence. Undoubtedly this mimicry of pebbles has saved many a seed from destruction by fish, bird, or reptile. Mr. Crosby, of the United States Bureau of Forestry, makes a still more curious observation. In Missouri, he says, grow large quantities of an iris, and when ripe the rattle of the seeds in the nods closely resembles the rattle or a snake. Cirazing animals invariably step , back after hitting these pods, and thus the green leaves of the plants ars spared to work for Cut= crow of seeds.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-07-22_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>FOOTBALL</head>
        <p>FOOTBALL</p>
        <p>NORTHERN RUGBY LEAGUE.</p>
        <p>The Re-Organisation Scheme Carried.</p>
        <p>On Tuesday night a special general meeting of the Northern Rugby League was held at the George Hotel 'Huddersfield, for the purpose of considering the re- organization scheme. Mr. J. Platt of Oldham, presided. All the clubs in the League, excepting Dewsbury, were represented. The meeting was called in pursuance of the following resolution passed at the annual meeting. of the League That in view of the large majority of clubs in favour of reconstruction, and the altered conditions owing to amendment of By-law 28, substituting the words " two thirds majority " for " three-fourths majority," the Management Committee be aslred to convene a special general meeting at an early date, to submit proposed alterations of rules to carry out the proposed reconstruc- tion scheme. The reconstruction scheme was adopted, and the Halifax-Hunslet scheme, proposed by the Halifax Club, as an amendment, was defeated by a large majority. • The by-laws were then amended to lit the altered conditions brought into operation by the reconstruction scheme, the chief features of which may be gathered , from the principal amendments, which are as follows : • Rule 8, alter to read :—" Clubs in membership with. the League shall arrange fixtures with whom they /please `(subject to special provision for season 1905-6), and club secretaries shall send to the secretary of the League a list of all matches arranged not later than July 15, and such fixtures shall count as League fixtures, provided home and home snatches are arranged, but not otherwise."</p>
        <p>Standing Order (passed on the suggestion of the Bramley Club, and making provision for next season) : ! —"That for season 1905-6 the clubs who would under ' the present conditions have been in the first or second division (as the case may be) for next season shall arrange matches with each club in its own county who would have been members of the same division if no reconstruction scheme had been adopted." Rule 9, alter to read :—"The scoring in the League shall be two points for a win and one point for a draw. at the end of each season's competition the club having the best percentage of points in respect to the number of possible points shall be declared the winner. If two or more clubs tie in respect to percentage of points the match score average shall decide, except when two or more clubs tie for first place, in which case they shall play off on ground fixed by the Management Committee. The proceeds of such playing-off ties shall be divided into three parts, one-third to each club and the remain- ing third to the League funds." Among the amendments of bye-laws on matters of detail is one providing that if any club fail to secure ten home and home matches it shall cease to be a member of the League. Another gives power to the Management Committee to order a match to be played. I between the winners of the cup and the' team selected by the committee from the rest of the clubs, or any other match the committee may think fit, and any club 1 player refusing to take part in such match may be dealt with for misconduct. ! A general meeting of club secretaries will be held at half-past five o'clock next Tuesday afternoon, at the Grosvenor Hotel, Manchester, for the purpose of arrang- ing fixtures. Prior to that meeting the venue of the international match will be fixed, and any club desiring the match to be played on its ground must notify the secretary before that meeting. ' Yesterday afternoon a meeting of the Committee of the above League was held, and the following registra- tions were confirmed : ! Put On—Broughton Rangers—E. J. Gaze, Exmouth ; Halifax—C. Denham, Brighouse Rangers, and H. Sun- derland, local ; Hull—W. Hall, Lancaster ; Oldham— Thomas Hamer, local, and W. James Ham, Cardiff ; Wigan—A. W. Little, Hawick. Struck Off—Brighouse !Rangers—C. Denham ; Hull—R. W. Tomlinson.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-07-22_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>VOLUNTEERS.</head>
        <p>THE VOLUNTEERS.</p>
        <p>Preparations for the Encampment.</p>
        <p>On Saturday, the 29th inst., the members of the Volunteer Battalion go into camp at Wind- mill Hill, Salisbury Plain. The advance party wilt leave on Tuesday next to make the usual arrangements for the reception of the main body of the battalion During the encampment the annual inspection will take place. Owing to the distance to be travelled only thos,e amen who can attend for the full period- can be present at inspection, and the Com- manding Officer is confident, therefore, that for the credit of the battalion, every man will do his utmost to attend camp this year in order that the required number may be present on inspection parade. Every memoer must be present on this parade in order to earn, the capitation grant for the battalion, unless he has received written leave of absence from the Command- ing Officer for some sufficient cause. Consequently every member unable to go to camp must obtain thiS- leave of absence.</p>
        <p>To-morrow (Sunday) morning the Church of England members of the battalion will attend divine service at the Parish Church.</p>
        <p>ROUTE MARCH TO APPLETON.</p>
        <p>There was a splendid muster of the battalion on Saturday for a route march to Appleton Thorne. The cyclists, machine gun section, signallers; stretcher bearers, and regimental transport were all on parade— the latter looking particularly smart in their new ser— vice dress—hut the Mounted Infantry did not take part. Altogether there were on parade 17 officers, 49 non- commissioned officers, and 578 rank and file. Headed by the band of the regiment, a start was made from the Drill Hall shortly after four o'clock, and the column nrocceded along Sankey-street,Bold-street,WilderspooL, London Bridge, and through the Dingle to a largo field at Appleton Thorne. Here the men were inspected by the Commanding Officer (Colonel Edelsten), and this was followed by a. general salute and march past in column, returning in quarter column. Refreshments were served on the field, Captain A.F.G. Potter as usual having charge of the arrangements. The return marcb was accomplished by way of Lomb Brook and Latch ford, Warrington being reached at about 9.30.</p>
        <p>LEIGH MAN DROWNED IN IRELAND. Information bad just been received in Manchester of a sad bathing fatality at Dublin, whereby a resident of Leigh lost his life and a Manchester man had a very narrow escape.—William Withers, aged 40, of Leigh, and James George, of 26, Princess-rend, Manchester, went out from Liverpool to Dublin to look for employ- ment as tailors. While bathing at Dollymount they' got out of their depth, Withers going down before his. companion's eyes, while George fainted on reaching the shore. A man passing found George in ail. unconscious condition, and in half an hour Withers body was recovered.</p>
      </div>
