        <p>" You will find them all very quiet and trade- itaid Dr. Drummond, stretching his large limbs luxuriously 011 the conch opposite his " I make it a rule not to lake tiny violent .eases, or any paticeds suspected of lionCaadal ten- deueiett. So I have no doubt you will have a 'very easy (line of it. Of course all the poor :things have ill usi.ns. Manyintagitto themselves or emperors. We let've the Pope here, and nt least one Jay Gould. You needn't pay any 'attention to them. Just humour them, and say 'that you will report the ease in the proper quer- "ter. That does no harm, you see, and it keeps t them quiet." After is few further instruetious Dr. Drum- mood rose, yawned, and Wile me good night, saying as he did so that I might consider myself 'out ditty after midnight, as he would be off in the morning before I was nip. As soon as he left me I threw open the win- Arm, and sat down to smoke a pipe in the cool oiight air before turning in. I was new to my surrottudings, having arrived only an hour bo- lero from Edinburgh to take charge of Dr. Drum- inond'a Private nitylatti fee a fortnight, while he "(alloyed a short run •in Switzerland. Judging from appearances—the large house and extensive •grounde, the handsome furniture, and the well supplied table-1 here could be little doubt, I thought, that Dr. Driminiond'a wits a high•elaste "establishment, and that tI would be very comfort- able tor the next fortnight. When I went down to breakfast next morning everything •I saw confirmed any impressions. 'The great drawing-room wax empty ; the French window opening opening on the lien were wide open, and scattered about the grounds were groups id 'quiet, orderly men, -most of them past middle ;life, some talking, some ireading, a dew sugaged hit gardening. The ladies' quarters were at the 'other side of the house. Passing on to -the lawn I made the acquaint. •anee of some •Of toy patients. They were all • gentlemen, and scarcely any of them exhibited traces of mental disorder, unless I dimmed to tltit upon the special delusion which the patient cherished. I wits turning back to the house when an in- signitieant•looking elderly num, whose clot lics were somewhat shabbier than those of his neigh- bours! accosted me, and asked MO if I was the new doctor. " Yes, 1 am taking Dr. Drunintond's place for .a short time," I answered. " Can I do any. thing for you?" " Indeed you CHIT i" said the patient, his voice trembling with excitement. " I want you In look into my ease. I don't know why I was brought here, but 1 am as sane AA any Mil alive I have been in this itsylum itearly five years, and eamta believe that I am legally detained. I wish you lould mention my ease to the visiting •ittegist rates or the Lunacy Commissioner." " But, my demur sir," I replied, " Dr. Drum- mond is the proper person to do that. He will 'be beck in a fortnight, mid you can talk to hint. .1 have no doubt he will do what is right, and if you are really cured, of course you will be dis- charged." " Cured ! I tell you I have never been ill. I beve limn made the victim of H. vile plot, a von- 4-pirney, sir, between nay son-iu-law, and a doctor' a friend of his, who (I have no doubt) 'signed my certificate. But Dr. Drummond is so deaf to anything. I can say, that 1 am forced to eonelude Out he is in the conspiracy too.' This wax very much the sort of thing I had ex- pected to hear, amid I attached tio real importance •to the poor wild talk. " Who are you ?" I itaked, by way of turning Ida thoughts into a fresh channel. " Well, I have been told that some one said I was Jay Gould. I'm sure I never said so. Autl I can't be Jay Gould, seeing that Jay Gould is • dead. No, sir. My name Sampson -Charles T'rederick Sampson. was on the Stock Ex- ' change in happier days, but finding that I was -hieing money instend of 'milking it, I retired, with a very comfortable fortune. And my son- . in-law has for his own reasons shut me up here, priimier for the rest of my life. It ix shameful, shaineful, that such things should be possi- , iit' a free country like England 1 And what want to ask you is—Will you help me to re. • . :rain my freedom ? I can make you a Hell man, Air, if you will help me." Mr. Sampson's milliner betrayed some excite. fluent, but not more, it seemed to me, titan might reasonably be expected in the case of a man who • . wan suffering, or thought he wits suffering, nil- ititaly. But it was no part of my business, considered, to pass judgment on the mental con- dition of Dr. DrUMMOIIa'S patients. And I knew very well Mut some of the most decidedly insane people in tlie world may at times appear as sane as is lamhop. So I thought it best to repeat the for- mula Dr. Drummond had given me for use ut 1 *ltch cities, mentally resolving, however, to keep , Mr, Sampson's cane in view during my stay. " Well, Mr. Sampson, all I can say is that I will report your ease in the proper quarter," I said moving away. • " Thank you, sir. That is all I want," said Mr. Sampson, with so much of purpose in his lotto •that I was for the moment startled. " What. .ever may have been the crua3 formerly," I said to n•Yself, "1 can't think that that man is any thiug •else snne now." Having little else to do than to read novels and smoke cigarettes on the lawn, there can be little wonder that I thought a good deal about the ease of. Mr. Satnpson. I had heard many stories Ca who lift not P—of rich men. with perhaps junt the suspicion of some litirmless crank, shut up in asylums by those who had, or hoped to have, the liandling of their money. Butt I found it impos- sible to believe that Dr. Drummond, rich and re- qateted an he was, would have any part in such a Plot. No, I acquitted him of auy active par- ticipation in is emispiracy ; but I could not help thinkitig that he bad been in this case perlitipa little slow in coining to the eoncluaion that his 4:atient was sane. For on that point I had now no doubt what- ever. I had had many conversations %lid' Mtn. and in all of them h, isj'luuyeul a mind clear and 'acute, though uncultivated hi the ordinary mean - ice' of the word. He Wm.; no reader, but he had travelled much, and he seemed to have a very fnir knowledge of life and the world we live in. 'How he came to be shut up with mad people I could not imagine. Several little thingn that came to my notice tiering my stay at Cumberlend Lodge (as tho "Whim was called), tended to confirm me in the belief that whatever the man might have been in .tha past, he was sane nom The day before I was to leave Cumberhuld I:salge Mr. Sampson came up to me and led me a little way apart. " You leave us to-morrow, Dr. Hepburn P" qie said. " Yes, to-ntorrow afternoon." " Well, you know that ant att sane as you aro —don't you now No ! Don't answer. It might put you in a false position. What I want You to do is this. When you are outside, help me 'to escape, and I will make you rich for life. Listen. I have a lot of money hid away that no 13110 knows anything about. If you manage to fret me out of this, heti of it will be yours, and I 'l"ce say the built share will come to as notch as twenty thousand poinals. Come, now. What do „You say to that? Isn't it temptieg ? Twenty thousand poroids !" It was tempting, for I aut n poor man, but I answered straight ctlongli—" Mr. Sampson, you inuat know well enough that I capnot do what You ask. It would be a most dimhonourable thing 'for me to do." " Then, will you do this Will you go to 'Pacauel Hudson, the blackguard ply poor .Elsie oinked on itaarying, and tell him that I give " That you give in ?" • ." Yes. Ho will know what that means. He ',wants the rest of my money, and can't lay his ,na"da on it, and if I promise to put him in the 11. of getting it, lie will cut off the supplies, and "II Dr. Drummond will suddenly discover that ,RlVe l'Ured. ‘‘ lik e g &gt;v. tel if T UP my mcney to get it, I suppose I must. • you take the message ?" Perluips I was wrong to take it. But I told 'll5-xelf that there are higher duties than those of Itional etiquette. The man seemed to me to tente and purposes sane ; and I had long ~7 co to e coatl esiite that Cumberland :Luelgett,me th :vas not no much an asylum for the in- ..-mtveitient plane for keeping trouble- ttheir 41unl° relitlives. who Were eccentric or queer 1„.1 • „mutat-, and whose presence in the family llt Wreles was for /ionic •n undesirable. I told ~, inpson that I could not do anything to aid • lust escaping, but that I would willingly take u° of ia relatives, and he gave Y hia re:go t° ally the Hill son-iu-law'm address—The Hermitage, Putney' . On my way through don I went to Put. , and called at 'Ore II itage. rhere wan little CU tiding to the name in</p>
