      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-07-22_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <p>~.pect of !hi, 1m4.. 11111.1.111 K (Pa-brick WOrlitlg., re 141140, /1.11 0110 could 1,4 P: 141 P. ghl nee with enibeliiNbe.l will, evety ornattielit ttt 1.10 hoorm deem aior 11011 A 1.11196:y. evidmiee...of IvOft 111 weie seattered Javhdi- ly about, hut tho farm of the toi4irons of the imago l‘ 11S1 while and motion. —not at all Lice what the fill) of a halo.? wife stud mother ought, to lie. I told her that I had coma from Cumber- land &amp;' no aloe, d 100 vis her father. " Day father !" slat exelatmed, ehtving her 110)114. " Have you seen him r Is he well ? .1)0 you think be will re.mver When I told lie.r that ill toy bolief her father wits no longer iitNatte, and that cloe might hope that he would ?moil he at. at liberty, her agita• that increased. She trembled, sod grow while, lott irrelevant and meaningless que,liotta to tile, throwing apprehensive glances at the .larriage drive front time to time.</p>
        <p>There is my husband she exclaimed, and looking out of the window I Ra w IL 131 I, portly nom of thirty-five or forty, in orthodox frock coat ;ilia tall lint, Walk Ii up to the main en- trance. Ile canto into the room where we were sitting, and taking no notice whatever of his wife, who sat Trembling with apprehension on the window-sent, came up to me with a " Who- the-devil-are-you P" air that stirred my bile on the instant.</p>
        <p>In a tcv,r words I told hint that I had been act- ing as Dr. Drummond's &amp;aim tennis, and that I had promised to call, 'adding that in my opinion his father-in-law was quite recovered, had would soon be set at liberty. "I fancy: that rests in other hands than yonvv,. Dr. Hepburn," sneered Mr. Hudson. "It takes a practised eye to tell mental disorder from met- tal health in those eases." .</p>
        <p>"And tin eye that im directed siniply and sole- ly to the question of the patient's wellbeing, Mr. lindoon." _</p>
        <p>" What the devil do you mean, sir P" he shouted. " You had better take care what you 'say. Two eminent medical men have certified that Charles Frederick Sampson is insane, and incapable of managing his own affairs. Till those two medical men certify that he is sane., Charles Frederick Sampson remains where he is." " But, Fred," began. Mrs. Hudson " if this gentleman, who is a doctor, thinks that papa has recovered—" • Mr.. Hudson said nothing, but lie turned his hold slowly round, and gave his wife a look so full of malevolence that she faltered, stammered, and finally stopped speaking in confusion. Tier lord and master rose. "If that is all you have to say, Mr.—tut—Hepburn, I suppose this interview had better terminate," said he. My blood wak boiling with indignation, and it was some seconds before I•could trust myself to speak. • "1 have not said all I have to say," I replied, and not being willing- that the unhappy woman should know the full extent of her husband's raseality, I added significantly—'' Perhaps I could see you for it minute or two alone r</p>
        <p>, 11.fre. loolied at um reproachfully, and her bush:lntl ctwleil, but I paid no attention to either. " Come this way," he eaid abruptly, and he took me into a entail room at the other aide of the hall.</p>
        <p>In a few words I put before this man his father-in-law's offer— he was to be released front the asyltint. and itt return he was to hand over set ip and sharee representing a very large bUlli of maney—no less than forty thousand pounds. Hudson took one or. two turns up and down the room before replying. " I believe there is truth in what the old Inca- tie says," he said abruptly. " When we collie to examine into his affairs alter he became insane I found that various blocks of shares had disap- peared. I imagined that they had beim sold, and the proceeds in all probability lost in speculation. But I have often felt that I had not got to the Whim bf it. This explains several things. So he lets all those thousands hidden away somewhere, has he Well, we shall never get hold of them so long as he remains where he is. That's one thing certain." He fell to biting his nails and pondering, and T took up my hat to go. He took no notice of my " Good-day," and I left him. Naturally I considered that my connection with Dr. Driunmond's pat lent was now at an end, but fate ordered otherwise. It appeared that neither party to thie strange compact would trust the other, mid I was asked to stand by, as it sere,' and see fair play. I consented to do this, amid the necessary formalities having been completed, Mr. Sampson was set at liberty, and came to London. I. went to see him at his total, andhe took me: with him to the office of a friend of his in the City, who produced front his safe a small tin box, . which he handed over to Mr. &amp;MINIM, taking his receipt for it. Then we drove back to the ' hotel at Whieh Mr. SM111)S011 was staying, and I went up to his private sitting-room. " Dr. Hepburn," said the old man, as he placed the tin box on the table, " this box con- ' tains scrip and shares for no less a sum than forty thousand pounds. What is to prevent tne giviiug. my rascally sow in .15 w the slip, and going ' to Canada Or Australia, to end my days in peace, a wealthy and respected matt •• Nothing, air, but your word." " But is it promise given under such 6mm- /dances binding P.• Then seeing that I !hesitated, he went Oil— " Every man, I lake it, has It right to his liberty. unless he has forfeited it by some crime. Grant • lug that I may have been eccentric, or subject to delitsions at one time, is that any reason for keeping mo in confinement for the rest of my life, to suit the purposes of a scoundrel ?" " Certainly not." " Yet I found it practically impossible to re- gain my liberty. Have you over observed in me ally symptoms of inteurty P" " I cannot say that 1 Lave." " ‘Vby then should 1 band over the remainder if my fortune to the man who has imprisoned me for years, mid ham ruined my daughter hap. piness P—;Listen, Dr. Hepburn. Six yearn ago, having one child, and being anxious to secure her against want, whatever the future might have mu store for me, I settled a large sum of money upou her, and made her practically inde- pendent of lue. That was the root of all our troubles. For, not long afterwards, my poor Elsie met this plausible scoundrel, Hudson, who in spite of me succeeded in winning her affec- tions. " They were married, and from that hour 1 lost my daughter. I eoula only see her by stealth, and I POOII discovered that she .was fur front happy. She stood in mortal fear of her hus- band, who was plotting to obtain the solo con- trol of her fortune. I threatened to take her away firm him, and in revenge for this, or in fear that I might bring his conduct before a court of law, and carry out my threats, he took ad- vantage of my state of excitement, and some ab- surd craze I had got into iuiy bead at that time, tand Illta me confined as a lunatic. Luckily I had deposited the bulk of my remaining property with an oil friend whom I could trust, and to- day 1 have recovered it, together with my free- dom—Do you sec any reason why I should keep faith_with ? Do you_?"..</p>
        <p>" I confess that I do not," said I. " But I do !" saki the old man in a tone of Ouch concentrated fiereenesm, that I shrank a little away from him. "Du you thilik that I would go off coolly to America, let Its say, and leave my Elsie to lead the life of a slave ? I will not." " Mr. Sampson," 1 said, " you are excitintr yourself too much. If you do not calm yourself I fear there may be a return of your mentat dix• order." Lit ertre 111 U 4 queer IUUK UU 5011 I.IIAIIII/g, KUO mon after 1 went away. It had been arranged that the tranefer of the scrip WWI to take place on the following day, and I was to be present on the occasion. I went to lhe hotel a little before the appointed time, and on entering the lull I met a doctor who watt something, of u speciMist, in mental dihordere, with whom I was slightly impended. We shook tluds, and he told me that he had been examining a patient, tto old man who had a t•itting-rouni on the second floor. I started. " Du you Meal&amp; Ur. Sampson P" r Isked.</p>
        <p>" That's the name. Do you know him P" I toil him that I did—that he had just been discharged from an ilsy 111111 Its cured, HMI that I had not been able to find any trace of mental disease in him. " Oh, that's all nonsense," laughed toy friend. " Dr. Curzon brought mute here, mid introduced me, leaving Its :time together, according to the shunt o iii ,iit,hi eases made and provided. I found him as mad as a March hare— talked miii sorts of nonsense. lie's evidently had a relapse. I ,4gned tie certificate without any hesitation and so dill Curzon. I understand there are mei:from an it-yhtni notv 111 the hotel to take hint away. But Ini wit be oil. Good-day. I stared after him in amazement,. I could not believe that Sitiiipsou was really mad. At most ho might have some harmless delusions. With kind treatment at home, awl freedom from worry, I felt certain he would be all right. The question in my mind was—Should I tell him that his son-in-law was playing him false? Yet, if Dr. Curzon and my friend were agreed that he ought to be under restraint. how could I inter- fete?</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-07-22_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, July 22, 1905. THE UNEMPLOYED.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, July 22, 1905. THE UNEMPLOYED.</p>
        <p>Not being able to make up my mind I• slowly amended the stairs to Mr. Sampson's room. He was not alone. A. man whom I took for an at- tendant from a lunatio asylum , was sitting in a corner of the room reading ft newspaper. " Come, Dr. Hepburn," said lho old man, rising to his feet. "'You are just in time. I ex- pect-my amiable son-io•law evc ry moment." Ile was 6ented at the tahte,_ the tin box lefore him.. An h u e • spoke be nlocked the bOK, glanced inside, and closed the lid again, without turning the key. • A knock came to the door and Hudson entered. Ho advanced with a knilio on his face, holding out his hand. The old matt did not take it.</p>
        <p>, " Sit down, sir," he said .fq daly, I have a word or two to say to .you. It wilt not lake, long." Hudtion seated himself, and Mr. Sampson pro- ceeded. " Yon took my datighler away from' sine. That I could have forgiven, if you bid treated leer well. But you did not. You have ruined her happiness, and used her worse than you use your dogs. Me, leer only protector, you shut up in an asylum. Now you demand from me 'the whole of my poperty. }fele it is. Take it—and with it take my curse—and take thisl" The 311`.11 sittillK in the corner dashed his paper to the floor, and sprang. forward. 1 did the tame. Iv., Were WO late. An lie spoke Mr. Sampson NO opened the Ism' cued taken out a huifu. As the last word left. him lips he seized his enemy by the throat will his left tonal, and with lila right plunged the knife into his heart. Hudson threw up his arms, and fell with a groan upon the floor.Jo three seconds he was dead.</p>
        <p>tne papers authorising his fatller-in-law'm detention in an asylum were found on the dead man's body, no doubt wss entertained Of Samp- son's madness. .He was 414'g ui I kit on the ground of insanity, and ordered to be detsined during his Majesty's plejo,nre. I have since heard that helms been releasid, his daughter making herself responsible for his 1.,a1e custody. . .</p>
        <p>Except in one mind there was no doubt that at the time when the murder was committed Samp• son was iusane. That mind was my own. I have never been able to satisfy myself on the question of the man's sanity or insanity. But, lean to the belief that it was not; no he deceived, but the two doctors, eminent men though they were, who certified hint as insane just before the crime was consummated. I have tro doubt that S£llllpBoll saw through his /4011..111. hi W'Fi intended treachery, and I believe he took advantage of ii to secure a vengeance that would he final end complete, Ina wonld entail no penalty tltat be was not prepared to endure. As he said to MO, he might have fled the count y, and lived in com- fort awl sunnily abrond, but Ito was determined to set his daughter frco Ii om the traniiy which made her life it burden hi her. This is my own solution of the problem, but I have 1 tln don lit that Dr. Curzon and his follow Ppeeialint would bold stoutly to the opyosito opinion.</p>
        <p>rivers, canals, and reads which wanted revolutiouising. Those pros:eat at that meeting were there to express their conviction that poverty was'unneceesary, and that there was no law of life, no Itw of nature, in fact no law whatever that could defend the problem of poverty. Hearged them not to relax their' efforts until England was a free and happy country where the cottages were the first consideration and the palaces were ignored, for the people would live when the peers and their palaces should have vanished like parasites from the earth. (Applause.) Mr. GAVAN DUFFY also spoke and said that when the winter came round they were not going to stand idly by and watch the tragedy of poverty again.' The time had come not for talk but for work. They were going to demand work from the Government. If they could not get it from them, they would demand it from the local authorities, and if they paid no attention, if the people who wanted work could not get work, then. rather than that the women and children should starve, they must raid the shops. (Applause). If there was a spark of humanity in them, they would not stand idly by and see the women and children starving to death when the shops were full of food. They were there that night to denounce the Tory party and not less, the Liberal party. If any of them thought that the return of a Liberal Government to power meant the solution of the unemployed problem or any other problem in which Labour was deeply concerned, he would ask them to be disillusioned. Why had not the Liberal party, when they had the power, used it to solve these pressing social problems Not a einem Liberal of any note or rank, continued the speaker, had said a word on behalf of the unemployed during the present session. it was not on the- Liberal and Tory party that the workers had to rely;_ but upon themselves. At the next election they wanted to return to Parliament a body of intelligent 'labour representa- tives who would go into the House of Commons to fight for the cause of labour without regard to the existence or convenience of either the Liberal or Tory party. (Applause.) The resolution was carried unanimously.</p>
      </div>
