      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-07-22_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>ST. HELENS.</head>
        <p>ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>CURIOT_IS COMPENSA.TION CASE.-A MAN WITH ODD LEGS.</p>
        <p>John Birchall, collier, of 20, Bewsey-street, Thatto askx•cl his Honour Judge Shand, on Wednesday, to order his late employers to continue paying com- pensation to him. Mr. P. C. Kelly said that on March 21, 1903, Birchall was standing in a refuge place in Eccleston Hall Colliery, when 4inc of the boxes of CCM which was passing along the road got off the rails and crushed him, breaking both hones near the ankle of the right leg. He had been paid half wages, us. H. per week, until May last, when the Colliery oompany stopped payment. Dr. Brindle, of Prescot, saw] the mat, svffered from a badly set and badly united fracture. The large hone was united, bet the smaller bone was not united. The man could not possibly work in a coalmine for he had two odd legs. Dr. O'Keeffe corroborated, and said the man's leg would never be efficient Mr. Peace, for the respondents, called four or five doctors, who said that the fracture of the leg was firmly united, and the tendons and muscles of the leg and ankle were free. The condition of the leg in no way incapacitated the , man front work. The man was still wearing supports to the leg, which ought to have been taken off long since. If he did this he would be able In three weeks to walk as far as anyone. The leg was one inch shorter than the other, but that would be com- pensated for in other ways. Dr. W. Berry, of Wigan Infirmary, who said he examined Birchall on April 17th on behalf of the New Permanent Miners' Relief Society, agreed that the man was fit for work. The Judge said he was satisfied that Birchall ought to go to work. Mr. Peace then asked his honour to make an award for a lump sum in order to conclade the business, and they agreed to pay £lO, which was awarded to Birchall, with costs.</p>
        <p>7,-..., -,.. t--1 rtt ~..- ~-pey ....• .! s'll' • ,I 1 . si. • `.f. , 1,,, 4 I y pi .., klj • rzr. 5, 7,-- M ft il ri,44 IAu.IF , .., a 1 i\- ~'c. , Spots, swellings, rashes and ....: ' - , pimples set up by the heat indi- - cote that the skin's functions " have been over•strained. Zarn-Buk j. . -,.. -:- . by reason of its great purity, j - richness, tund refinemer,t, soon , ~ overcomes these disorders. ' : - making the skin clear . ~ - - end Itralthy once ,--• .- Mort. Ifilimerte—n+t.a.,,v, lit, , •-.....VMMV*7 sr- ,. 14.4F4f...A s.,:k_ff 7, ~,- : ii rt'(l ...,1',117 : ''... iecd 411,:i' je,a.',. out.h. h Y. t? v•-: „:15 vll4Fe-4,, :::,.: 2.9 'sor. /or Or an rhenifils or front tke 4 Zcia-Buri: e '0.,1, lied CIY4S Street, E.C.</p>
