      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-07-22_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>DEMONSTRATION AT WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>DEMONSTRATION AT WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>Will Thera b 3 Riots</p>
        <p>`The claims of tun Government Unemployed Bill for immediate attention in Parliament were strongly urged at a demonstration in favour of the measure which was held at Bridge Foot on Saturday evening. The meeting was stated to he held "under the auspices of the Independent Labour Party and trade unionists of War- rington" and was I irgely attended. Mr. Philip Snowden, Labour candidate for Blackburn, was announced as tke principal speaker, but was unable to attend. The meeting was addressed by Mr. Ben 'fillet, Labour candidate for Eccles, Mr. Ga,van Duffy, a well- known Socialist lecturer, and others. Nil.. E. Iforrocks, prE-si dent of thelLabour Representation Coin mittee,pp- sided. Prior to the meeting there was a procession through the main streets, headed by a band and the banner of tI3, Gas Workers' and General Labourers' Union,</p>
        <p>-The -CHAIRMAN annnanced the receipt of letters of apology for non-attendance from Mr. J. Podmore, jun., president of the Trades and Labour Council, and Coun- cillors Stott and Wright. C )uncillor, Stott, in his letter, said the Unemployed Bill must if possible be passed this session by the npplication of outside pressure .to the present discredited Government. If nothing was done for the unemployed before next winter arrived, he was afraid Mr. Keir Hardie's prophecy regarding probable rioting would be only too true.— The Chairman said the object of that and similar demon- stratious was to assist, perseade or even force the Government to pass the Unemployed Bill during the present session. ide was, however, strongly of opinion that they would !leves get from the Government the legislation on the unemployed question which they drirwl. Mr. Balfonr had declared that demonstrations outside the House of Commons would have no effect on the legislation in that Chamber. Probably not ; but if demonstrations iottside the House would have no effect on the men "ho should represent the uorkers of this country. the workers would go one better, and have a demonstration on the floor of the House before many years had passed. (Applause). That would be achieved by returning such men as Mr. Philip Snowden and Mr. Til let to Partiament. (Applause.) Mr. 0. Brctuev,secrettiry of the local Labour Repre- sentation Coramittee,moved : " That this Mass Meeting I • of workers re-lairms the declarations on the unemployed problem as agreed to at the Special Conference of the Labour Representation Committee, at Liverpool, in January last, that unemployment is due to monopoly and I the lack of a proper organisation of industry ; that fuller powers should he conferred upon local authorities for dealing with the question ; that great public works should be endentaken by the Government ; that a Ministry of Labour should be created ; and that the penalty of disfranchisement should not be incurred by able-bodied workers for whom work is provided. It accepts the Unemployed Bill in so far as 'it recognises the responsibility of public authorities to find work for the unemployed which shall not carry with it the taint of pauperism or the penalty of disfranchisement ; but declares that if the Bill is to be satisfactory it • must I apply to the whole country ; all limitations upon rates' of wages paid must be removed ; most of the cost must be found by the National Exche.pier ; and the powers of the committees created must be greatly extended. This meeting, therefore, urges the Govern-sent to accept such amendments and then pass the Bill this session." It NVIIS to him a suprising and lamentable fact. that the workers should stand so patiently and bear so quietly the hardships attendant upon unemployment. He believed that had the workers shown a stronger resentment. of those hardships they would soon have been remedied. They would, however, continue until the workingmen roused themselves. They were told by people who professed to sympathise with them that there were always a certain number of men who would not work: They had that elass of people at both ends of the stick—(laughter) —and if they were going to deal with them at all, they must deal with those at the top end of the stick first. I (Apolnnse.) Mr. %V ILL Inm KNowt.Es, president or the Warrington ' Independent Labour Party, seconded and said that although the Unemployed Bill was a Government measure, the Labour p t.rty had been its only real supporters in the House of Commons. The Aliens Bill was of nothing like the same importance as the Unemployed Bill and therefore the latter should have had precedence. Be was afraid at this stage it would be almost impossible to pass the Bill during the present session- Continuing Mr. Knowles said the very fact of the working classes entering the Liberal or Tory party was a sort of traitnrship to their own class, because by doing so they divided their power. Under the present economic conditions those who worked hardest were the poorest, while rich loafers revelled in* luxury and debauchery. It was the object of socialists to remedy that state of affairs. . _</p>
        <p>Mr. BEN TILLET supported the motion. He said he was net at all pessimistic about the outcome of the agitation. Prob thly the Government would not have common-sense enough to take advantage of the moment and add credit to themselves when they were turned out and had to go before the electorate. Mr. Balfour haul rendered signal service to their movement and had done more to open the eyes of the British workman than any number of lectures and agitating speeches might do. Mr. Balfour, the philosophic doubter, the economic muddler, and the political Juggler generally had for once in his life rendered a service by accident. (Laughter.) They ought to pass a vote of thanks to him for the nasty and contemptuous speeches he had made in the House of Commons. As a matter of fact, knowinr, the weakness of his position, Mr. Balfour might have had in his minds desire to pass the measure, but directly it came before the country the landowners and the capitalists in his party did not use peaceful persuasion but they used the force of their position, end .power, and money, and they told Mr. Balfour that if he dared to help the poor they ,would I tight him for all they were worth. Mr. Balfour, the ; supple and subtle philosopher, had to bow to the inevit- able. They were glad that even the iron hand of the i vicious and greedy classes had been disclosed. The , Labour Party had lost nothing by it. The capitalists were so dominant that not even the most powerful minister of modern times in the person of Mr. Balfour ' would dare to fight against their wishes. He (Mr. i Tillett) said "Thank God for the greediness ' and stupidity of the bovine lot who own all the money you earn for them." Mr. Balfour waS not moved by the thought of poverty because be never actually saw it. What to him were breaking mother's hearts and starving children ? What to him was the moral and physical deterioration of these men who were his fellow citzens ? What to him were the consequences to the nation ? Ere was 'secure behind the wealth of his land ; his friends were secure behind the bulwarks of their capital, and the workers might go to the workhouse or an early grave, or sit out at nights, or go into casual wards. What did it matter to Mr. Balfour? All , that he I cared for was to spend 250 millions for is set of i scoundrels in South Africa. Mr. Balfour did not care for the millions starving, but he let the Al rican millionaire rob him. The unemployed already had a case that was overwhelming; hut the powers-that-be were callous cud tool: no notice. Mr. Balfour did not think there would be , riots next winter. If he (Mr. Tilletl was alive then, he was going to niche the hungry feel the power that they possessed. (Applause) The "well-fed beasts " in the House of Commons—as Mr. Keir Hardie had termed tfiem.—were brutal to the last degree of callous- ness. Mr. Balfour was not treating with Mr. Keir Hardie alone—he could not even sneer him out of exis- tence—but he had to deal with two milieus of the organized workers of the country. That was what Mr. Balfour had to face and he would have to face them in Manchester, when he came with tongue in cheek to fool the people there. (Applause.) In the Ouse of further remarks, Mr. Tillet said gr. Balfour was a British Cossack without the pluck of the Russian one for the hitter did co out and stand a chance of being shot. If Mr. Balfoor wanted to play the Czar in this country he would have the same show that the Czar of Russia had get. (Applause.) The workers were not prepared to dis:mss, this grave tragedy in meek terms. llt was appalling in its strength and awfulness. The workers would do well to heed the words of Mr. Balfour. of the Home Secretary, and their political repre- sentatives. He (Mr. Tillett) thanked Mr. Balfour, with all his cynicism, and his wretched friends for having :proved up to the hilt their (the Socialists') words—that neither he nor his class want xl to do the slightest good for the people but if they, could without danger to their own necks, force them into slavery, they would do it to-morrow. (Applause.) Mr. rinet referred to the laud enestion as being at the root iif the unemployed evil, pointing out that some 50,000 families practically monopolised the land. Poverty was due to the fact that the means of life were in this !-ands of a set of scoundrels who had stolen them from silly people who were to-day suffering the pangs of hunger and in some cases were ' not prepared to fight for their children. There were 30,000,000 acres' of land untitled and lunused, and even the afforestation of that land would occupy the unemployed, and save us from purchasing timber from other countries. Then there were the</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-07-22_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>REV. J. F. MATTHEWS VIEWS.</head>
        <p>THE REV. J. F. MATTHEWS VIEWS.</p>
        <p>Remedy In the Hands of the People.</p>
        <p>In his sermon at the Latchford Baptist Church on Sunday evening the Rev. J. F. Matthews also dealt with the letter from Mr. Keir Hardie. The letter he said, contained what amounted to an indictment of the Christian ministry, for its callousness to one specific incident recorded in the public press, an incident the ocourrence of which was a disgrace to humanity Let him say in answer that the incident escaped his notice. He should, never have heard of it but for Mr. Keir Hardie's letter ; and such incidents did nut usually escape the social worker and visitor among the poor. Again he might tell Mr,Reir Harclie,what had obviously escaped him' that such incidents would be multiplied a hundredfold were it not for the constant visitation and assistance the ministers and churches were able to afford. Their hearts were wrung with grief and agony very often because they could not do a tithe of what they gladly would do. Mr. Keir Hardie should not have used the word callous. He had forgotten all the</p>
        <p>RELIEF WORK and the social agencies employed by small and large churches in poor localities for the help of the poor and the unemployed. Every Christian minister knew that casuals and the inmates of our workhouses would be greatly increased but for the constant succour regu- larly afforded by special "Church poor funds." Many unerhployed to whom a Mlle work would be a boon— work such as they could do—were saved, not only from increasing the pressure of the rates, but from the hate- ful life of vagrancy, or something worse, into which they would assuredly fall,by timely aid from the sources indicated. Neither they nor the churches as a whole were guilty of this neglect of the unemployed, nor the advocacy of their cause. It wasquite true, on the other hand,that the churches,saving only the Salvation %silly, had no vast organization to seal with the unemployed,latit even the Salvation Army found its resources all too limited even to touch the fringe of the problem. That, however, did not argue either coldness to or ignorance of, add certainly not apathy in regard to the remedies needed to seoure work for the willing }Ands. He offered the following suggestions towards</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-07-22_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>SOLUTION OF TRH PROBLEM :</head>
        <p>A SOLUTION OF TRH PROBLEM :</p>
        <p>"(1) The creation of a strong, moral sentimental of national responsibility in rogerd to the whole question. (2) The vigorous and prompt exercise of powers with which Boards of Guardians are already invested. (3) The return of men to Parliament who had had their noses on the grindstone. Men who know what the struggle was and who weregfamiliar with industrial and economic pressure from within." So long as they sent lawyers, millionaires, manufacturers, and coon try squires to Parliament. so long would the present status quo be maintained. They might just as well put a fox to guard a hen roost, or a wolf to take of a lamb. Not that these men were unsympathetic ; they too were victims of the Same economic system, but they did not know that phrase of the problem, so familiar to the man who earns a weekly wage. (4) The appointment of a labour department with a labour minister as a necessary item in the solution of this great problem." Let them work for the coming of that time when the nation would ' unitedly recognize that every man and woman born with a mouth to feed and hands to work should have the latter prmanently secured to him, that the first might always be met.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-07-22_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>REV. JOHN YONGE AND THE DUTY OF</head>
        <p>REV. JOHN YONGE AND THE DUTY OF</p>
        <p>THE CHURCHES.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. Kier Hardie's Appaal, Preaching at Wycliffe Church on Sunday evening the Rev. John Yonge dealt with the unemployed question, his remarks being based on a circular letter on the sub- ject sent by Mr. J. Keir Hardie, M.P., to ministers of religion. The sooner and more completely, slid Mr. Yonge, that the 'Christian people of this country realize Christ's complete identiacation with all suffering and helpless men, women and ' children; the better it will be. At present, failure to realize this is the cause of the alarm- ing and even criminal apathy which has permitted con- ditions to arise and continue. The tremendous want and woe created by lack of work is, perhaps, the most terrible of all the facts ofour social conditions. There had he con- tinued, been many attempts at relief but so far nothing effective had been arrived at. The present Governrnentlpro- mised legislation in relief and a Bill was introduced into the House of Commons which however did not meet with 154 any great enthusiasm some people deeming it only a Majority for Fletcher's 12. trap to catch votes. Competent judges were however of opinion that in the hands of capable men the Bill I THIRD ROUND. could be made of very considerable service, and-attempts I CORPORATION V. LONGFORD WIRE CO.—Played on had been made to arouse public opinion on its behalf the green of the King and Queen on Saturday. Score:— and if possible to pass it against the reluctance of the , CORPORATION. LONGPORD WIRE CO. Government. Mr. Hardie was abundantly justified in J. Massey making his appeal. The sad fact had to be faced that 15 there wale On all hands what seemed like A. Hands J. Harrison 5 E. Clare AN INDOMITABLE INDIFFERENCE IW. Newnes W. concerning the Bill. There appeared to be a disposition Monks l5 J. Simister POWIS • 8 S. Radcliffe to postpone it, or to regard the prevailing evil as one • T. Eaves impossible of remedy. " The poor ye have always with J. , 10 W. Holloway you " was a' saying which prese