      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-07-22_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <p>could work and wanted to work, but who c3uld not obtain employment, and who were face to faoe with want for themselves and their own children. Those people were</p>
        <p>A PERIL TO THE STATE. He supposed that comparatively few were stall familiar with the dimensions of that great social difficulty. They knew what it meant when as in the coal strike some yearsa,,o,their fellow-townsmen were out of work in largo numbers and had to be provided for to a great degree by local generosity. The question of the unemployed was that conditions magnified by being spread more or less all over the country and by being permanent year in, year out. On New Years Day last year one person in 36 outside London and one in 31 inside London were in leceipt of relief from the rates. There was strong reason to anticipate that matters would be considerably worse on New Years Day 1906. The case was heartrending and is not to be passed by an indifference or left to the inadequate machinery of the poor law. Men, women and children in a Christian land were not to be left to starvation and homelessness because they could not find honest employ- ment. Coristian people mist face the facts and deal with them. If our Government would not effectually intervene the nation must compel them. if the Churches would follow the Master in whom they professed to believe they would ornue to the rescue of those sons and daughters of need and</p>
        <p>MAKE TIIE GOVERNMENT DO.THE NECE,SSARY WORK. He confessed that the matter was one N hich when fairly realized created a sense of sorrow, shame and indigna- tion." Governments seem to be able to waste money like water upon reckless schemes for Imperial aggrandizement but they hesitated to find help and honest livelihood for their own people. The. State must find the remedy for this condi tion.lt was an incumbent duty nod its doingadmited of no further delay. Amongst the Free Churches their leaders were prepared to lead tbem into fuller and franker relations with the course of work and labour, and whatever they I could do to show their sympathy ins great question like I that presented by the vast army of the unemployed, they ought to attempt. In measure as they became filled with the spirit of Christ they would become helpers of the helpless, succourers of the needy ' and deliverers of the oppressed. It may seem that what they could do • with that pitiable need was nothing in comparison with its extent, but if in obeying and trusting Him they discussed and agitated and promoted opinion, stirred up their rulers and created Governments which would do the right thing. He would make their little united endeavour compass a divine success. "At any rate we must not be indifferent. We must not stand aloof. We must stand by the cause of the weak and the needy and the helpless. It is the cause of our Master and therefore it is the cause of His diciples. Ministers and churches must abandon their too exclusive and selfish Christianity and be ready Ito make life worth living even for the poorest in the land."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-07-22_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>SAN KEY STATION IMPROVEMENT. --- Additional Booking Office to be</head>
        <p>SAN KEY STATION IMPROVEMENT. --- Additional Booking Office to be</p>
        <p>Provided.</p>
        <p>There was welcome news for the inhabitants of Great Sankey at the meeting of the Parish Council which waz held in the Council School on Monday evening. For some considerable time the Council has been agitating for an improvement in the booking arrangements at the Cheshire Lines station which hay been regarded as inconvenient and dangerous, as passengers leaving by the north platform have had to book on the south side and then cross over. To cross by the high- way bridge is a very roundabout procedure, and perhaps the majority of passengers have preferred to run the risk of walking over the metals. Recently , Mr. James Pinion, manager of the committee, inter- viewed a deputation from the Parish Council at the ' station and as a result the committee have decided to 1 I erect an additional booking office on the north platform. At the meeting of the Parish Council on Monday the Clerk (Mr. Joseph Smith) read' the following letter from Mr. Piniou:—Dear Sir,—With reference to your letter of the 16th nit and the meeting with your chair- man and some other members of your Council at Sankey on the 19th of the said month, I have pleasure in Inform- ing you that we shall provide a.booking office on the up platform at Sankey station and I trust this facility will be a convenience to the pass4agers using the station. The question of fares is still under consideration. The Chairman (Mr. G. Thompson) said they were much obliged to Mr. Pinion for the prompt way in tvhieh he had grant.d their request. The deputation met him on June 19th and he would have a great deal to bring before the committee, and he (Mr. Thompsou) did not think the Council could have received a inure prompt reply. They still hoped to have the ' fares to Warrington reduced, as that would be a great benedt to the township. Another improvement, which, if the committee could see their Way to carry it, out, would be much appreciated was the ' making of a footway from the other side of the bridge to the station, for the use of people living near the schools. He moved that the thanks of the Council be accorded to Mr. Pinion for the way in which he had taken up the matter and gained the extra facilities. Mr. H. Fairelough seconded and the motion was carried. .</p>
        <p>. • A vote of thanks was also passed to the local Press for their action in urging the improvement.</p>
