        <p>et KANO AFRICAN rAtittla with red tali. liemin VA to talk, 255., approval.—lidoKEAND, Seabright Villa, Castle-Uouglae, Scotland.</p>
        <p>Mr. BALFOUR, as WO A" REPUTATION FOR know, does not read EFFICIENCY " ! the newspapers, or</p>
        <p>he could not have failed to be struck by the chorus of anger and ridicule with which the ignominious collapse of the Redistribution proposals was greeted by his own "There is no disguising the fact,"says "The Globe, "that the Speaker's ruling :has placed the Prime Minister in a humiliating and almost ridicu- lous position. It seems almost incredible that in a matter so closely affecting the interests of the party and the reputation of the Government for efficiency, Mr. BALFOUR should have omitted the preliminary step of ascertaining whether the course he proposed to adopt was in order." This, from a journal which for a long time was a thick-and-thin partisan of the Government is sufficiently remarkable. The same tone was taken by "The Times," "The Standard," "The Morning Post" and " The Pall Mall Gazette." No Government has ever been more heartily condemned by its own organs. But Mr. BALFOUR'S spirits were not daBh3d. He went to the Foreign Office meeting of his party with the jaunty air of a man who had done everything entirely to his own satis• faction. He told the assembled Unionists that they must avert a dissolution in August by saving the Government from defeat in a snap division. As for Redistribution, he was quite unabashed. The Government would appoint a Boundary Commission in the Autumn, and pro- duce their Bill next year. Mr. CHAMBERLAIN gave a broad hint that the Bill would have to be something very different from the proposals which had just been dropped; but he declared that he had changed his mind about dissolution, and that the Government must be kept in office now and next Session too. This no doubt is very accommodating on Mr. CHAMBERLAIN'S part. He was all for an appeal to the country months ago. He is now willing that it should be postponed to stave off the inevitable catastrope as long as possible. He sees nothing illegitimate in Mr. BALFOUR'S resolve to appoint a Boundary Commission without the sanction of Parliament, and in the teeth of the very precedent which Mr. BALFOUR has cited in his haphazard way as a justification of a most unconstitutional act.</p>
        <p>It is not true that in 1867 Mr. Disnanu ap_ pointed a Boundary Commission in Mr. BALF)IIR'S fashion. On the cor_trary, he was at the pains to show that this sort of course would be most im- proper. In the House, the Prime Minister repeated the statement he had made at the Foreign Office ; bit he added that the Commission would not necessarily base its proceedings on the principles of the Resolutions he has abandoned. We :may take this to mean that there will be an entirely new basis for Redistribution in the second thoughts of the Government. But the new basis will not have the sanction of the House of Commons before the Commission goes to work. According to one report of the meeting at the Foreign Office, Mr. CHAMBERLAIN pointed out that the scheme, whatever it may prove to be, must be subject to amendment when it is presented to Parliament in a Bill. Whether Mr. BALFOUR assented to this view or not, we do not know. But it is quite upon the cards that the Bill will be unsatisfactory, and if it is to be amended, what will become of the Commis- sioners' labours? They will arrange the con- stituencies according to a settled plan, and if that plan should be upset the arrangement will not stand. This will do no more good to what "The Globe," with ironical humour, calls the "reputation of the Government for efficiency," than the recent ignominious collapse. But it is not Mr. BALFOUR'S reputation wnich is at stake. That has ceased to exist. It is the public interest. Itsis the reputation of Parliament as the repre- sentative of public opinion. The lamentable muddle in which Mr. BALFOUR finds himself and his party affects not them alone, but the whole nation. The only means of extrication is a dissolution, which would put an end to the Government and relieve the country of an incubus. But we are to be saddled with this for at least another year to gratify Mr. BALroua's appetite for evasion. 1</p>
        <p>Mr. Balfour's discredited and tottering Govern- ment has been defeated on an amendment by Mr. Redmond for the reduction of an Irish vote.lnlB9s. Lord Rosebery's ministry resigned after their de- feat on the question of cordite supply. The question now is: will the Balfourian cabinet dissolves as they are bound in honour to do now that the House of Commons has endorsed the oft-repeated verdict of the constituencies. In all probability they will not. Mr. Balfour has a special aptitude for airily waving aside questions of personal and political honour when they arise at an inconvenient</p>
        <p>moment, and we do not suppose he will cause a dissolution in August even if in honour bound. His speech immediately after the publication of the fatal figures proves that he by no means accepts them as notice to quit. The difficulty with Mr. Balfour lies in his being so thi2k- skinned. Like the organ-grinder you cannot con- vince him that he is not wanted. With true Balfourian sangfroid he said in answer to Mr. Redmond : " Unless I am able to carry on the business of the country in this House with dignity I shall certainly not attempt to carry it on." In the opinion of the country he has been quite unable to do so for two years at least. What can you do with a man of this type ?</p>
        <p>The grave problem of the unemployed cannot any longer be overlooked or even casually con- sidered. As a nation we have too often and too lightly reflected that " the poor we have always with us," forgetting at the same time our collec- tive and individual responsibility for the betterment of the conditions which render such a fact self-evident. That respon sibility is, we are glad to say, being rapidly brought home to the thinking people of this country. In addition to the poor—the chronically poor—wo have now the unemployed in great and increasing numbers. There is a vast difference between the two, and the realization of that difference constitutes the serious social problem with which the nation is now face to face. The unemployed at any rate, we should not have " always with us," though the poor will remain. In this wealthy land there should be some means of providing work for the two million people who are in want of it. Primarily the remedy can only come through an aroused public opinion—evidences of which,happily we have all around us. A word of commendation is due to the Rev. John Yong° and the Rev. J. F. Matthews for their pulpit utterances on Sunday. They haw o been the first ministers locally to realise the part Christianity is called upon to play in the sad "tragedy of poverty."</p>
        <p>A Labour demonstration on Saturday night at Bridge Foot likewise espoused the cause of the unemployed, and incidently endeavoured to per- suade or force the Government to pass the- Unemployed Bill during the present session. Unfortunately there is little hope of this much being achieved. Mr. Balfour has played the poor man false and displayed a ursadful inisconception of the needs and perils of the situation. No wonder that the tone of the meeting was distinctly savage, or that the language used exceaded the bounds of reason. We can find extenuation for Mr. Gavan Duffy's advocacy of the desperate expedient to "raid the shops' should nothing be done to mitigate the horrors of another winter for the workle.zs, but we must nevertheless deprecate most strongly this reckless appeal to the forces of disorder. We admit that the time has passed for the workers to discuss the question in meek terms,but there are many methods which must be tried before there is any thought of rioting. The Government. evidently does not appreciate the seriousness of the situation, or we should have no juggling with redistribution and such more or less academic matters in the face of a national peril. They must be brought to book. The unemployed are already widely organised, I and they can enforce their claims in unmis- takable fashion if they persistently agitate and demonstrate ! A Ministry of Llbour is a sad and crying need of the age. The future is big with the promise of it. In the meantime the utmost legiti- mate pressure must be brought to bear on those who rule but govern not to make the Unemployed Bill a real, workable measure,</p>
        <p>There is a gleam of light showing through the li+ze which has enveloped the question of physical instruction for school children. Failing to obtain satisfaction from the Gymnasium Committee, the Education Committee passed a resolution confin- ing the instruction to their own schools. This was well debated on Monday, with the result that there is now some likelihood of the Gymnasium being utilised to the full extent by the schools in the neighbourhood. Alderman Tinnion and his confr6res will, however, have to give up the idea of superintending the instruction given by the school-teachers. On this point the Educa- tion Committee are firm; and with qualified teachers their position is impregnable. We do not see that the Gymnasium Committee need complain if physical instruction is taken entirely out of their hands. They have rendered excellent public ser- vice in the past, and it should be a source of grati- fication to them that there is a prospect of physi- cal instruction being comprehensively carried out by the teachers in all our elementary schools. There is still scope for the labours of the Committee in the superintendence of gymnastic training, for which purpose the building in Palmyra-square . originated.</p>
        <p>In making a presentation to Mr. J. W. Howarth the Warrington Ragged School workers were honouring one to whom honour is due. Since he undertook the duties of financial secretary to the institution Mr. Howarth has spared no pains to urge its claims to public support, and perhaps the best testimony to his enthusiasm on behalf of the poor children is the fact that a boy who has benefited by the work was anxious to contribute his mite in the shape of 3d. to the testimmial fund. Mr. Howarth, in acknow- ledging the good wishes, took occasion to strongly criticise those who hold aloof from or express open hostility to the work of the mission. He sees in the institution great possibilities of social good, if only those who are in a position to lend much-needed assistance would come forward. Hampered as it has been by lowness of f•inds and other obstacles, the mission has already made itself a power in the town. It can fulfil a still more useful place if more earnest workers of the type of Mr. Howarth are willing to strive for the ideal of a socially "beautiful Warrington."</p>
        <p>The Society of American Women in London has just embarked upon an educational scheme which should have far-reaching and very excellent results, both in this country and in , the States. Inspired by the idea which the late Mr. Cecil Rhodes had in founding scholarships for Colonial and foreign students in the English Universities the society has resolved upon a plan for the creation of ninety-two perpetual scholarships at English Universities for women graduates fron3 America. By this means, two women from every State in the Union will be enabled to visit this country for a two years' course. The idea is to give women a chance of broadening their out- look, and no doubt students will, if the scheme is carried through successfully, appreciate to the full the enterprise of the society. Madame Thayer, who is the originator of the idea, and who has been talking to a representative of a London journal on the subject, is confident that the funds will be forthcoming. The money required to °stab- ' lish the scholarships will be about ,2,000,000, for it is held that they should each be worth £2O a-year to be of use. The proposal is to be brought before the big Conference of women's clubs which meets at Boston next year, and here Madame Thayer hopes that the money will be raised. On this side the Atlantic the hope will be echoed, fo.e the advantages of increased communication in this way between the two countries on the most impor- tant of all modern developments—education— cannot fail to be very real.</p>
        <p>During the annual training of the Ist Dumbartonshire Volunteers at Bogside, Ayrshire, on Wednesday, 50010 transport horses became restive and bolted,and a heevY waggon passed over some of the men who tried to stop them, killing one and injuring another.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-07-22_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <head>NOTES AND NOTIONS.</head>
        <p>NOTES AND NOTIONS.</p>
        <p>Retribution, and not redistribution will be the 'Government's chief concern ere long.</p>
        <p>* * * Hilda 'Wildblood has attended the National Schools at Leigh fn. six years and four months without once -being absent. She belies her name. * * * "The broadest and most tolerant of all denomina- tions" is the claim put forth for the Baptists by the Rev. H. Dunnico.</p>
        <p>* * * , The committee of Cheltenham Public Library have decided to "black" all betting news in the newspapers which appear on the stands in the public reading room.</p>
        <p>* * A prisoner who appeared before the Warrington magistrates on Monday pointed out that there was a lack of" co-operative " evidence against him. The Bench however decided that there was ample corrobor- ation and the delinquent is now one of His Majesty's guests.</p>
        <p>* 5 * Liberals, register! The list of voters for 1905 will be published on October Ist, and it is of the utmost importance that every loyal member of the party should see that hi 4 claim to exercise the franchise is duly recognised. All information may be obtained on -application at the Liberal Offices, Bewsey-street. * * * • Our Tory friends have come out in their true colours. They have been feeling decidedly blue at the results of the bye-elections, and a little of the prevailing tint -seems to have escaped during the small hours and fastened itself on the Liberal possters. This playful talk about counting chickens is ure indication of an uneasy spirit.</p>
        <p>* * * There is a Protestantism of a particularly militant type at Garston. On Sunday two rival- factions attempted to hold meetings on the same plot of land and lively scenes were the result. We are afraid such incidents-do a great deal more harm than good to the cause of religion. They afford an excellent Pportunity to the scoffer to exclaim "How these Christians love one another" ! * * * There was quite a novel works trip from Warring, ton last week end. The employees of Mr. A. H. Lee of Brick-street Tapestry works,journeyed to Prestatyn and camped for a couple of days. The experiences under canvas appear to have been of the happiest description, and the employees were able to commence ,work on Monday with a renewed vigour, thanks to the ' Lhealthful breezes of the pretty Welsh resort. * * * We have all been drenched by the watering-cart at one time or another, and can therefore sympathise with the lady at Llangollen who had her finery spoiled by the Urban Council sprinkler. Unlike most of us, however, she took the law into her own hands and mounting the cart belaboured the careless driver with , her umbrella. There was quite a fracas and now there are threats ,of summonses for assault, and damages all round. * * * , The first instalment of 115 of Warrington's poor girls will leave for the Children's Summer Camp at Prestatyn to-day {Saturday), and thence, onwards, the camp will be kept busy with successive batches of - Youngsters from the slums of Warrington. Funds are still wanted, and either Mr. A. H. Le. 3 or Mr: W. H. J. Jenkins will be delighted to receive cash contribu- -tions however large or small. * * * The " Heathens " have another grudge against the Warrington Corporation. In past years it has been rho practice on Walking Day to decorate the Warring- entrance to the village by means of streamers hung across the roadway. The introduction of the cars has " rendered the hanging of streamers impossible and the • villagers are now bemoaning the compulsory abandon- ment of this form of decoration. * _ *</p>
        <p>_ A lady bather at St. Bees, Cumberland, caught a shark, five feet long, the other day. She noticed the fish struggling in shallow water and hauled it unceremoniously ashore by the tail. That shark must have been suffering from the heat wave, we should imagine. It must have been a bit slow or it would r have fallen such an easy victim to a lady bather. e wonder if the latter would have been so bold had Ishe known the species of fish she was treating in so cavalier a fashion?</p>
        <p>* * * The danger and inconvenience of Sankey Station L.C.) will shortly be mitigated. Mr. Pinion, tnanager of the C.L.C., has agreed to provide a book- ' ing office on the North side in addition to the one on the South side. This will spare a lot of people a journey round the bridge and relieve others from the necessity of crossing the metals when they have booked. With, so many expresses passing through, and with a ,Curve at each end of the station, it is a wonder there Quve not been more fatalities at Sankey. * * * Among other unpleasant things that the Standard "a Company is responsible for are earthquakes— • •ttecording to a negro preacher. His explanation is as , , "De earf, my friends, revolves on axels, as we all xuovr. Somefin is needed to keep the axels greased, .7° when de earf was made, petrolyum was put inside , dat purpose. De Standard Oil Comp'ny comes along a:-nd strax dat petrolym by boring holes in the 'earf. De earf stix on its asel an' won't go round no inure, den there is a hot box, jest as if de earf was a big railway train—and den, my frien', dere is trouble." * * * Is The following fine herd of "bulls" is alleged to have found in a leading article in the " Western ewB" of Galway : —"Being the weaker, England artlanted her heel on the throat of Ireland fora Zt• ltury, and robbed us without pity or remorse." 10 rob a man of his purse, and then maltreat him for not having it, would pass muster amongst pitiless "rutal crimes, but to kill and slay a man to the point of death, and then murder him for not dying quick ,t flough ! is one point better in the catalogue of human teethl4°33'. It is enongh to make Irishmen set their and talk silently in groups." ,4 We are afraid that many inhabitants of Warrington not fully realize the glories of the country which irts the to wri, especially on the Cheshire side. Hill Cliff eat present is looking at its best and a \ walk '▪ rough its pleasant fields and well-wooded lanes laipnot fail to be refreshing to the jaded 'town worker. t. ere this country forming the background of a sea- resort it would be' boomed" as one of the most Itartning attractions. Imagine how much Blackpool ---,that desert of bricks and mortar—would be improved with pill Cliffe for a background?</p>
        <p>* * correspondent emphasises our note of last week jle,.,tbe need for swimming instruction if the public We hope the Baths Committee will give the titter earnest consideration now that they have such ii,ereArd of juvenile bathers. There is no reason why e provision of the desired facilities should cause ;13' financial loss on the part of the committee—at 03r rate to commence with. A considerable number I).f bathers would probably be willing to pay an addi- -11,.0nal fee for competent teaching. The question of i 1413 instruction for the juveniles s one more particu- t/ for the Education Committee. * * R:ltilletic pursuits receive every encouragement at the ,„7,_eondary School and the Success of the first annual s on Wednesday showed that the scholars are ed""rlg co-operators in this congenial part of the „tleational scheme. There were one or two novel i'ents—notably the bicycle races for both boys and • tßhil.s. The boys bad to run round the track guiding te%r machines which, of course, shoped a marked beil.`lencY to become unruly, especially when they were zaelr's wheeled backwards. The winner of the girls' tin,„.e was the competitor who succeeded id going at the "est pace d the strenuous efforts of the riders to blaint • an • inn their equilbriums on machines which were '4lost at a standstill were highly amusing to watch. * * * tili‘Tok°l.thern Union adherents generally will not, we be i be enamoured of the new reconsttniction scheme. ten -League will be no League at all in the ordinary and it is to be feared that there mat tbe a diminution of public interest in for Cues which form no part of a real struggle th suPremacY among the competing clubs. the legislators had any confidence in the scheme - itselves they would not have added a proviso to r,sejecert to the old order of things should it not be a egB. This is asking for trouble with a vengeance. or,',.s,e constant changes are having a prejudicial effect lar Te Ir future of the Union. which is, unfortunately, the ota • rt is to he retained .gitanie .wilt have to be popularised by render- nm • „ ore scientific and open. Some of the tactics fir'lmoyed last season were a disgrace to clubs in the division and did much to discontent the followers • 11°Le gamy.</p>
