      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-07-22_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <head>LOCAL NEWS.'</head>
        <p>LOCAL NEWS.'</p>
        <p>Coming Events. The third anniversary of the Rev. Herbert Dunni co's pastorate at Golborne-street Baptist Church, will be celebrated by special services to-morrow (Sunday). Mr. J. L. Paton, high master of Manchester Grammar School,.will be the speaker at an open meeting of Wycliffe P.S.A.. Society to-morrow (Sunday) afternoon. Death of Mr. David Walton.</p>
        <p>Many of our readers will regret to hear of the death of Mr. Dar id Walton, a well-known Warringtonian, Who passed away at Wigan on Tuesday. The deceased whb was forty-nine years of age, was a:native of the town and had held the post of traveller, for the firm of John Cliorley, iron and steel merchant, for twenty years. He leaves a widow and three children. The funeral took place at the Warrington Cemetery on Thursday afternoon. Trap Accident.</p>
        <p>On Monday morning Mrs, Hartley, wife of Major Hartley, of Orford Barracks, met with a rather serious accident. She was driving a horse attached to a high dog cart, in company with a lady, two children, and the coachman, and when passing along Scotland-road the horse slippedand fell,-throwing Mrs. Hartley and her lady friend out of the vehicle. Inspector Taylor and P.C. Corbett, who happened to be on the spot, at once • rendered assistance to the ladies who were seKerely shaken, but otherwise fortunately uninjured. • ASK for CUNNINGHAM'S HIGH-CLASS .MINSRAL WATERS.—Advt. Cattle and Horse Fairs.</p>
        <p>Warrington's half-yearly cattle fair was held on the plot of land in Arpley fields adjoininethe ground belonging to the Warrington Cricket Club on Monday. The number of animals was much smaller than usual, but the quality was quite up to the standard of previous years. Calvers brought in from £l5 to £22 ' • heifers, from £9 to £ll ; and 'younger cattle, from £6 to £7. This is the first time the fair has lnen held on this spot, and, judging from the opinion expressed by the farmers present, the change is a move in the right direction, The horse fair was held on Tuesday.—An increased number of animals were offered, but the quality was not generally so good. One or two animals however, fetched as much as £4O.</p>
        <p>"PREmtnn." CYCLES at 12 guineas with change speed gear &amp;a, unevalled.—J. S. GARLAND, 51 to 55, Bridge at., Warrington.—Advt. Cottage Homes, Padgate. On Saturday the whole of the boys from the above Homes to the number of 101 enjoyed the hospitality of the Cheshire Lines Engineers Cricket Club, on their field in Battersby-lane, Warrington. Needless to say, the youngsters thoroughly enjoyed themselves romping about the grounds in the bright sunshine and engaging in good old English sports. Then they all did full justice to the good things provided for them. Mr. G. Walker presented the prizes and presents to each boy. A special prize of a watch and chain was voted to John Hart as being the most popular boy in the homes. The committee consisted of Messrs. Hill, Smith, Irvin Wych and Riley, who carried out all arrangements in grand style, assisted by the officers of the Homes. The committes desire to take this opportunity of thanking the few outside friends who helped financially to provide the good things for this deserving annual treat.</p>
        <p>Scholars' VI p. The sixth annual trip in connection with the upper division of boys of the Heath Side Schools took place on Saturday, when Miller's Dale and Buxton were visited. Some forty boys, under the charge of Messrs. F. J. Emson and A. Malcolm, undertook the journey. Warrington was left shortly before nine o'clock and Miller's Dale was reached in good time. The walk through Miller's Dale, Chee Dale and Ashwood Dale and on to Buxton—some six miles—proved very interesting: On arrival in Buxtoirthe various objects of interest in the town were visited. After lunch at Buxton the home journey was commenced, Warrington being reached at nine o'clock. there not having been a single hitch to mar the day's outing. The expenses of the trip were provided for by the boys themselves, who had saved up their coppers in the school bank since Februaty last, though several of the managers of the schools rendered assistance by private donations in order that the boys might have some additional treat during the day's outing, not allowed for in the savings.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-07-22_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>PUBLIC BENEFIT. UNDER TEIE Cuacx FOR BEST BOOTS IN LANCASIIIRE.—AdVt.</head>
        <p>PUBLIC BENEFIT. UNDER TEIE Cuacx FOR BEST BOOTS IN LANCASIIIRE.—AdVt.</p>
        <p>A Novel Outing.</p>
        <p>The employees of Mr. Arthur H. Lee's Tapestry Works had a very novel and most enjoyable outing last week for their annual pic-nic. Mr. Lee enter- tained them at the Warrington Camp for Poor Chil- dren, Prestatyn, prior to the children going to camp. The party consisting of about 70, arrived in camp at about 5 o'clock on Friday evening and stayed until about the same time on Sunday evening, many sleep- ing under canvas for the first time in their lives. The novelty of it, (and the hard beds) did not allow much sleep the first night, the camp being well astir at four 'o'clock on Saturday morning. Those who saw ladies actually wading at this early hour, must have thought Prestatyn besieged by some Ivery 'energetic people Renewed energy was however displayed and well stored up, at breakfast, which was served at about ' eight o'clock, the cook being somewhat alarmed by the vitality shown in this direction. Visits to Dyserth Falls, Rhyl, and iihe surrounding neighbourhood madel up a very pleasant day, all meals being served gipsy fashion in camp. Night found all tired enough to sleep On the proverbial clothes line. The sentries onl duty the second night reported quiet watches, the only" sound of revelry" being a very strong suspicion of heavy snoring which they ascertained to be only too true, especially (confidential) in the ladies' quarters. A visit to l'sh Mountain was made on Sunday by most of the 'party, justice being done to a speciality of Mr. Cook's called " Sea Pie "upon returning from the climb. The return to Warrington was made after tea, the party arriving about 8 o'clock, fearing they might be taken for real gipsies, so much had "Old Sol" tanned them. All voted it a most enjoyable out- ing, and thought that if "big uns "could enjoy it so much, what a treat it must be to the "little uns," to most of whom it is the only chance of seeing the wonder-world of mountain and sea, and breathing delightful pure air. A collection will be made amongst the employees for the childremand an appeal is respectfully made to the general public to support this good work and thereby brighten somewhat the lives of very poor children. About 450 boys and girls are being taken to carni) during the next *few weeks, but funds are badly needed to carry it on and if possible to extend it. The Hon. Treasurer is Mr. Arthur ,H. Lee, Tapestry Works, Brick-street.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-07-22_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, July 22, 1905.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, July 22, 1905.</p>
