        <p>UNDER THE CLOCK for Good BOOTS.—Advt. Ambulance Corps at Walton. Warrington is fortunate in possessing an enthu- siastic band of ambulance workers whose qualifica-</p>
        <p>tion to render " first aid " to the injured has proved of incalculable service. On Saturday afternoon the members of the Warrington corps joinedwith contingents from Messrs. Crosfield's division, the Winsford, Alsager' and linntsford divisions in a route march to Walton Lea where a field had been placed at their disposal by Sir Gilbert Greenall, Bart. The men to the number of about fifty assembled at the Gymnasium under the direction of Chief Superinten- dent Captain Wareing; F.R.G.S., Superintendent Baldry, and second officer and hon. secretary, Mr. T. if. Hulse. Headed by Messrs, P. Walker and Son's band, under the leadership of Mr, J. Peterson, they marched along Palmyra Spare, Rylands-street, Bridge-street, and New Road, to Walton. Here, for about an hour and a half, the officers drilled the men under the superintendence Of the District Superintendent Secretary, of No. 4, North-Western District, Mr. A. L. Garnett, of Burnley. Through the kindness of Lady Greenall an excellent knife and fork tea was provided. Afterwards Captain Waring pro- posed a vote of thanks to Lady Greenall for providing the tea and to Sir Gilbert for the use of the field. Mr.</p>
        <p>Garnett fitlitonded.Speaking generally he remarked that the S. John's Ambulance Brigade was carried on by the working classes, who were the backbone of the movement. There were a few o them who, in social</p>
        <p>parlance, might not have to use their hands for physical labour, bat they worked very hard with the brain, and to those who were sufficiently thoughtful to think of them on these occasions they were Much . .</p>
        <p>obliged. The motion was carried with hearty applause. Captain Waring observed that the recent victory of the Warrington team at Southport showed that the men must have worked hard to obtain the cup from the best ambulance teams in the North-West of Lancashire.</p>
        <p>Wear, hear.)-on the motion of Mr. Hulse, a hearty vote of thanks was accorded to the band for their services. The men returned to Warrington at about</p>
        <p>seven o'clock by way of Stockton Heath and the Causeway.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-07-22_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>CRAWFORD'S GOLDEN BUTTER</head>
        <p>CRAWFORD'S GOLDEN BUTTER</p>
        <p>BISCUITS.</p>
        <p>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.</p>
        <p>Anniversary Services.</p>
        <p>The anniversary services in connection with the Elles- mere-street Sunday school were held on Sunday. In the morning and evening, at the Mission, Mr. J. Upson occupied the pulpit, while in the afternoon, in Bold- street Weslevan Church, an address was delivered by the Rev. G. H. Ca`rnson. Special hymns were rendered by the children of the Mission, assisted by other scholars in the circuit. Mr. Levi Richardson was the conductor, I Mr. Hickman presiding at the organ. There was a good congregation at each service.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-07-22_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>Wycliffe P.S.A. Society.</head>
        <p>Wycliffe P.S.A. Society.</p>
        <p>The usual meeting was held on Sunday, when there waa a fair attendance. Mr. W. Beswick presided and urged the brethren to build up a manly character, full of brightness and usefulness. Mr J. A. Jamieson, Man- chester spoke upon "Like a nail fastened in a sure place." As in the practical world, we were always mindful he said, that the nails driven were in a sure place, so in the spiritual world we ought to be very careful where we drive the nail. There were many beliefs in the world, but Our great foundation was Christ, and we built upon that sure foundation, and we drive our nails in the structure of faith, hope, and charity. Thea our temple of love, VMS a fit place for God to dvr3ll in. The address was listened to with great interest. The Secretary announced that at the open meeting next Sunday. Mr. J. L. Paton, MA., high master of Manchester Grammar school would give the address.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-07-22_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <head>Rea. H. Minnie° and the Baptist Congress.</head>
        <p>Rea. H. Minnie° and the Baptist Congress.</p>
        <p>The Baptist Church was crowded on Sunday evening last, when the Pastor have an address on thoughts sug- gested by the " Baptist World's Congress." in the course of his address the preacher stated that the idea of such aCongress was put forth by Dr. Gibbon as far back as the year 1790. The scheme, however, was dropped because of the great continental war brought about by Napoleon. The idea was again taken up in 1891 by Col. Griffin, but nothing was done. The American news- papers, however, took the matter up, and four years ago Dr. Prestridge, an American, threw himself ardently into the plan, and it was largely, if not wholly due to that gentleman that the great Congress had taken place. Dr. Prestridge had all along contended that England was entitled to the honour of calling the Con- gress on account of being the mother country, and because it would give the Baptists throughout the world an opportunity of ranging themselves alongside the British Baptists in their great fight against the education Acts of 1902 and 1903. Mr. Minnie() then proceeded to point out what is not generally known—that the Baptist denomination is the largest Protestant body in the world, representing 7,000,000 members. At the recent congress 4,000 delegates were present representing all nationalities in all parts of the world, and he did not hesitate to say that such a gathering had never been witnessed since the day of Pentecost. They were gathered there from all parts, in one place and with one accord and every man heard the Gospel in his own tongue. Mr. Dunnico then proceeded to give a brief account of the various meetings and the particular features of the congress. He pointed out that notwithstanding that within the Baptist denomination there were several very narrow sections, yet speaking generally, the denomination was the broadest and most tolerant of all denominations.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-07-22_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>PRESENTATION TO MR. J. W. HOWARTH.</head>
        <p>PRESENTATION TO MR. J. W. HOWARTH.</p>
        <p>His Services to the Ragged School and the Library.</p>
        <p>A REPLY TO CRITICS.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. W. Howarth, the assistant librarian at tha Warrington Municipal Library, who will shortly leave the town to take over the duties of librarian at Chelms- ford, has rendered very valuable service to the Warring- ton Ragged School Mission as financial secretary. On Thursday evening he was the recipient of a present consisting of a purse of gold and a beautifully framed photograph from the teachers, workers, and a number of friends of the mission. The presentation was made at a meeting held at the Ragged School, Bank street, Mr. Alfred Huime presiding iu the absence of Mr. James Dodds who telegraphed that indisposition ' prevented his attendance. Amongst others present were Councillor Skellanti (founder and superintendent of the mission), Councillor Pemberton, Mr. %V. Owen (treasurer), and Mr. J. Marsh (secretary of the testi- monial fund). . _ _</p>
        <p>I The CHAIRMIN said he could speak more of Mr. Howarth's work in connection with the Library than on) behalf of that Mission. It afforded him very real pleasure to take part in that gathering, because he had the highest respect for Mr. Howarth. He was a man whom he (the speaker) thought hadnot been appreciated as he should have been in his work in Warrington. They all remembered those dark days when the Warrington Library had no catalogue—(laughter)—when the last numbers of the old catalogue, dirty, dog- eared, torn, and disease-laden, had crawled away to the dust bin to die—(laughter)—before a new catalogue, perhaps' was thought of, when the Warring- ton Library might fitly have been compared to a garden whose choicest blooms were being wasted on the desert air, and when the ratepayers were not merely not allowed to walk on the grass, but had no means of acmes to the paths, and could simply pluck a bad here and there that was hanging through the rail- ings. In those days Mr. Howarth was of real value to Warrington because he was in himself a walking catalogue. (Hear, hear.) He was a man who had understood his work thoroughly but he had been badly treated. Whilst he had been denied any reasonable increase of salary he had had to be content to breath the same air as someone who had been perhaps treated very well in that respect, and it was not satisfactory to labour under those conditions. He (Mr. Halme) was afraid the Town Council, like a good many individuals amongst them, were very glad to throw their responsibilities on to someone else. It was all very fine when people were not satisfied with the way that things were done for the Council to say in a superior sort of way "Oar motto is Deus (ISt inermenturn." They knew from what had been going on lately that it was the Council and its committees which gave these little increases—(laughter)—but they felt that in deserving cases they gave too little whilst in others they gave too much. He (Mr. Ffulme) felt very strongly that Mr. Howarth was the man who had made the library as useful as it had been to the Warrington public, and he had not received the treatment die deserved at the hands of his committee. In wishing him success in his new undertaking he would like to express the hope that Mr. Howarth's new committee would appre- ciate him at his true worth. (Applause.) Councillor SKELLAND. before handing the purse of gold to Mr. Howarth, said they were rendering honour to one to whom honour was due. Mr. Howarth had been connected with the work there about four years and they had found him to be honest, straightforward and honourable—in fact, he had proved the best financial secretary they had ever had. (Hear, hear.) Before he came Alderman B-innett assured him I (the speaker) that Mr. Howarth would be eminently fitted for the work, and that had proved to be the case. ' Since he took over the duties the Mission had made con- siderable progress, and many new friends and subscribers had been gained. He hoped the workers would persevere in their effort to make Warrington more beautiful. He was very sorry Mr. Howarth was leaving them, but they were delighted that he had been promoted to a place where they hoped his services would be better appre- ciated. (Anplanse). Councillor Skelland then handed the purse of gold to Mr. Howarth. In acknowledging the gift Mr. HOWARTH said he had tried to do his little best in a quiet way and whether he had succeeded or not he left to the judgment of others. He related how he came to take up the Ragged School work, pointing out that after attending a children's fancy dress ball he was painfully struck by the contrast he saw in the appearance of some little ones, shoeless and wet through, who were huddled in a doorway which he passed on his way home. A week or two later he went to the poor children's treat and there made the acquaintance of Councillor Skelland with the result that he became actively associated with the mission. He had not accomplished a twentieth part of the work which he set out to do, for he saw great possibilities in the institution. He had tried to do his best under very difficult circumstances. He alluded to what seemed to be an organized opposition against that mission. He had tried his best to fight it dawn, but it emulated from men in authority who ought to know better. He was sorry to have to speak so straightly, but he knew it was so and until that was overcome the mission workers had a big up-hill fight to face. The opposition senned very unreasonable. The mission was full of possibilities of good social work amongst hundreds of neglected little ones in this town. Why did not the town authorities realize that and try and go forward and help on the work instead of picking and putting a spoke in the wheel at every opportabity? He could not understand it. If he had not been a Corporation servant he should have spoken out very strongly long ago. If the name of "Ragged School. ' was offensive to many of the leading men and women of the town let them change it to "The poor children's institution ''—and make it what it ought to be, a place where, especially in winter time, the children could spend a happy hour or t-vo away from their miserable home conditions. They had nothing to do with parental responsibilities there. It was the duty of the authorities of the town and of the nation, for the sake of the future generation, to try to do all they could to make the lives of poor, neglected children healthy to train them up morally and to feed and clothe them if their parents would not or could not do so. If they could do it they should he made, but if they could not it was another matter. Mr. Howarth concluded by heartily thanking the subscribers. Mr. J. OWEN then presented Mr. Howarth with a I framed photograph of the teachers and mission workers and paid a tribute to his earnest and valuable work. Referring to the object of the mission Mr. Owen ex- I pressed regret that they were hampered in their efforts for cleanliness by being called upon to pay trifling water bills. He considered that was a shame. (Hear hear.) Councillor PEMBERTON then spoke of Mr. Howarth's, work at the Library, painting out that he would bear personal testimqny to his worth as he had had to use the Library very frequently indeed for reference purposes. Bid as the Library had been, and strong as had been the criticism, words failed him to describe what the position would have been if Mr. Howarth had not been there. What was Warrington's loss would be Caelmsford's gain. (Applause.) If it had been possible there were certain members of the com- mittee and of the Town Council, irrespective of parties or politics, who would have retained Mr. Howarth's services, but unfortunately they had against them many co-opted members who were mostly in favour of museum work. They-could not understand why a person should want a book when he could go and look at beauti' ful butterflies, and stuffed birds and animals.(Laughter.) The public who paid for the library should call the tune, and if they wanted books they should have them. (Applause.) A great deal more than half the income of the library was spent on the museum and therefore the people who really wanted to read books were unfairly treated. It seemed to him that the majority of people who went on the Town Council were the rate- payers very humble servants when they were seeking votes, but they were very much the masters when they had obtained them. Councillor Pemberton emphasised the value of the Ragged school pointing out that it was little use attempting to educate children who were under-fed. By Mr. Howarth's departure the public of Warrington would lose a good servant and the Ragged School an energetic worker.lt had been his (Councillor Pemberton's) painful duty often to have to criticise public officials, and therefore it was a great pleasure to him to be able to praise one. (Applause.) During the evening letters expressing high apprecia- tion of Mr. Howarth's good work nal enclosing sub- scriptions were reacrfrom Mr. J. Charlton Parr and Mrs Croefield.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-07-22_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>GRAPPENHALL'S NEW RECTOR.</head>
        <p>GRAPPENHALL'S NEW RECTOR.</p>
        <p>Rev. H. V. Pigot Accepts the Living.</p>
        <p>The Rev. H. V. Pigot, ILA., vicar of St. Thomas', Stockton 11lath, has been offered and has accepted the living of St. Wilfrid's Church, Grappenhall, rendered vacant by the death of the Rev. Richard Greenall. The patron of the living, which has a gross income of almost £6OO, is Sir Gilbert Greenall, Bart. The Rev. H. V. Pigot graduated at Oxford taking his 8.A., in 1877 and his KA, in 1885_ He was ordained Deacon in 1887 and priest the following year. His first curacy was that of Bolton-le-Sands which he held from 1887 to 1890, when he came to Warrington as Vicar of St. •Paul's, being held in high esteem. In 1809 he was appointed to his present living and during his residence m Stockton Heath has made himself popular with his parishioners, by whom his impending departure will be regretted. '</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-07-22_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>SECONDARY SCHOOL SPORTS.</head>
        <p>SECONDARY SCHOOL SPORTS.</p>
        <p>List of Winners.</p>
        <p>In their delightfully situated recreation field at Pad- nate the scholars attending the Warrington Municipal Secondary School held their first annual sports on Wednesday afternoon. The weather was ideal, the sun shining_brilliantly while a gentle breeze tempered the heat. It was not surprising, therefore, that many of the parents and friends of the scholars, as well as mem- bers of the Education Committee, availed themselves of the opportunity of spending a few hours amidst such pleasant surroundings. A very interesting pro- gramme,embracing thirty-one events,had been arranged, and everything passed off without a hitch. Some exceptionally keen contests were witnessed, boys and girls striving hard for honours in their respective races Instead of prizes being offered to individual scholars, two shields NC ere offered for competition among the forms of the Upper and Lower Schools. The Upper School consisted of all the pupil teachers and Forms 7,6, and 5, the Lower School of Forms 4,3, 2 and 1.</p>
        <p>The aggregate marks obtained were as follows:— Upper School: Scholarship P.T.'s, 60:1st year P.T.'5,59 ; Form VIT., 302: Form VI., 1118 • Form V., 157. Lower School :Form 1V.,303 :Form lll,lo9Form 11., 14 ;Form; L. 60. The names of all those in the winning forms who gained marks will be engraved on the shields. The winners were—Form Vii in the Upper school and Form IV. in the Lower School.</p>
