      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-07-22_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>WORD TO THE WISE.</head>
        <p>A WORD TO THE WISE.</p>
        <p>The fickle nature of the climate of this country often gives rise to a variety of ailments and complaints, which assuming at first the form of only a slight indisposition, if neglected and unheeded may become the germ of serious diseases, which will undermine the constitution, and finally result in a long and lingering illness, and perhaps terminate fatally. The variable temperature and the changeable weather of the last month or so, makes it incumbent on all of us to exercise wise and prudent precautions to counteract the evil effects which the weather may have had upon our health and comfort. Already we frequently hear such complaints as No appetite," "These frequent headaches," "This languid feeling," and dozens of other expressions which all point to the urgent need of a good Tonic. Now there are several tonic mixtures offered to the Public, but none which have been so uniformly successful as Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters. The Vegetable Tonic. This Preparation is acknowledged to be The Best Remedy of The Age for Nervousness, Weakness. Chest Affections, Palpitation of the Heart, Indigestion, Liver Complaint, and Influenza. The numerous cases of marvellous cures effected, and the restoration to perfect health, of those who had long suffered and had vainly tried other remedies, can be accounted for by simply stating the effect which Gwilyrn Evans' Quinine Bitters has upon the System. Ist. It strikes at the source of the disease, and by removing the causes of disease, the evil effects soon vanish. 2nd. It strengthens that part of the system which is weakest, and therefore, most liable to the attacks of colds and all diseases. 3rd. It purifies the blood, and thus gives new life and force to all parts of the body, for disease cannot exist where there is a free circulation of pure blood- 4th. It gives healthy action to the digestive organs and to the liver, thus aiding to keep away Indigestion and the host of different forms of disease which re- ult from it, sth. By removing impurities, strengthening the weak parts of the system, and purifying the blood,the human frame is well fortified to withstand the attacks of disease. It is repelled, and its place taken by health and strength, through the effective action of Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters, The Vegetable Tonic. Sold everywhere in bottles 2s. 9d. and 4s. lid, each, or will be sent carriage free for these prices, direct from the Sole Proprietors :—Quinine Bitters Manufacturing Com- pany, Limited, Llanelly, South \Vales. Beware of Imitations. See that you get the genuine Preparation with the name, " Gwilym Evans "on Label, Stamp, and Bottle, without which none is genuine.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-07-22_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <head>BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES.</head>
        <p>BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES.</p>
        <p>CAUGHT RED-11ANDED.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington Police Court on Monday Fred Davies, no settled abode, and James Higham, Bank- street, were charged with stealing a quantity of block tin value .ClO,the property of the Whitecross, Company Limited.—Mr. Steel, who appeared to prosecute stated that on the night of the 11th lust, James Woods, the watchman employed by the prosecutors went round the works and found everything was right.At twenty:minutes past midnight he was again going his round and had reached the stores when he saw certain bars of block tin on the floor. Immediately on turning the corner he saw two men running away. fie went for the lodge man and returned to the stores, which he entered, leav- ing the lodge-man outside. A few minutes later the lodge-man called to him, and on going outside he saw his colleague holding the prisoner Davies. Entrance 1 to the stores had been effected by means of a fanlight, ! and a tin had been missed through the window which had been opened from the inside. The prisoner Higham was subsequently arrested by Superintendent Monaghan and Detective Forster. When charged Davies said " I got in through the , skylight which was smashed and opened the window and • let fligham in, and then handed the tin through the window to another man. Higham got out and let the other man get away." Higham said "I thought he (Davies) was a man. He has given the game away right enough. If I had been caught inside I would not have given anyone else away." Higham also threatened what he would do for Davies when he got the chance. —Prisoners were remanded.—On Thursday, prisoners were again brought before the magistrates.—Mr. Steel explained that two other men were implicated and that the policehad not been able to arrest them. He asked for is further remand until Monday.—The application was granted.</p>
        <p>FACTORY_ ACT PROSECUTIONS.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington Police Court on Monday, Edward Eddison, Mersey-street, was charged in four instances with employing women contrary to the provisions of the Factory Act—Mr. Lewis, H.M. Inspector of factories, stated that the defendant employed women as fustian cutters' and was entitled to have them at work from 7 a.m. to 7 p.m. On the 2.9 th June, Inspector Eatock visited the defendants works at 5.35 a.m. and found four women at work.—Defendant said he left the key to the works with his foreman and had no idea that the women started work so early.—The foreman was called and stated that he lodged with one of the women who took the key without his knowledge. A fine of us. Gd. (in the costa) ass inflicted in the first case, defendant in the other three instances being ordered to pay the cost of the summonses.—lnspector Lewis stated that in any case where the defendant was not guilty he v..as entitled to bring the guilty person into court to answer to the charges.—Alice Higharn, Haydockstreet, was summoned for a similar offence.—Defendant stated that the woman was at work without her knowledge. She had been ill and had left the key with the women she employed. Defendant was fined in the costs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-07-22_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>EXTK7SIVB THEFT OF LEATHER.</head>
        <p>EXTK7SIVB THEFT OF LEATHER.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington Police Court on Thursday two youths, Sidney Blease, 37, Hoyle-street, and Joseph Maguire, Stoney-yard were charged with stealing a quantity of leather, the property of the ranners-lane Tanning Co.—Superintendent Monaghan said that at one time Blease was employed by the prosecutors but left about 18 months ago. It came to his knowledge that the prisoners had recently been spending money very freely buying new clothes and driving about with a horse and trap, and selling leather. The previous evening he arrested them and questioned them as to how the leather came into their possession. Maguire would say nothing. Blease, however, admitted they had stolen it from the Tanner's-lane tannery. He said the two got on the railway siding and then into the yard over a wall. They placed the leather in a waggon and then hired a horse end trap and went round selling it. They sold a quantity to a man who resided in Catherine- street, another who lived in Flowley-lane, and one who lived in Market-street.—Mr. John Fairclough : These 'people must have known the lads could not come by the leather honestly.—Superintendent Monaghan : They said they represented a gentleman at Lymm, for whom they were selling it.—Prisoners were remanded until Monday.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-07-22_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>Warrington Borough Police Court.</head>
        <p>Warrington Borough Police Court.</p>
        <p>Minor Cases.</p>
        <p>SATURDAY.—Before Mr. R. T. Fairclough (presiding) Aldermen Tinnion, and Mr. A. J. Jolley. ASSAIILTS.—Mary Fisher, 2, Welsh's-yard, was charged with having been drunk and disorderly in Barb:mid- -street on Friday night, with assaulting r, John McKay, and with breaking a window, value Is. Prosecutor said that on Friday night he heard a disturbance going on outside his house. He went to the door, when the prisoner rushed at him and struck him in the face. He went into the house imd locked • the door, and after- wards heard a smash of glass and found that, the prisoner had broken his window. Superintendent Monaghan said this made the prisoner's 10th appearance. A fine of 10s. and costs was imposed.—David Buckley, 159, Longford-street, was charged on a warrant with assaulting Mary Ann Buckley, on July Bth. Com- plainant said the prisoner was her brother-in-law. He •came to her house on Saturday night and used filthy language. Ho was asked to leave, but said he would please himself. She then went to take hold of him to 'pot him out when hestruck her in the face, and later challenged her to -fight. Prisoner was fined ss. and costs.—William Beckett, 74, School-brow, was charged with assaulting his wife, Annie Beckett, on July Bth.- Complainant stated that on Friday night, her husband .came home and would not have his tea. He knocked her down, hanged her head on the floor, and kicked her Prisoner said the complainant's sister was always in the house, and he objected to her being there, and that was the cause of the bother. Prisoner was fined in the costs-12s.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-07-22_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>BLIND CYCLISTS' MIRACULOUS ESCAPE.</head>
        <p>BLIND CYCLISTS' MIRACULOUS ESCAPE.</p>
        <p>Two London cyclists, touring from Camberwell to Manchester, had a miraculous ascape from death at Ashbourne, Staffordshire, on Wednesday night. The men, who were brothers, named Grantham, were riding a tandem, and the one behind was blind. Coming down Derby-road bill, one of the most dangerous hills in the ,country, the machine swerved, and the men were dashed with great violence against a factory wall. One was picked up insensible and bleeding profusely, and the .blind man was bruised.</p>
        <p>TRUSSES.—The new Water Pad Truss, never gets Hard.—The new Washable Waterproof Trusses always •clet.n, cool and comfortable.—The Triangular Fluid Pad Truss is effectual in most difficult cases. Trusses of every known description.—WlNTE St, WRIGHT, Surgical Instrument and Artificial Lib Makers, 98, Renshaw it. Liverpool. (Advt 2.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-07-22_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>(ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. 3</head>
        <p>(ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. 3</p>
        <p>LOVE AND A MOTOR - CAR.</p>
        <p>BY COWRIE COLQUFIOUN,</p>
        <p>Author of "The Finger of Fate," "His Better Self," "A Son of the South," "The Winning of Frida," &amp;c.</p>
        <p>' The Rev. Septimus Sisson sat in his dingy sitting-room afflicted with melancholia. He was often in this mood, for he was poor and forlorn. One hundred pounds per annum, most of which sum found its way into his landlady's pocket, was all that he had to keep up the dignity of the curate of St. Paul's; and as he had other calls upon him—for his generous instincts had been accurately gauged by sundry choir boys interested in the formation of cricket and football clubs— he found the struggle for eNitence sufficiently keen. So much so, that it had come to this—he must eke out his stipend somehow without throwing himself on the fund which exists to prevent young curates from absolutely starving. What was he to do? That was the ques- tion. He could not write for the newspapers. There was not enough literary work for :the second and third sons of middle-class parents, much less for a seventh son in a curacy worth one hundred pounds per annum. Besides, every- body wrote nowadays. But everybody did not play. And the curate looked at his violin, the only companion of his solitude, and wondered whether he could not turn his skill with the instrument to good account. "The curate of St. Paul's taking in pupils! How funny!" And he laughed half-heartedly at the idea. The next moment he remembered that his position was not a subject for levity. As if the Rev: Septimes was ever guilty of such a crime against the proprieties. He must End another way out. He thought hard, but not even the ghost of an idea entered his head, and weary of cudgelling his brains he at last sought diversion in a news- paper. He found little in the news to interest him, and turned to the "Wants." What was this ? His attention was arrested by an advertise- ment, and he read it again and again. "Wanted, lessons in violin playing from a first- class amateur.—Address, Box 34, Gazette Office." "A first-class amateur !" Well, he had always been considered such, he told himself. And as if to test his own skill, he seized his violin, placed an "Andante" by Chopin on the music-stand, and began to play as though his life dopended on a correct rendering of the piece. "Dear me, what's come over Mr. Sisson ?" exclaimed the landlady to herself in the room below. "Playing his fiddle at this time of night." : It was, indeed, late for music practice in this quiet street of surburbia. But the curate heeded ' not the hour. He played one piece after another. Bach, Meyerbeer, Rossini, Schubert—he inter- preted them all. And being satisfied with las performance he sat down and answered the advertisement. His melancholy had now disappeared. He was filled with a mighty hope, the hope of earning a little extra money to tide him over his difficulty. Great was his surprise on coming down to his boiled egg, toast and tea three days afterwards, to find a scented envelope on his table. He tore It open with feverish haste. "A pupil! A pupil!" he cried, and read in delicately feminine handwriting: "Will Mr. S. Sisson please call at the above address on Wednesday afternoon at three o'clock."</p>
        <p>That was all. - For the Rev. Septimus Sisson the potentiali- ties of this scented note were very great. Clearly here was a pupil who would be prepared to pay well for excellent tuition. Then a doubt came into his mind. "Was it right," he asked himself, "for a curate to teach the violin for money?" He argued the matter, and came to the con- culsion that he was doing the Church a service by keeping off the Sustentation Fund. At any rate, he would not be guilty of duplicity. He would go to Dover-street in his true colours—a humble clergyman of the Church, who found it necessary to utilise his gift of music as a sub- sidiary means of maintenance. Accordingly the Rev. Septimus shaved himself unusually clean on Wednesday morning, put on his newest-shaped collar, brushed his coat well, and set out for the mansion in Dover-street with his fiddle-case in his hand. The footman surveyed him from lug hat to his boots as he ushered him into the library, and wondered what was the connection between a clergyman and a fiddle-case. The curate sat in an armchair richly upholstered in morocco, and very nearly broke a command- ment, so great was his admiration, if not envy, of the collection of books at either side of the room. Suddenly he beard the frouirou of a woman's dress, and the next moment there appeared in the room, almost before he had time to rise, a young lady in white, beautiful as a goddess, with laughing eyes and a rosebud of a mouth. He nearly lost his aplomb. He hail not bargained for a young lady pupil, but had rather led himself to believe that the handwriting on the scented paper was that of a middle-aged lady who had conceived a craze for the violin. She, too, was surprised on beholding a young clerical gentleman with a fiddle-case on.his knee. "I'm Miss Herbert," she said, simply, "and I want someone to play with me the works of the great masters whom I love. But—" and she paused for a moment. There was a doubt implied in the "but," and he divined it. "I also love the great masters.," he said, "and have striven for a long time to interpret them." The merest suspicion crept into her mind at this remark. A curate raving over Schubert and Mozart seemed an incredible thing. "Shall we begin at once?" she said, as if anxious to test the amateur maestro's skill. "I am at your service, Miss Herbert." And forthwith he produced his violin from its ease. She took hers from the bottom shelf of a cabinet, and both began to tune up. "What shall it be ?" she asked..</p>
        <p>"Anything you like," he replied. They looked at each other for a moment, and each divined the other's thoughts. She was certainly dubious of his skill, and he, suspecting dubiety, was anxious to undergo any test she might impose upon him. "Here is a piece by Chopin," she said, fishing out some music from a disorderly heap by the side of the music cabinet.</p>
        <p>"Chopin let it be," he said, quietly. They began and played the piece throughout. "Delightful! " she exclaimed, her eyes shining with enthusiasm. "I never had anyone to play with me like that before. You'll do. Oh, yes, you'll suit me." "It might have been done better," he answered. "Pardon me for saying so, but you were inclined to drag the time a little. Let us play it over again, or perhaps you would like to try something else ? " "No," she said, humbly, "we'll go through it ,again." Chopin was again interpreted, also various pieces by Rossini, Rubenstein and Mozart. For an hour they played. He could have played for another hour, but she had had enough for that day. Besides the enthusiasm which music always inspired in her she was clearly excited at the discovery she had made—a Church of England curate the best amateur violinist she had met with. Then they fell to tallting'about the eom- posers. He talked well ;he was out of himself. And she was deeply interested; she had not met anyone like him before. He was in the middle of a dissertation on Bach when the door flew open, end a young man bounded unceremoniously into the room. "I say, Sybil," he cried, then stopped short. "I beg pardon," he resumed, "I did not know you had a visitor." "It is cnly my new music teacher," she said. She regretted the words as soon as they were uttered. She glanced at the curate, who bit his lip. Then a flush overspread her face. "What, another of them?" "Now, don't be rude, Hal. Mr. Sisson is quite the best amateur violinist I have met. Come and be introduced properly. My cousin, Mr. Darley —Mr. Sisson." The young men looked at each other before bowing, and there was that in the glance of both which said as plain as words that they did not desire each other's acquaintance. Mr. Henry Darley disliked the idea of his cousin playing the violin with a good-looking young curate. As for the Rev. Septimus Sisson, he felt ashamed that circumstances should have compelled him to assume his present role. • There was no doubting the former's aspire.. lions in regard to Miss Herbert. Ile was one of</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-07-22_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday July 22, 1905</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday July 22, 1905</p>
        <p>! those young gentlemen who are in training for , the positions of husbands of heiresses. He had no profession. It had been decreed by his father and mother, who were not without some wealth, that he should marry his rich cousin. His mother, Miss Herbert's aunt, and the widowed Mrs. Devereux, another aunt, who managed the household in Dover-street, had determined to keep Sybil's fortune in the family. And so Mr. Henry Darley lived in the prosreet of having a good time by-and-bye, with a house in town, a tine place in the country, lots of shooting and hunting, and trips to Paris and the Riviera every , year. "Come, Sybil," he said, "let us have a gallop !in the Park. The horses are in tine condition, and it is just the day for a ride." . "Not to-day, Hal; to-morrow, certainly." ! He had been so much accustomed to assume mastery over the heiress, that an unexpected thwarting of his wishes piqued him. The blood rushed to his face; he let it be seen that he was ! annoyed. , "Oh, perhaps you want to do some sawing," he blurted out, and flung out of the room. , The enthusiasm engendered by the music left ' Miss Herbert's face. Her eyes no longer sparkled. "My cousin is angry," she said. "I am afraid I have been the innocent cause of his anger," said the curate, rising to put his violin in its ease. "Perhaps it would be better if he does not see me here again." "No, no," she protested. "You will come at the same time this day week. We have entered into a bargain, and you cannot think of with- drawing from it already. That would not be fair." Even a poor cure has his feelings, and need it surprise any of us that he felt himself unable to withstand so charming and eloquent a pleader? So when he picked up his fiddle-case to leave he had given her hir promise to return , the following week. , He turned up at the appointela time, and found his pupil distrait. "Hal is still angry," she said. "He has left the house because you were coming here.' "Really, I am very sorry, Miss Herbert. I can leave at once, and any little arrangement that we have entered into can be quashed." "Not for twenty Hals !" she cried, and her eyes, always luminous, flashed defiance. "He does not like music a bit," she continued, "and he hates the violin more than any instrument." Then she fell a-thinking for a few seconds. Visions of Mr. Henry Darley in a fit of temper, smashing her violin when she became his wife, troubled her. A contingency like this was fright- ful to contemplate, and she put the idea away from her. "What shall we play to-day, Mr. Sisson?" she asked. "I :have brought a few pieces by Gentled, which you may not have tried." They were new to her, and she was vastly pleased with them. Again enthusiasm shone in her face, and once or twice, when she made a little slip,her laugh,trilling in its music,rang out. When the practice was over they chatted for awhile, after the manner of old friends. His talk on other subjects besides music interested her. Quite unintentionally he let fall remarks about his church, his parish, and his mode of life which excited her sympathy; and when he left she found herself saying what a shame it was that so gifted and estimable a young gentleman should be thrust away in a London parish, where the only prospect before him was unremitting labour. The curate now looked forward to his weeltlY visit to Dover-street. Something more than the prospect of being able to pay his laundry bill with greater ease made him eager to go there. Sybil Herbert had completely enmeshed him. He Who had never before allowed himself to be fascinated by a young woman, for the reason that he was too poor ever to think of marriage, now succumbed to her beauty and winning ways. "I must not give way to the spell," he told him- self. "I must be myself again." And it seemed that he would have no difficult, in creeping back into his shell, for the next time he went to Dover-street Mrs. Devereux remained in the libeary all the time be and Sybil played.' She spoke little, but watched her niece's every movement. She had been sent to watch. There was no doubt about that. Henry Darley had told his aunt things, one of which was that the curate was not as harmless as his cloth implied, that his good looks and his skill on the violin had ! fascinated Sybil, and that she might be foetid enough to give him encouragement, and then farewell to her fortune. . . .</p>
        <p>The playing over, Mr. Sisson had to take hie leave, and Mrs. Devereux plainly intimated by her looks that she hoped she had seen the last of him. But he caught sight of Sybil's face, and fancied he saw in it pity, pride, and somethini, else. It was this indefinable something that bath 11 him be of good cheer. 1 With a guilty feeling that he had harboured I some unholy thought, he longed for tho day of the next lesson. When it came round he begat to play over some new pieces which he was sure I would please Sybil. Whilst in the middle of ar "Andante," he was interrupted by a teuf-tent sound; the noise grew louder and louder, and then ceased. He looked out of the window, and beheld a sight which nearly took his breatt away. It was a visitor in a motor-car, no othet than his Aunt Martha, whom he had not seer since he left college. He ran to the door and opened it. "Come in, my dear aunt. What an unexpected pleasure. I was beginning to think that you had all forgotten me." Aunt Martha, who was a very active lady of sixty, strong, vigorous, and well preserved. adjusted her pince-4e: more firmly on her nose, and looked sternly at her nephew. "You do not object to my buying that, Sep ?" the asked, pointing to the motor-car. "Why should I object to your buying that of anything else you might fancy, dear aunt." "Then I will conic in." And she sank into a chair whieh he placed for her, took a 1011 E, breath; and heaved sigh of infinite relief. "What a life I have had, Sep," she eon.' firmed. "I have visited all your brothers, and they have mortally offended me. Why, they had the audacity to tell me that at my time of life ii was madness tei buy a motor-car. I knew what they meant, ef course. They thought that I wal spending money which they should spend 80MI day. But I have changed that notion, Sep. have struck them out, boy—all of them. And why I came here was to see if you were of theit way of thinking. But you are not; and what) am asking myself is why I did not take to yet instead of them. But your father always spoke depreciatingly of you.' She would have talked on, but her nephei, interrupted her. "You will be tired, aunt; my landlady will gel you some tea ready in a minute. lam very pleased to see you, and you will stay till I come back. I can get your motor stabled; I will tell, your man to look after it now." His solicitude for her motor touched he] deeply. "Thank you, nephew; thank you. You are very kind. I will take a cup of tea with you, but I must go to the Coburg Hotel to-night." "I am going in that direction, aunt." "You going to the West End ? Whatever are you going there for ?" "I have a violin pupil in Dover-street." "A violin pupil ! Then you still fiddle ? If was the fiddle which annoyed your father so: much," she said, fixing her grey eyes upon him. "I know it, aunt; hut I must play, it is my only hobby." "Well, well, I have no objection to the fiddle. You can go with me, and we shall find room for your fiddle-box, too." Aant Martha was even more cheerful after het cup of tea, and during her drive to Dover-street,, she chatted in a way she had never done before. The motor-ear stopped at Miss Herberes door. "I'll wait while you give your lesson," she said. "Oh, my dear Runt, you will catch cold." , "Fiddlesticks!" He did not want to cross hey, and so entered the house. But he had only been a few moments gone when he reappeared with Miss Herbert. "Miss Herbert wants you to come in, aunt." "'Very kind of Miss Herbert, indeed," she said, adjusting her pince-nez, and looking at Sybil intently. "Is she your pupil? Well, who would have thought it. Yes, I'll come in." The old lady was comfortably accommodated with an armchair and an illustrated paper until the lesson should finish. But she did not read. Her sharp eyes were everywhere. She watched her nephew and Miss Herbert closely, and Was quick to note their bearing towards each other. Then she examined the rich furnishings of the room, and made mental notes. Suddenly Mrs. Devereux sailed in, followed by Henry Darley. They had seen the motor-car at the door, and were curious to know who Sybira otker visitor might be besides the curate.</p>
        <p>Both stood aghast on seeing the old lady, hel dust cloak lease, watching the violinists. "Sybil, dear, you should have told us that yea expected visitcrs," said Mrs, Devercux. . _ _</p>
        <p>„ Aunt Martha started up. "Introduce me, Sep.” "Oh, it is Mr. Sisson's friend, and not Sybil's. And Mrs. Devereux sought an explanation. "You do not appear to relish my presence here, madam. Your daughter will explain." "Dear aunt, this lady is Mr. Sisson's aunt." "Was this a conspiracy," thought Mrs. Devereux—"a scheme to trap Sybil ?" "Really, :Henry," she said, turning to her nephew, you should help your cousin to entertain unexpected visitors; it is your duty as her affianced husband."</p>
        <p>Sybil's face turned crimson. "You have no right to say that, aunt." Aunt Martha jumped 'up, drew herself up to her full height, and fixing her pince-nez firmly, said: "This is an unfortunate contretemps. lam invited here by this young lady while my nephew gives her a violin lesson, and you, madam, and this gentleman, make it clear by your looks that I am an intruder."</p>
        <p>The curate put his violin in its ease. Sybil looked at him, pity in her iye4. "I never approved Sybil's new teacher from the first," said Henry Darley. "And what have you against Sep, sir? Do you fear that he will become your rival ?" Henry Darley laughed, rather rudely as it Seemed to Aunt Martha.</p>
        <p>"Why should you laugh, sir? Is it not true?" s. Devereux frowned. She was sure a con- spiracy was afoot. "This is most unfortunate, aunt. lam sure I have done no wrong in asking Mrs. Sisson into Inv own house." "Your own house!" cried Aunt Martha. "Then you are mistress, and this lady is—" "My aunt And chaperon." "And you like my nephew to play with yon ? " Sybil glanced at the curate and was silent; but her silence spoke volumes to Aunt Martha. "Oh, I see how it is," concluded the old lady. "I do not see why we should continue the dis- cussion with this person," said Henry Harley, tartly. "Fie, Hal!" remonstrated Sybil.</p>
        <p>"Look here, young man, you turn up your nose iat my nephew, because you think he s only a curate, and 80 poor that he has to tench the violin. Disabuse your mind at once. He is heir to one hundred thousand pounds! Ido not wish to be either inquisitive or impertinent, but how much are you heir to? Precious little, I should fancy." Aunt Martha's speech fell like a thunderclap on all her auditors. And to the curate most of all. Sybil forgot herself sufficiently as to clap her hands; she could not help it; and Sep looked at his aunt dazedly. Mrs. Devereuz, chagrined beyond measure swept out of the room, and her nephew, sheepish and crestfallen, followed her. "I am sorry to have been the cause of this little commotion in your house," Miss Herbert. "But I will leave at once. You have been keep- ing an eye on the motor, Sep ? " "Pray do not mention it, Mrs. Sisson. It is one of those little things which happen un- expectedly." "Forgive my seeming presumption but I like you, and if you are agreeable I wane to improve an .acquaintance begun under such unfortunate auspices. I have only beard you play a little, and I want to hear you again; so you and Sep can come to the Coburg and dine with me to-night at seven sharp. I would not have asked you to come alone had you not made it clear that you are your own mistress." Of all the little dinner parties in the hotel that night there was none more happier than Mrs. Sisson's. Aunt Martha had not spent such a pleasant evening for a long time. As for her nephew, the knowledge that his aunt had made him her heir, and the reality of meeting Sybil on terms of perfect equality, nearly turned his head. Aunt Martha kept up a constant flow of con- versation, and then suddenly stopped. "That reminds me," she said, abruptly. "I must see that my motor has been properly housed," and forthwith left the room. Sybil was sitting on the sofa. The curate moved towards her. All his old humility had disappeared. "Miss Herbert," 1., oegan, "at the risk of poaching on another mae's preserves, I am going to ask you to let me be your friend, and---" He paused, and observing a happy smile suffuse her face, continued, "something more if I prove myself worthy." They looked into each other's eyes. He seized her hand, and it lay unresisting in his. At this interesting moment Aunt Martha reappeared. Her hand went up to her pince—nez. "Ah!" she said, "I see how the land lies. I approve," sh,e added, with a smile, "tlw more so as lam convinced that it is not my hundred thousand pounds, but love that has proved the conqueror.' [THE Elio.] _</p>
      </div>
