      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-07-22_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>Dangerous Footpath.</head>
        <p>A Dangerous Footpath.</p>
        <p>Mr. Brighouse held an inquest on. Saturday at the Horse and Jockey, Martinscroft, on James Kelly, an Irish farm labourer, who was found drowned in the river Mersey near Woolstort Wharf the previous Thursday. The evidence showed that on Sunday„the, Ptli inst, the deceased and another labourer, named Dominic Brannon, went for a walk along the riverside They called at an inn, where .they each had three pints of beer and one pint of ale and ginger beer. At another public house they each had a pint of beer. Some other labourers were in the house, and all the men commenced drinking together, several quarts of beer being consumed. The men walked home along the footpath alongside the river, and when near the bridge crossing the river Brannon missed the deceased. Brannon went on to his lodgings, and as the deceased did not appear he went back, in search of him, anti crossed over to Thelwall, but could find no trace of him, and he then gave information to the police. A diligent search was made, but the body was not found until Thursday evening.7-The jury returned a verdict of "Found drowned," and added that "in their opipion, the public footpath along which the deceased wak travelling is, in its present state, dangerous to life, and ought to be made safe by the authority having. , control."</p>
        <p>The attention of miners is again directed to the atmospheric changes in progress over our coalfields. Following the passage of rme rain depressions, the barometer is now rising decidedly in all directions from a very large anticyclone Which is lying out on the Atlantic. Drying northerly Winds are consequently becoming general, and it is therefore urged that every care should be taken to keep the dust sufliciently moistened, and to beware of escapes or accumulations of fire-damp.</p>
      </div>
