      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-07-22_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>MY DE.</head>
        <p>MY DE.</p>
        <p>'Now I want to talk to y6u this week about the special prize of a watch that I am offering. How many of you, I wonder, have watches. This is an opportunity of gaining ona with very little bother. There is, ,nothing to pay, only send us the names and addressee of your friends who wish to join the C'aildren's Corner. I want to get as many members as potsible. All you have to do is to use the forms which I sent you last week, and to the one who succeeds in getting the greatest number of members by August 31st I will give this beautiful watch; also 50 splendid books to be given to those who get the next highest number of names. I have received several very interesting letters this</p>
        <p>but that did not matter to her; she merely put her head down and walked straight ahead For- tunately, the canvas flaps gave way; for, if they had not, Clytie was prepared to carry away the whole tent.</p>
        <p>Even after she was free frorp the tent, she did not behave riotously, as if she did not know the difference between liberty and license; she walked soberly away from the tent and alcng the path across the common, until she came to the Main street of the town. She was very deliberate and very quiet, and did not pause once until she good before a little shop, which was as tightly closed up as shutters and blind-doors Could make it. '</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-07-22_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>(To be continued.)</head>
        <p>(To be continued.)</p>
        <p>CIRCUMVENTION. Once I knew a little maid Who declared she'd not be weighed. Though we tried full half-an-hour th persuade her; No eivirenties would avail: She would not go on the scale. -Shall I tell you how, in spite of her, we Nvig:tect her?</p>
        <p>Says Papa: •" At any rate, must ascertain my weight"; So, with Bessie in his arms (who never guesses What it is he's going to do) He steps on. We weigh the two; Then we take Papa's weight out, and that leaves Bessie's,</p>
        <p>Once I knew .a little lad, Who the funniest notion had ,ILat 'twould, somehow, hurt to have his pic- ture taken; And although we plead and plead, Still he only shook his head. Shall I tell you how his firm resolve was shaken?</p>
        <p>Says Mamma: "Just wait a bit. Here's old Rover; he will sit." So, while Jamie (never dreaming what the game is) Holds his paw, brimful of glee, "Just to keep him stiH, you see,"— Lo, in taking Rover's picture, we get Jamie's!</p>
