      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-07-22_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>Great Cycle Picnic.</head>
        <p>A Great Cycle Picnic.</p>
        <p>At this time of year the tension and strain under which cycle manufacturers work is occasionally temporarily relaxed for a day or so in an excursion to the sea for the recreation of hard-worked em- ployes. One of the most notable of these excur- sions has just taken place i the New Hudson Cycle Company conducting a monster trip of its em- ployes from Birmingham to Yarmouth. Upwards of a thousand cycle - trade workers sat down to dinner in the Drill Hall, under the presidency of. the chairman of the company, Mr. E. A. Wilson, and a more contented, prosperous-looking, well- conducted crowd of working people it would be difficult to find. . .</p>
        <p>In a short speech after the dinner, the chairman made some remarks which largely accounted for the general air of smiling happiness observable among the assembled company. The workers are being found continuous employment all the year round; ever since Christmas overtime has been worked, and, according to present indications, there is no likelihood of the employes having .to go on short time this side of Christmas. After an announcement like this no wonder the rest of the day was a great success. Trips to sea, motor-car rides, driving excursions, and boating parties filled up the time, which passed all too rapidly, till eight o'clock, when the party crowded into the three special, trains of Pullman and cor- ridor cars for the return journey. Some of the cars boasted of pianos, mandolines, and other instruments, with which the monotony of the journey back was lightened, the whole.plrty reach- ing Birmingham shortly after midnight.</p>
        <p>Should Cyclists be Numbered? Nothing seems more remote from practical poli- tics at the present time than any proposal to make riders of the modest push bicycle carry numbers and pay a registration fee. And yet the ancient bogey has lately been revived in a Birmingham journal, though with what object it is difficult to imagine. . Any lingering . hostility towards the cyclist on the part of the general public has of late years been drowned in the wave of indignation excited by the disgraceful behaviour on the highr' way of certain motorists. Police and public alike have had their attention diverted from the humbler travellers on light single track machines by the aggressions of the automobilist. In comparison with the swift and ponderous car, the cycle appears a safe and seemly mode of locomotion hardly less offensive than pedestrianism. And so it 'is. The outcry raised against cyclists before the advent of-the motor car was largely inspired by prejudice. Many who then declaimed against the pastime are riders themselves to - day. Indeed, wheelfolk form a body so numerous and law -abiding that, it woulcl be folly to propose the Infliction of a use- less hardship upon them.</p>
        <p>Cycling and Photography.</p>
        <p>are kindred bobbies which seem to be more in evidence than ever this season. If any readers of these notes possess re- cently taken nega- tives of cycling sub- jects suitable for illustration pur-</p>
        <p>poses, which fhey would like to sell, they may possibly find a market for them by addressing "The Clubman," do this paper.</p>
        <p>ji , Nfr6ezz i?„,„„ \‘‘ 7, • -45ULie,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-07-22_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>Bowden Improvement.</head>
        <p>A Bowden Improvement.</p>
        <p>Being persuaded that the brakeless cycle is full of real terrors, and being somewhat dis- satisfied with the brake fitted to his cycle some twenty days ago, the writer determined to have. fitted a Bowden back rim brake to his light - (- rimmed mount before starting for a holiday among the passes of the Highlands. He was for- tunate in securing a speci- men of the new pattern outside lever, just brought out by the Company. It is not an inverted lever, yet it fits snugly under the handle - grip, being very neat in appearance. An absolutely direct straight pull on the wire is obtained, so that it need not be bent. When fitted on the same side as the bell, it is possible for the rider to use bell and brake-lever simultaneously. After a full test of the brake on the curly descents suet with in Scotland, the writer has no hesitation in declaring the detail improvement to be a great success, The enormous confidence felt on a Bow- denized cycle when flying steep hills must be ex- perienced to be fully appreciated. The leather shoes work in perfect amity with the smooth, hard surface of a Fairbanks rim.</p>
        <p>wood. 4% ' ,t,, 11,4,11 k 0 LT. r"4- 1 TIIE BRAKELESS BICYCLE.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-07-22_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>Importance of Footwear,</head>
        <p>The Importance of Footwear,</p>
        <p>The ordinary footwear of every-day life is not, as a rule, comfortable for cycling, and probably ex- plains the tendency to debased pedalling we hear of. The stout, wide welt of a comfortable walking boot occupies a greater space than the standard pedal affords, while the thicker sole produces a feeling of awkwardness in pedalling. One may put up with the inconvenience of boots for a short dis- tance, bet the comfort and ease of ankling peculiar to the shoe makes a '"Teat difference when a pro- tracted ride is taken. A good cycling shoe should be a snug tit—neither tight enough to constrain the foot, nor loose enough to slip at the heel. In the shoe which laced up from the toecap it was thought that finality had been reached; but the pedes-cyclo type of strap fastening goes one better, being com- fortable, grit-proof, and sightly. As With every- thing else, in buying cycling shoes cheapness should be eschewed if lasting satisfaction be desired.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-07-22_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>Cycles on Easy Terms.</head>
        <p>Cycles on Easy Terms.</p>
        <p>The instalment system has been recently made highly attractive to cycle buyers by the new plan adopted by the New Hudson Cycle, Company, Limited, of Birmingham. Our local cycle agents who Sell the New Hudson machines, we understand, also sell them by this convenient instalment system whenever desired.</p>
