      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-07-22_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <p>I</p>
        <p>Faked Machines at Hill Climbs.</p>
        <p>A correspondent con:llain:4 in tile 'Athletic • News ' that machines usett by competitors in hill clinibs are specially constructed for the occasion, are incapable of being ridden downi a steep hill, and are unfit for general use. While the writer cannot speak for every hill climb, the results of his long and close observation of the machines used , in the Catford climbs are in direct opposition to the correspondent's contention. This may be elite to ,the rigid scrutiny, measurement, and ,weighing, to which the machines of Catfordian entrants are subjected before they tackle the hill. Anyhow, it is an incontestible fact that certain successful bicycles have been Used year after year in this con- test, and have been ridden thousands "of miles on . the road between centests. • None but 'a well built cycle has any chance of successfully scaling a hill like Westerham, in a," time test Whiah necessitates hurrying on the part of the Would-be victor. And year after year evidence has demonstrated that only light machines carry winners to the top. The consistent absence of long crank enthusiasts, from what is virtually the open hill climbing champion- ship of .the year, also speaks volumes. hpermatessaisor 41</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-07-22_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>HAYDOCK PIT DISASTER.</head>
        <p>HAYDOCK PIT DISASTER.</p>
        <p>Manager and Four Others Killed. GREAT FALL OF ROOF.</p>
        <p>The disttict of Haydock, near St. Helens, was on Sunday afternoon thrown into a state of great excite- ment by the terrible disaster in the New Boston Colliery, resulting in the loss of several lives and injuries to other men. The New Boston Colliery is owned by Messrs. Richards Evans, and Co., who are proprietors of over a dozen collieries in South-West Lancashire. The news of the accident spread with amazing rapidity, and it being Sunday afternoon enormous crowds soon gathered around the pit, and some pathetic scenes were witnessed. It seems that at five o'clock on Sunday morning a gang of men were lowered down the shaft to repair the loting of the four-foot seam of the mine where a day two ago there was a heavy fall, which had somewhat .;ked the working arrangements. was about two o'clock when, without the slightest ication or warning, a sudden crash was heard just )ut the spot where some eight men were at work. ops and bars cracked and smashed, and down came an folmous quantity—weighing many tons—of stones, trt and other debris. All the unfortunate men were ;ompletely buried. The other workers quickly realised ,he position of affairs, and, while signals were sent up the shaft for assistance, they at once proceeded to try and rescue the unfortunate victims. Henry Naylor was found just at the edge of the debris. He was quickly removed to the surface, and, though badly bruised, was able to walk to his home. Two hours elapsed before another body was reached, and this proved to be that of Henry cook, who was terribly cut about the head and badly crushed. He was, ho%%ever, alive, and was conveyed to the Cottage Hospital.</p>
        <p>LIST OF KILLED AND INJURED. About this time other bodies were observed, but in each case it was seen that life, was extinct, and, when all the eight men had been accounted for, it was found that five had lost their lives. The following ore the names of the killed : Sam Cook (34), manager of the colliery, of Church- road, Haydock, James took (36), brother of the last-named, of Vesta- lane, Haydock. Thomas Woollam (30), of Mil Mold-lane, Ifaydock. 1 Thomas Leyland (45), of Penny-lane, Haydock. Daniel Barnes, a young man, of Blackbrook-road, Hardock. The injured are:— Henry Naylor (61), Liverpool-road, Ashton-in-Mater- field. Wright Pimblett (21), Nelson-street, Earlestown. Henry Cook, brother of the two Cooks killed, Black- brook-road, Haydock. The deceased man Leyland leaves a widow and twelve young children.</p>
        <p>No wonder thet e are so many 'discontented servants.</p>
        <p>. • N o wonder there are so _ many haggard c -, ~,U • '- 1 ry ci ,6 rt..CC.. WiVeje , . •</p>
