      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-07-22_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, July 22, 1905.</p>
        <p>DrKiy or i or) reigrun ors 01 alga gutae, mid in Armourers Section there tire ;.;52 skilled artificers who rank as sergeants (hi both sections the pay is from ss. tid. to Bs. a day. The Royal Armv Medical Corm ;i4 a 'body of 3.000 nersous. wnom are Weirnmt onicv,n ;I. day), 125 sergeants Iyising to ss. a 311 corporals who are paid 3s. Id. a day. Ii each grade are clerks wilt; are paid at a bighe! ode—for instance, a corporal clerk gets'ds day. clerks receive increments for lengra service independently of their army rank (me other special corps remains to be mentioned : this is the Army Pay Corps, whose members. are supposed to possess a gift for military finance, and are carefully selected from soldiers serving in the comixdant f= :Inches. Promotion • and hi. (Tease of pay dermal entirely on length f service, and except that uniform is worn and foreign service Compulsory, employment in tile Army Pay Corps is similar. to work in a bank : there is no Indian service, but every seventh man is serving in Egypt or the colonies.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-07-22_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>CALL RIGHTS RESERTLPI</head>
        <p>CALL RIGHTS RESERTLPI</p>
        <p>TIIE ARMY AS A PROFESSION,</p>
        <p>MAJOR G. W. REDWAY.</p>
        <p>'lndications abound that the Army must 1;. made in the ilear future a real profession for : considerable number of those who enlist s. private soldiers. A new style of recruit will 1., ,required, since in every fighting unit of , hundred infantry there must be no fewer Qs! 13 soldier-experts of varions grades, from colon:- sergeant to squad commander. These leader: must be intelligent men, quick to learn awl competent to instruct others ; their individual intelligence will be continually exercised, the:. powers and prestige will be considerable, noe 'heir emoluments will correspond. We shall neeti men of the stamp who now fill our cricket, fool- all end athletic clubs ; we must take them from •he banker's counter, from the stockbrolmr', Ace, , and from the large drapery establish ;. we shall want man who have " served their time " as butchers and bakers, as saddler, mil wheelwrights, as blacksmiths and telegraph. jim rators.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-07-22_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>TEE ARMY RE-MODELLEt-.</head>
        <p>TEE ARMY RE-MODELLEt-.</p>
        <p>They will all be enlisted for three years, . just as if they took civil employment for that term, and at the end of the engagement ' they may return, if they like, to civil life will: ii rension of 64. a day for thirteen years as reserv- ists. This, however, is not what the War Office ' will desire in the case of any man who is '' wort!, his salt." Such a man will be tempted by pr. motion and extra pay to make another contract to complete eight years with the colours, and alter that further trial of his abilities, a re- engagement tei comp'.ete 21 years far pensiet will be arranged. At the age...A 20 such a nom as we have in view, one who hits entered the Army to make it a profession, will be on the roll of sergeants, receiving at least 235. 4d. a week, having his own private quarters in bar- racks, his bread and meat ration, his clothe'. his coals, his gas, his cook and his doctor gratis A batman will net as his valet, and accommodit- ' lion for his wife will be found, if Le chooses to rearry ; but married or single, as a member of the Sergeants' Mess, he will enjoy the privileges of a first-rate club for a subscription of. is. mm month. 'What pleasanter profession can be imagined for any voting man whose financial rircumstances eompel him to start life as an etnploye ? How many city clerks, how many , shop assistants, 'how many skilled artisans be- tween the age of 20 and 30 can depend on re.- reiving 23 shillings a week as a clear surplus of Meanie over expenditure, to save or spend in luxuries ? ln such easy circnmstlinces the N.U.O.:s of the British Amity will in future live, while enjoying foreign travel, outdoor em- ployment, abundant leisure, and opportunity for amusement, sport or self-improvement.'</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-07-22_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>TIIE CIIOICL OF A ICLCDIENT.</head>
        <p>TIIE CIIOICL OF A ICLCDIENT.</p>
        <p>The future career of many a youngster who possesses health, strength, and brains, but lacks the capital wherewith to become an,J employer of labour, may depend on his losing no time in applying to the War Office for per- mission to try and win one or other of some 30,000 appointments that are at the present , moment easily within reach. In a regiment of • Household Cavalry—a very select corps in every j sense of the word—there are only 408 enlisted men and .of these nearly 25, per cent. are above the degree of private soldier. In the " Illues j there are 02 appointmenta. to be filled by pro- motion from the ranks. the prizes ranging from commissions as riding-master or quarter- master (who each receive from les. Cd. to las. Cd. a day) down to the appointment of shoeinrs- smith at 3s. 3d. a day. I:, the cavalry of the Line the percentage of N.C.O.'s to privates is much lower than in the Ilos.sehold Cavalry, but yet out of every six men enlisted one at least is destined. to Wear the chevrons. Cavalry warrant officers receive daily (is. 4d. or Os. 6d, sergeauts from ss. 4d, to 3s. Bd. Allied io the Cavalry are 28 service batteries of lloyal Horse 'Artillery. where the privates are called gunners or drivers. To each group of 112 men there are :11 leader and experts who are paid from 3s. to ss. 4d. it day.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-07-22_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>Tilt SCIENTIFIC COPTS.</head>
        <p>Tilt SCIENTIFIC COPTS.</p>
        <p>Of Field and Mountain Artillery, 164 bat- teries are kept up, and each battery absorbs nine sergeants, five corporals, five bombardiers, and six artificers, whose pay ranges from 3s. to to ss. 2d. a day. The depOts and riding estab- lishment end the mounted police require a still higher proportion of warrant officers and N.C.O.'s. Men who are fond of the seaside and who are not less than 5i feet in‘height may be recommended to join the garrison gunners who pervade Southsea and the Isle of Wight, and occupy various pleasant resorts between Dover and Weymouth. This branch of the servicc provides a livelihood for coins 1,400 sergeants whose pay is from ss. 6d. to 4s. 21. per day. awl who are graded as master-gunners. military staff-clerks, instructors in gunnery, quartc.i mater sergeants, pay sergeants, orderly-room clerks, colonels 7 clerks, sergeant-cooks and sergeant-tailors. From the sergeants are 'selected about 100 sergeant-majors, bandmasters. super- intending clerks, and master-gunners who raids as warrant officers, mid are paid either Os. Gd ot 75. per day. The Royal Artillery and the Royal Engineers are both known as "scientific " corps. but the latter is perhaps the most interesting of the two, with its numerous departments, sections and groups of experts. There is the lnidging battalion, the balloon deptt, the tffiegraph battalion, the fortress units, the submarine- mining units (employing 214 electricians for coast defence), railway companies, and the companies which are eternally engaged on the Ordnance Survey. Then there is a. staff, cola. prising 28 corporals, 19 sergeants, and 4 warrant officers, engaged with telegraphs and telephones. , Fifty-nine sergeants and 8 warrant officers are instructors or clerks at the School of Military Engineering, ancl appointments as military fore- men-of-works and engineer clerks (451) include positions for 70 warrant officers. The military nechiudes (194) are all sergeants or warrant officers. The War Office, the Record Office, the garrison schools aucl military colleges employ 18 warrant officers and 38 sergeants. The pay of a ]loyal Engineer ranges from 7s. in the ease of a warrant officer to 4s. 3d. for junior sergeants, and any expert farrier or carriage-smith Ntay clear ss. 3d. or 4s. 7d. a day by wearing the uniform of the old sappers and miners.</p>
        <p>PROMOTION IN A " MARCIIING " REGIMENT.</p>
        <p>Let us now see what career is open to the ordinm7 foot soldier. The Brigade of Guards, like the Household Cavalry, is always stationed at home in time of neace, but the " line " infantry soldier must expect to spend a con- siderable portion of his service in India or the colonies. In a guards regiment there are 5 warrant officers, 131 sergeants, 51 drummers and fifers, and 132 corporals. The sergeants get from 3s. 6d. to 58. a day. In the so-called marching regiments, promotion runs from ser- geant, 3s. 41. a day, to colour-sergeant 4s, day, sergeant instructor of musketry 4s. 3d. a, day, quartermaster-sergeant ss. a day, to ser- geant-major, who as a warrant officer is paid fis, a day, besides certain allowances on a special scale. It is also the custom to reserve the ap- pointment of quarter-master, with a commission and at least 9s. a day, for some senior sergeant who educationally and socially is not unfitted for the officers' mess. The most lucrative posts in the Army, however, are not gained by those who seek " the bubble reputation even at the (111111011'S mouth," but by the business-like individuals who join either the Army Ordnance Corps, the Royal Army Medical Corps, or the Army Service Corps. In these units even the privates draw a special rate of pay over and above that issued to corresponding ranks in the combatant branches of the Armv. For example, an Army Service Corps driver will be paid 3s. 20. a day, almost as much as an infantry sergeant. The Army Service Corps is exempt from service in India and supplies only 400 or 500 men to the colonies. It consists of about 6,500 persons Stgag€,d in supply and transport duties. 01 these one-third at least are N.C.O.'s or warrant cAizera„ and there are nearly 100 sergeants receiv- ing fs,rdm 4s. 7d. to Gs. 3d. a day. Appointment, as warrant officers are • given (250), and there are over 500 billets for farriers, carriage smiths, wheelers and collar makers, who are graded as corporals and paid 4s. a day. The Army Ordnance Corps has the care of all munitions of war and the clothing and equipment of troops. It is worked mainly by 90 warrant officers, called conductors or sub-conductors, who re- ceive 6s. 3d. to is. a day, and by 107 sergeants who are paid 4s. 7d. to 6s. 34. a day. The Machinery_ Artificers Oestioli cOluists of a *Aro,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-07-22_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>PLENTY OF EXTRA FAY.</head>
        <p>PLENTY OF EXTRA FAY.</p>
        <p>All the rates of pay in every. bran&amp; of the service are issued. daily for 365 days in the year except when in 'hospital, in prison, or absent from duty without leave. It is, of course, anderstood that this 'military income is free from. all stoppages except for such food as may be required in addition to the free issue of bread and meat. , A small outlay for washing and the upkeep of kit is neceSsary, but on the other hand there are numerous windfalls of which the industrious soldier can hardly avoid reaping the benefit. To begin with, a certain quantity of clothing is granted • yearly whether wanted or not, and the careful soldier may easily dispense with half of it and draw its value in cash. Additional pay is granted for service with mounted in- fantry, it special allowance is given for service in India, money prizes may be gained by crack swordsmea and lancers, by skilled signallers, by good shots and expert gunlayers. Some of these prizes take the shape of a itivepound note. Every kind of employment outside the beaten track of soldiering is termed " extra duty" and curries special. pay. Extra duty pay is issued to the following among others':—Rough riders. compAny-accout I tan tq, provost-sergeants, corn- 'pounders, telegraph and telephone clerks, auctioneers, shorthand writers, librarians, and cooks. Whenever performing the duties of superior rank, extra duty pay is issued to the soldier wins is acting for', his senior. Employ- ment on police ditties carries extra pay, and so does duty on practice ranges. Guniayers and range-takers- in the artillery receive extra pay, so does an acting schoolmaster in the infantry, and the caretaker of mobilization stores. Tile acting garrison gunner, the assistant instructor of signalling, and the chief wardmaster in it military hospital for lunatics all claim extra pay.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-07-22_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>PAY PENSIONS AND PREMIUMS.</head>
        <p>PAY PENSIONS AND PREMIUMS.</p>
        <p>The sergeant in charge of a telegraph office in the field may receive " charge ". pay. Space 'prevents our detailing how the pro- fessional soldier can earn additional pay, work- ing pay, engineer pay and establishment pay. but we should not omit to mention that 18 years' service with an irreproachable character involves the presentation of a silver medal and £5, while the " diAinguished conduct " medal carries either a 'cash reward of £2O, a life annuity, or an extra pension. This medal mar be earned by long, valuable, and meritorious service, as well as by valour in the field. On active service, of course, all pay is issued clear of every stoppage or drawback, . warrant officers then draw field allowance, and the families of all =Med soldiers are provided for by a special grant of • " separation " allowance. After a service of thrice seven years, and while yet on the sunny side of 40, the veteran soldier may claim his discharge to pension. Of courSe the pension is for life and varies from 38. 6d. a day in the case of a warrant officer, to 2s. 9d. in the case of a senior sergeant. The pension of a junior sergeant is 2s. 3d.' or 2s. 6d. a day, and the generic term sergeant includes 40 species of professional soldiers, from the .collarmaker quartermaster-sergeant, to the cor- poral-of7horse. Retirement is not compulsory even after 21 years in the Army, and many pre- fer to serve on in various capacities at home stations, chiefly as instructors to volunteers, militia mid yeomanry, billets which enable them to take their growing families out of Barracks, and generally to live the life of civilians while drawing a higher rate of pay and money 'allow- ances in lien of lodging, gas, coals, medicine and clothing. Such extra service will result later on in an extra pension. ,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-07-22_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>AN 110NOULABIA SSION.</head>
        <p>AN 110NOULABIA SSION.</p>
        <p>To the (lass from which the officers of the Army mainly come, military service in peace time means little more to the majority than membership of a social and sporting clnb a club which is sub- sidized by the NN'ar 'Office in return for nominal service rendered by its members. To the class which yields. the rank and file of the Army, colour service is a species of tertiary education, a three-years' or eight-years' course for the athletic" working man, to which he looks back in after life as a pleasant episode in his career. But to some thirty thousand members of the lower iniddle-class, young men who are trained clerks, commercial assistants, or skilled me- chanics, the class to Nvllich petty administration and the minor arts ;ilia crafts come as' a second nature, the Army may-be Confidently recom- mended as a means of livelihood, which if not altogether as theciihed by Mr. Brodrick—who said recently that the Army was a " high and noble " mis well as a " successful and respect- sble " career—is, at all events, a profession which compares favoolably with the art of k•andiug the4sugar. the mystery of serving writs, or such inglorious occuptions as that which is denoted by the barber's pole. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-07-22_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>WARRINGTON CO-OPERATIVE SOCIETY.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON CO-OPERATIVE SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>Half-yearly Meeting. The half-yearly meeting. of the members of the Warrington Co-operative society was held inthe Co- operative Hall, Sankey-street, on Monday evening, Mr.M. Jeffrey presiding over a large attendance.</p>
        <p>TriE LATE -MR., BIRCHALL. The CHAIRMAN said that at their meeting three Months ago they deplored the absence of their esteemed president and looked for ard with great hope to his being with them once again. They bad certain things in hand which they. desired Mr. Biretta!' should see carried to a successful issue. It had however been willed otherwise and while they deplored his -death they knew their loss would be his gain. They would not that night move a formal vote of condolence as it had heed done In their name by the committee. Continuing Mr. Jeffrey said they had had another successful quarter ' and everything wages prosperous as they could wish. At a meeting of the committee it was resolved that an enlarged portrait of the late Mr. Birchall be ob- tained and placed in the committee room. The minutes were approved, as was also the balance sheet which was very satisfactoiy.</p>
        <p>ELECTION OF PRESIDENT. Mr. S. DUNBOBBIN moved the election of Mr. Jeffrey as president until the ansual meeting of the society. Mr. G. WALLINGTON Seconded. He had known Mr. Jeffrey for many years and he did not think they could do better than elect him. The motion was unanimously en tried. Mr. HALEY asked whether there was a vice-president of the society. The SECRETARY explained that Mr. Jeffrey had been virtually the vice-president, although he had not actually been elected to that office. ' Mr. HALEY suggested that at the annual meeting the creation of an office of vice-president be considered. Mr. JEFFREY returned thanks for his election. He did not hope to ell the office so ably as Mr. Birchall had done. He would however do his best and no man could do more. ELECTION OF COMMITTEE MEN. The next businefts was the election of three committee men, Messrs. B. Farrar E. Malone and T. Critchley retiring. Messrs. W. daigh, J. Owen, E. Malone, B. Farrar, T. Critchley and Lowe were nominated, and upon a vote being taken the retiring members were re-elected. THE TESTIMONIAL TO THE SECRETARY. Mr. Ilainn asked whether anything had been done with regard to the presentation to Mr. Rathbone. Mr. JEFFREY said that they had lately been passing through a critical time. They bad, however, got the matter well in hand, and the Committee in a short time would be able to call a meeting with regard to making , he presentation.</p>
