      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-07-22_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>FATAL BRAWL AT WIDNES.</head>
        <p>FATAL BRAWL AT WIDNES.</p>
        <p>Police Court Proceedings.</p>
        <p>PRISONER COMMITTED FOR TRIAL.</p>
        <p>At the Widnes Police Court, on Monday, before the Mayor (in the chair), and Major Waring, William Marriott, a married man residing in Appleton-street, was charged with causing the death of his brother-in- law William Hewitt, aged fifty-three years, by striking him on the head with a yard brush on Saturday, July 8. Hewitt died on the following Monday. at 1.35, at the Accident Hospital, and at the inquest held on Wednes- day last week the jury, after a lengthy deliberation, re- turned an open verdict,there being insufficient evidence to show how deceased came about his death. _ . .</p>
        <p>Mr. Knowles defended. Superintendent Strickland said the Marriotts and the liewitts lived together at 5' Appleton-street. On the Bth July prisoner and the deceased went to the Widnes sports and remained together until four o'clock when Marriott went home, and feeling tired went to bed. Two hours afterwards Hewitt came home under the influence of drink, and commenced quarrelling with Mrs. Marriott about allowing her son to sell oranges at the sports. The bother progressed, and the parties almost got to blows. Marriott by this time awoke, and hearing deceased threaten his wife came downstairs and asked Hewitt, " What game are you up to ?" and invited him into the yard. Both adjourned to the back premises, and to use Marriott's own words he picked up a yard brush with the intention of knocking a stick out or Hewitt's hand, and instead of hitting the stick struck him on the head. Deceased lay there for some time, and was placed on the sofa, and afterwards taken to bed. From 6.30 on the Saturday until three o'clock the following morning no medical assistance had been summoned, until Police-constable Davidson sent for Dr. Hutchinson, who ordered hi removal to the hospital, where he died. Evidence was given in support of this statement. Marriott, in his defence, said he remembered well the occurrence. " I struck at the stick in Hewitt's hand, with the intention of knocking it away, but accidentally hit him on the head." He had no idea of doing him any injury. The bench co mmitted the prisoner to the next Liver- pool Assizes.</p>
        <p>Comm&amp;term, FAILURES.—According to " Kemp's ' Mercantile Gazette," the total number of Commercial Failures recorded in England and \Vales during the week ending Friday, July 14th, was 157, viz. : —New bankruptcy proceedings published in the London Gazette, 86; deeds of arrangement registered, 71. The respective numbers in the corresponding week of last year were: Bankruptcies, 82; deeds of arrangement 87—total 161; being a decrease of 12. The total number of commercial failures recorded during the 23 weeks of the present year is 4,884 ; the total number recorded in the corresponding 28 weeks of last year was 4,319 showing an increase of 35.</p>
      </div>
