      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-07-22_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>LETTERS TO THE EDITOR</head>
        <p>LETTERS TO THE EDITOR</p>
        <p>NOTICE TO CORRESPONDENTS. Authors of letters intended for publication must in all cases send their names and addresses to the Editor as a guarantee of good faith.</p>
        <p>A Swimming Instructor Wanted. Sir.-1 think the suggestion as to an instructor in swimming at the Baths in your "Notes and Notions"last week worth consideration. Surely it could be arranged without much trouble and with very little expense, that on certain days of the week and at certain hours, the baths could be placed at the disposal of those who , wished to learn the art of swimming and that the Instructor should be there to help them. An extra fee could be charged those who wished to avail themselves of these privileges and lam sure it would not only be profitable in itself but it would induce those to come -regularly to the Baths who refrain from so doing at present, owing to the fact that they cannot swim and do not like to have to learn where there are so many others who can already swim.—Yours</p>
        <p>A.W.P.</p>
        <p>"Raid the Shops." Sir,—Mr. Gavan-Duffy marred the 'unemployed , meeting held at Bridge Foot on Saturday evening last by a statement, which, at Urst, we were not inclined to take seriously, but which he repeated deliberately and with all seriousness, to the effect that if the unemployed could not get any satisfaction from the Government with reference to the " unemployed workmen Bill " they were to approach the local authorities and if they failed to get any assistance there they were to " raid the shops." That remark, coming from a person who professes to be a socialist and addressed to a body of workmen, most of whom were in hearty sympathy with the objects of the meeting, will provoke a reaction which may have serious effects upon the progress of the movement. The very suggestion of violence, I contend,is contrary to the tenets of socialism. It is false to suggest that a social transformltion can be possible by resorting to indiriduat violence. Socialists do not fight individuals but classes, and the man who is so niggardly as to attempt to inculcate such erroneous views is a traitor to his party. If he has not yet conquered his own inclination for such despicable methods he would be doing himself and the movement no harm by keeping his month closed. Let him read the first book of the "Socialist Library," recently published. I would refer him to page 131 of that book, particularly to the passage: , —" Our sincere wish is that the social revolution, when its evolution is ripe, will be effected peacefully, as so many other revolutions have been carried out without bloodshed." There are means, surely, by which the unemployed can be helped without resorting to robbery and violence. Mr. Gavan Duffy knows full-well the stamp of men who would be delighted witn such a proposal. Are they the men he desires to help? Honest men, be they ever so poor and hard-up, would scorn to take advantage of such unmanly advice. Mr. Gavan Duffy should have hesitated before shouting "raid the shops "—a phrase pregnant with such disastrous andtriminal meaning. I have yet to learn that "brute force" is part of the pro- gramme of the Socialists,—Yours truly. NUNQUALM.</p>
        <p>" Don't Count Your Chickens Before They are Hatched." Sir,—l would suggest to our Tory Friends, that it is not wise on their part to start defacing their opponents Bills. This may lead to reprisals which I should very much deplore. It is my desire (and everyone who knows Mr. Crosfield, will know that it is also his desire) that this contest should be fought out in a thoroughly fair and honourable manner, and that nothing should be done to cause unnecessary ill-feeling on either side. Having said this much I should also like to say, that under ordinary circumstances, I should quite appreciate the advice given by our opponents, not to count our chickens before they are hatched, but I think the Tories will admit that there has been such a glorious "hatching "going on recently in various parts of the country, and that as the" chickens" continually turn out to be Liberals, there is some cause for jubilation in our ranks. At all the Bye-Elections we either win the seat o substantially reduce the Tory majority. In no part or the country are the Tories able to hold their own, and therefore, when we see the whole country protesting against the horrors of our present political situation I should not like to insult the Warrington electors by supposing that they alone would remain defenders of such a Government as the one at present existing. It must not be forgotten that the country is now pro- testing against the dishonourable tactics of the present Prime Minister and his followers, in the endeavour to degrade and insult the House of Commons, to strangle legitimate debate, and force retrograde legislation -upon the country by the use of the gag. The country deplores the wanton sacrifice of our brave soldiers' lives in South Africa, the waste of the tax-payers' millions, the increased cost of food brought about by the unjust system of taxation imposed by the Govern- ment on the poorest sections of the community, and the trifling of Mr. Balfour with Mr. Chamberlain's agitation for a return to Protection with all its consequent horrors of dear food and reduced wages. This, together with the scandals arising from the introduction and continuation of Chinese Labour in the South African compounds, and the recent savage and brutal floggings of the workers by their masters now admitted to be the case bya member of the present Government, and the endeavour of the Government and their Law Officers to screen culprits in high places as in the case of the late Whittaker Wright, and the war officials connected with the present contracts scandals. These and other offences too numerous to mention, the country is now protesting against, and my knowledge of the Warrington people will still induce me, whenever there is a protest made at any bye-election to emphatic- cally declare "WARRDZOTON TO FOLLOW." Yours truly, J. HOWES.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-07-22_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>LATEST NEWS.</head>
        <p>LATEST NEWS.</p>
        <p>DEFEAT OF THE GOVERNMENT. BEATEN ON AN IRISH AMENDMENT.</p>
        <p>A Minority of 3!</p>
        <p>Remarkable Speech by Mr. Balfour. The Government were defeated in the House of Commons early this (Friday) morning. The House had been engaged during the evening in a discussion on the Irish land question, and Mr. Redmond moved a reduction of the vote for the expenses of the Land Commissioners. On a division there voted: For the Reduction Against l9ll</p>
        <p>I,Tajority for Reduction There was a prolonged scene of excitement when the figures were announced, members on the Liberal side standing and shouting exultantly. On the motion for adjournment, Sir H. Campbell- Bannerman asked whether in view of the defeat of the Government Mr. Balfour had any statement to make. Mr. Balfour said it was impossible for him to make any statement at all at present. Mr. Redmond said the Prime Minister had repeatedly declared that he would resign, if defeated in the House; and, if he disregarded this voto of want of con- lidence, he would be taking a course opposed to all pre- cedent. Was he going to swallow this humiliation as he had swallowed every one for the last few years. If he did cling to office, the duty would be thrown on the Opposition of using every means of preventing such an outrage. MR. BALFOUR'S INTENTIONS.</p>
        <p>Mr. Balfour (speaking very coolly) said Mr. Redmond had greatly agitated himself. The matter could be settled in due time. It was a mere accident (at which remark there was a great ironical shout). He was not conscious of any humiliations. (Loud uproar). Until this evening the Government had re- ceived the unfeigned support of his party, which con- stituted a large majority in the House. Unless it was possible for themto carry on the business of the country, with dignity, they would not do so • They had been de- feated, as many previous Governments had been defeated on a vote on the estimates, and they must consider weather they should again submit the vote. By a happy accident there would be an ample oppor tunity of proving in a few hours whether the Govern ment enjoyed the confidence of the majority of the House. He felt under no obligation to give any decision on the present occasion, as he required to consult his colleagues before giving it. To-morrow being a morn- ing sitting, he could not make the statement, but he would make it on Monday. Mr. Redmond asked whether it would be in order to move that the House adjourn until Monday, as to do business to-morrow would be a farce under the circum- stances. The Speaker ruled the motion could not be made. Mr. Asquith appealed to the Prime Minister to adjourn until Monday. Mr. Balfour said that was impracticable, because the Government had to consider whether they would ask the House to reverse its decision. The business to-morrow would be the Scotch Churches Bill, which was quite uncontroversial. He would make a statement as to the Government's intentions on Monday.</p>
        <p>The position of the Government will be discussed at meetings which are to be held next week under the auspices of the Warrington Liberal Association. Ti Ist meeting will be held on Wednesday night,in Hardy- street, St. John's Ward, and the second at Bridge foot near Arpley Station. Both meetings will be addressed by Mr. Neilson, Liberal and Labour candidate for the Newport division of Shropshire, and Mr. J. Howes.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-07-22_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS.</head>
        <p>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS.</p>
        <p>Cricket.</p>
        <p>IHard lines indeed ! Warrington thought of achieving a victory at Stanley's expense on Saturday, but " there's many slip,"—and after an exciting finish Stanley were ; triumphant by the small margin of live runs. The match was a League fixture, which makes Warrington's defeat,the harder to accept, for they were badly in need of a coupe of points. It will require every effort on ; Warrinfton's part if they are to emerge from the League Icompetition with honours.</p>
        <p>Stanley went to the wicket first and compiled 127 runs in a short spice of time, the chief contributors being Molyneux (30), Moore (27), and Hakepeace (2). Mason's bowling proved responsible for the dismissal of the whole of the home team, his analysis working out at 30 wickets for 51 runs.</p>
        <p>Warrington started very unpromisingly, in fact the Stanley men were filled with hopes of an easy victory. The tail, however, dispelled this idea and steadily added to the score until dye runs only separated the teams when the last wicket fell. J. W. Bibby batted in splendid style and carried his bat through to the eighth wicket for 51 He hit out with his usual vigour and made ten fours.</p>
        <p>The match was very keenly contested throughout, and each eleven is deserving of credit. The fielding of the Stanley men was somewhat lax towards the close and this, says a Liverpool writer, "was undoubtedly a gift of several runs to the Warringtonians at a critical period." •</p>
        <p>'before them and won the championship with compara- tive eafie. Thisseason their record is the reverse and at pres Ont they are second on the list of candidates for the wooden spoon—not that there is much fear of their falling so low—and their avoidance of that position is as much the result of Penketh Parks poor play, as owing to their own endeavours. On Saturday they figured on the Balls Head green when it was hoped to obtain revenge for the defeat sustained earlier in the season. They did not succeed. On the contrary they were badly beaten by over 60 points.</p>
        <p>Cemetery started with a lead of two points gained by G. Holbrook at the expense of J. Swanton. Their success was however only short lived J. W. Worsley reversing the balance of points. J. E. Harrison at the 6th end was ten points in arrear to J. Bird. A run of eight equalized matters but Bird triumphed. The succeeding games, with one exception went to the home team who never once looked like taking a second place. As was expected the Horse and Jockey players on their own green proved too strong for Fearnhead and won by 35 points only four of the games being won by visiting players.</p>
        <p>The batting averages this week work out as follows : I Times Most I not in Inns. out, inns, runs. average E. A. Connor Mason J. W., Bibby A. Thewlis P. B. Hawtin W. H. Podmore J. Thompson W. Richardson l2 ... 0 ... 41 ... 163 ... 13.50</p>
        <p>The position of the clubs up-to-date is as follows:— P. Nva L. D. For. Agst. Pts. Balls Head 7 . 6 1 0 ... 734 ...1087 ... 12 Sankey 7 . 5 2 ... 0 ...1132 ... 893 ... 10 Fearnbead 8 . 5 3 ... 0 ...1117 ...1161 ... 10 Horse &amp;Jockey 7 ... 3 ... 4 ... 0 ... 951 ...1018 ... 6 Cemetery 7 . 2 5 ... 0 ... 940 —1053 ... 4 Penketh 6 . 0 6 . 0 ... 734 ...1007 0</p>
        <p>The individual merit table continues to be interest- ing. Three players only remain unbeaten and between the three there is not a great deal to choose. Appended is the list of those trundlers who have won live or more games :</p>
        <p>PM. P. W. L. For. Ag,st. A. Shaw, Bull's Head 7 7 0 105 ... 45 IL Jackson, Horse and J 7 ... 7 ... 0 ... 105 ... 76 F. Berry. Bull's Head 6 6 . 0 .. 90 ... 34 Joseph Weston,Fearnhead 8 ... 7 ... 1 ... 109 ... 82 J. W. Jolley,lßull's Elead 7 ... 6 1 ... 100 ... 61 T. Ellison, Cemetery 7 6 1 . 103 ... 65 W. Ellison, Sankey 7 6 1 ... 98 ... 63 G. Rutter, Sankey 7 6 1 .. 98 ... 66 J.Goulden, Sankey 7 6 1 . 91 ... 78 A. Gleave, Bull's Head 6 ... 5 1 ... 85 ... 46 H. Houghton, Sankey J. Swanton,Bull's Head 7 ... 5 2 ... 101 ... 76</p>
        <p>To-day Warrington meet Earlestown in a League J. Roberts,Fearnhead 7 ... 5 ... 2 ... 102 ... 87 match at Arpley and Mason takes a well-earned J. Jackson, Sankey 7 ... 5 ... 2 ... 93 ... 67 benefit. Daring his first season with Warrington the H. Clare' Boll' Head 7 . 5 . 2 ... 93 ... 76 professional has rendered yeoman service, and has G. Ball, Bull's Head proved his undoubted ability with both the bat and the, J.ll7.llTorslev,ll:lll's Head 7 ... 5 ... 2 ... 91 ... 78 ball. I hope Warringtonians will turn up in force to- J. Bird, Ball's Head i day and give him a" bumper." --- ---- 1 Two matches are down for decision to-day and in Warrington have fallen low in the League table and each the home team should win. They are, Horse and it is now almost hopeless to expect them to carry off Jockey v. Penketh, and Cemetery v. Fearnhead. the premier honours or even to be placed second. In the second division the local team has an excellent Rylands No 2 and Fletchers—teams which figure in chance of claiming the league. The position of the the works competition are evidently well matched. On clubs up-to-date is as follows : the Bth inst they met and Rylands triumphed by 4 FIRST DIVISION. I points. Owing however to a technical breach of the P. W. L. D. P. 1 rules by the winners the match had to here-played and Stanley Newton 7 .. 4 ... 2 ... 1 ... 9 viewedthe match with confidence and their assurance was Earlestown St. Helens 6 ... 3 ... 2 ... 1 ... 7 Instead they proceeded to make amends, and Wells Warrington St. Helens Rees 8 ... 2 ... 4 ... 2 ... 6 W. Cottam and F. Hessom peeled at 9 after Heesom had Prescot SECOND DIVISION. Rylands led by three points. A. Bailey and Broadhurst P. W. L. D. Pts. equalized matters and the totals stood 63-62, when J. Prescot Warrington Earlestown St. Helens Recs... Newton</p>
        <p>Stanley St. Helens 6 1 4 1 ... 3</p>
        <p>They were not long in that position however and the next game again saw Rylands rejoicing though their joy was short lived as they immediately fell behind—a position from which they were unable to extricate them- selves.</p>
        <p>It is freely rumoured that Mason contemplates leav- ing Warrington at the end of the present season, being it is stated, dissatisfied with the financial arrangements. If will be a pity if satisfactory arrangements cannot be Longford as events proved, were not faced with a made to retain his services as he is the most popular difficult task in the third round of the competition in their match with the Corporation team. At the outset professional Warrington haverhad and is classed amongst the finest of" coaches." they obtained a lead of 14 points, and gradually improv- ing their position, they ran out winners by 149-124. ----</p>
        <p>Warrington second are making a bold bid for the --- championship of the second division and on Saturday Fletcher's are to-day anticipating a victory at the they easily accounted for the Stanley second contingent expense of the L. and N.W. team, but I am inclined to Batting first the visitors fared badly against the deliveries imagine they will be rudely shocked by the Railway of Bannett who is rapidly coming to the front as a bowler. men. Pearson and Knowles (No. 1) should have a fairly They were all dismissed for 86, and Warrington respon- comfortable task in their match with Rylands (No. 1). ded with 146, R. Pierpoint giving the best bitting dis- The draw for the semi-final of the competition will be play with 32 to his credit. This is the second time held on Monday. during the present season that Warrington second have been victorious over Stanley. I</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-07-22_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>Cycling.</head>
        <p>Cycling.</p>
        <p>Sankey Wire Mills still maintain their unbeaten A Leigh cyclist has been drowned in Pickmere Mere, record, but on Saturday they were very fortunate in near Budworth, through bathing immediately after a averting defeat. Hough Green proved doughty oppon- heavy meal. It is the height of folly to do this at any ents' and ran up the substantial total of 137. The Wire time, but when vitality has been lowered by a long Mills had scored GC with 9 wickets down when the time cycle ride in the heat the danger is intensified. Pick- limit was reached—a narrow escape ! Cole batted mere has for some time possessed no attractions fog me. splendidly for the vistors, his innings realizing 76. A few years ago I arrived there just as a Manchester Hawthorn (18) was the top scorer for the Wire Mills. 1 youth was drowning in the same way as the Leigh , victim, and the place has seemed gloomy ever since.</p>
        <p>The Wire Mills 2nd eleven came a cropper at Gmppen- hall,the villagers claiming the victory by a majority of 17. F. Woodward (25) was the top scorer, S. Jakeman (12 not out) doing the best service for the losers.</p>
        <p>On Saturday the 'Warrington Cycling Club had an enjoyable run to Hunt's Cross. After the rain in the morning the weather was beautifully line and the roads were in excellent order. Seventeen members turned out and made their wayj via Crouton to HUnt's Cross. An excellent tea was provided by Host Hildritch and after several games of bowls had been indulged in the return journey was made through Hale and Ditton.</p>
        <p>Warrington Mersey .defeated 'Pletcher Russells' by a 40 runs margin H. Cross batting in good style for 39.</p>
        <p>Penketh were beaten on their own ground by Moore and Daresbury for whom S. Taylor bowled most I effectively, capturing seven wickets for 15 runs. C. R. All n and A. Antrobus were in grand form with the bat, each compiling 23.</p>
        <p>On Tuesday evening the weather was again ideal and sixteen members rode to Winwick via Fearnhead and Croft. Several interesting games of bowls were decided on the "Swan" green.</p>
        <p>Latchford Baptists were unable to account for St. Cuthbert's who proved victors by 11 runs. Chile batted exceptionally well for 22, whilst J. Callon rendered splendid service wita the ball, his analysis being six wickets for eight runs.</p>
        <p>Bowling. An unique main was held on the Cemetery Hotel green on Thursday afternoon. Mixed competitions for ladies and gentlemen are of fairly frequent occurrence, but there are few, if any, records of mains open exclu- sively to members of the fair sex, and in this respect the Cemetery members have set the example. The main on Thursday was largely in the nature of an experiment, being treated by many as a huge joke. The event was, however, successful beyond all anticipations, and so enjoyable an afternoon was spent that it was decided to afford the ladies an annual opportunity of figuring on the green.</p>
        <p>Thirty six ladies entered the competition and their trundling was of a much higher order than was to ba expected considering their in-experience. After close contests the first prize winner was Mrs. J. Willett who defeated Mrs. Allison 7-s.other prize winners in order o merit were Mrs. J, Berry, Mrs. J. Ruthwell, Mrs. A. J. Jones, Miss T. Jones, Mrs. Templeton, and Mrs. Wood. The first and second ladies were the two oldest taking partand the younger competitors have been subjected to a large amount of "chaff " for being defeated by their elders. It will be noticed that the majority of the successful ladies are wives of prominent bowlers connected with the club and it has been suggested that these gentlemen coached their better halves. One, whose name I purposely refrain from mentioning, candidly admitted that he had instructed his wife by means of marbles on the kitchen floor</p>
        <p>Daring the proceedings an excellent tea was served and the ladies were photographed by Mr. Birtles. Naturally there was a little speechmaking but this con- sisted chiefly of votes of thanks to the donors of prizes, Messrs. Allison, A. H. Crostleld, Foster, Willett, W. J. Shaw,Wood, Hill J. Newton, A. Taylor and Jones, and to the committee responsible for the excellent arrange- ments of which Mr.liaylwas the chairman andlilr. Wood the hon. sec. When the prize winners were announced a pleasing incident occurred, Mr. Cross, a spectator presenting Mrs. Allison with a boqouet.</p>
        <p>The championship of the Warrington District League is new assured, incidentally also the destination of the wooden spoon is placed beyond all doubt. The first named honour goes to the flu Is Head Club and the other distinction to the Penketh players. Of course the season is not by any means ended and accidents may liappen which will rob the present leaders of the ultimate occupation of that high position, but anything of the kind is hardly to be feared as the Church street men are a little to old in such contests to lose their heads over the success which they have so far achieved. It is hopeless to expect any other club to equal their excel- lent record, and unless the Church-street men are beaten on their own green—and on present form no Warrington club is equal to such a task—the other league teams will have to be content with merely a passing glimpse of the championship medals.</p>
        <p>Interest will therefore be chiefly centred in the struggle for the second position which lies between Sankey and Fearnhead, and it would be a difficult matter to anticipate which will succeed, though at pre- sent Sankey have the better record of the two. It is highly probable that at the end of the season the twoi clubs will be bracketed equal in regard to the matches won and as in that case the two points scored will decide, I venture to think, that Sankey will claim the advantage, though the Horse and Jockey club are also not by any means out of the running.</p>
        <p>Penketh Park have still to win their first match,and it would appear that even that task is beyond thern,matches on their own green having so far gone against them. A striking contrast is afforded by a comparison of their record with that of the league leaders. Out of a total of 72 games played Penketh have won 17, a percentage of 23.61, while Bull's Head have a percentage of games won of 75.</p>
        <p>The surprise of the seaso,) has been afforded by the Cemetery Club. Last season they carried everything</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-07-22_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>Local Athletic Successes.</head>
        <p>Local Athletic Successes.</p>
        <p>C.Berrv, the Warrington rider whose success in winn• lug the N.C.U. Championship (Manchester district) was noted last week, achieved two striking victories on Saturday, At Leeds Athletic sports he figured in the half and quarter mile races and secured first prize in each. In the first he had a strong rival in J. Crowther, a noted Leeds man, but managed to beat him by a couple of lengths. His time was 1 min. 33-ssec. Berry won the quarter mile by a length, after a fine race in 32 4-ssecs,—At Salford Police Sports, on Saturday, J. Houghton of Warrington. was second in the half-mile bicycle race, and M. Stephens, of Earleston, gained a similar place in the mile.—At Prescot Wireworks Sports on Saturday, H. Mounfield, of Warrington, was second in the two miles bicycle race.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-07-22_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>MOTOR NOTES.</head>
        <p>MOTOR NOTES.</p>
        <p>There is no doubt that Lancia's remarkable driving in the first two and a half rounds of the Gordon-Bennett race, coupled with the excellent positions attained by the drivers of the other two cars entered, has done quite as much good to the firm responsible for their manufacture as has Thery's victory achieved for the Richard-Brasier. It was pure ill-luck—the radiators irretrievably damaged by a stone thrown up from the road—that cost the cup for Italy.</p>
        <p>The showing made by the Mercedes cars came as a bitter disappointment. Hope centred round Jenatzy who wen the Trophy 1903 and lost it by the merest fraction the following year. But, until that memorable time in Ireland when he carried away the Cup from England, he had always had the most consistent bad luck in all his racing experiences, and it certainly seems as though, save on that one occasion, this ill-luck is s till to be his lot. Misfortunes, which in no way reflected either upon his skill as a driver or upon the design and mechanism of his racer, crowded thick and fast upon him from his very first start_in the Auvergne Circuit, and at length, culminating in a broken spring, forced him to retire early.</p>
        <p>England made a better show than we had dared to hope might be the case ' • but for many reasons it is obvious that she cannot hold her own with her con- tinental rivals, and it would really be better if the Automobile Club decided to stand down for the future and leave the Gordon Bennett Cup to be raced for by competitors who are not restricted by their laws to a speed of twenty miles an hour under any circumstancPs. So long as the speed limit is enforced, so long is it absolutely absurd for us to try to race successfully against those who can practise upon their own highways without any such legal restrictions to consider.</p>
        <p>At the same time we do not advocate that the high I roads should be haphazard as happy hunting grounds for racing motorists; far from it. But we believe that if the speed limit were removed and all offences arising from furious driving were to be dealt with under the old Highways Act, the alteration would he better for the public as well as for the drivers themselves. It is not the speed at which a car is driven but the manner in which it is driven that is really so often objectionable I and all those found guilty of driving in any manner dangerous to the public—it always being proved that 'someone has actually been in danger at the time— should be dealt with with the utmost severity. Conse- quently, there is no reason why in the early hours—say, at two or three o'clock in the morning—a speed sprint should not be indulged in, always bearing in mind that the few persons who would be on the road at that time must not be imperilled or inconvenienced, and that, if they should be. the law would ho still in a perfectly strong position for dealing with the offence committed.</p>
        <p>Letters from German settlers in the German colony of Csmeroons, West Africa, published in the " Colonial Gazette," state that a native tribe known as the N 3 erns killed and ate no fewer than 2,000 negroes and eight Gorman settlers within a month.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-07-22_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
