      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-07-22_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>'The Examiner, Saturday, July 22, 1905.</head>
        <p>'The Examiner, Saturday, July 22, 1905.</p>
        <p>EDUCATION COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>THE SALARIES OF SECONDARY SCHOOL TEACHERS.</p>
        <p>Proposed Appointment of Matron.</p>
        <p>THE QUESTION OF PHYSICAL INSTRUCTION.</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting of the Warrington Education Committee was held at the Town Hall on Nlonday even- ing, under the presidency of Alderman Bennett. There were also present :—Alderman Pierpoint (vice-chair- man), the Mayor (Alderman Bolton), the Revs. Canon Morley Stevenson, John Yonge, and Father Wilson, Alderman Tinnion, Councillors James Evans, Sankey, Wilkinson, Fulton Smith, Brongh, Ellison, Maginn, Hulse and Lord, Messrs. J. Upson, C. E. Parker, H. Roberts, E. Plinston, A. J. Jolley, J. D. Buckton, R. T. Fairclough, H. Kingdon, W. Bibby, T. May, and J. Moore Murray (secretary and director). SECONDARY SCHOOL SALARIES. THE PROPOSED APPOINTMENT OF MATRON. At a meeting of the School Management Committee as Governors of the Secondary school, which had been convened at the request of the Board of Education, the following of H.M. Inspectors ware present, viz. :—Miss A. D. Crosby, Mr. LI. S. Lloyd, Mr. D. A. Macnanghton, Mr. H. Allport, and Mr.A. R. Smith. From the remarks of the Inspectors, it appeared that they were, on the whole, satisfied with the work of the school. Several matters were mentioned by them, the majority of which were already receiving the attention of the Governors. In regard to the remainder, the Governors undertook to give them their careful consideration. On the recommendation of the sub-committee appointed to consider the matters, it was resolved That the following be adopted as the scale of salaries for the Headmaster and the assistant teachers in the Secondary school and Technical Institute, viz : Commencing salary. Maximum. Head master 3OO ... 400 Physics, Chemistry, and Mechanical masters Assistant masters (graduates) 120 ... 15.5 Senior assistant mistress 125 ... 140 Assistant mistress (graduates) 100 ... 125 f 1 /f Manual Instructor</p>
        <p>Position.</p>
        <p>That the year ending the Ist August and the Ist June, 1905, respectively, be counted as a year of service to Ntr. E. S. A. Robson, M.Sc., headmaster, and Mr. Paul Fisher, B. Sc., Chemistry master ; and that their salaries be increased as follows, viz :—Mr. E. S. A. Robson, M.Sc.' from £250 to £300; Mr. Paul Fisher, B. Sc., from £l4O to £l5O. That a senior assistant mistress be appointed, who shall be responsible for the health and care of the girls in the Secondary school and who shall act generally ss the matron of the school; that Miss Elizabeth Frost be appointed senior assistant mistress at the Secondary school and Technical Institute, and that her salary be increased, in accordance with the scale from £ll5 to £125 per annum, as from the Ist August, 1905; that in consequence of Mr. H. 0. Armstrong (the Manual Instructor at the Secondary schhol and Technical Institute) being engaged in the evening beyond the time specified in his agreement, the same be amended; and that his salary be increased from £ll5 to £125 pet annum' as from the Ist August, 1903. That the following appointments for the occasional staff at the Technical Institute for the coming session be made at the salaries stated viz :—Mr. George 'Carruthers, commercial arithmetic, £27 65.; Mr. Herbert W. Porritt, book-keeping, commercial law and routine £42; Mr. Francis C. Herzog, French and German, £52; Mr. George J. Brown, shorthand, £39; Mr. Cesar Ojel. Spanish. £lB Mrs. Mary Dobson, dressmaking and Imillinery, £39; Mr. Alfred N. Legge, shorthand. £l3; Mr. Isaac C. Pharaoh, book-keeping, £l3; Dr. A. Hodgkinson, physiology and hygiene, £49 10s. ; Dr. G. H. Leigh, sick nursing. £l6 16s. ; Mr. Frank Lowe. chemistry, £42; Mr. Frederick G. Ruddock, iron and steel and metallurgy, £27 ; Mr. F. Austin Blockev, tin- ning, £1 is. per lesson and travelling expenses •, Mr. W. A. Littlejohn, steam and applied mechanics, £42: Mr. William A. Stansfleld, mathematics.£24 10s. ; Mr. James Young, mathematics, £24 10s. ; Mr. Thomas Hughes, plumbing, £l9 12s. ; Mr. W. Cobb, plumbing, £ll us. •, Mr. David Reekie. Nature-study, £1 is. per lesson with travelling and other expenses. scl that'</p>
        <p>Mr. BITCKTON : I would have said exactly the same [lt wainoted that, hi' this arrangement, several new classes could be formed, and that a saving of £177 of the expression "unnecessary criticism" as of the Rs. in the salaries of the occasional staff, as compared with expression "cavilling." those paid last session, could be effected.] I Mr. ROBERTS : lam not responsible for any impres- That an additional assistant master, qualified to sion Mr. Buckton likes to take from remarks of mine, teach engineering subjects, he appointed to the but lam not going to allow him to describe in language Secondary school end Technical Institute, at a nom- I which is unjust and unfair any observations of mine or mencing salary in accordance with the scale set forth anyone else. above, viz :-2140 per annum. I Alderman PIERPOINT said it was absolutely necessary As Canon Morley Stevenson, the chairman of the that a matron should be appointed. The impression committee, had not arrived at the commencement of conveyed to him by some speakers was that a little act the meeting, the adoption of the minutes was formally of favouritism was alleged, and he should therefore moved by Mr. UPSON. support the amendment. Councillor FULTON SMITH seconded. The Rev. Father Wilms; did not think any act of Councillor EvANS asked for information regarding favouritism had been perpetrated, and he did not see what could be gained by referring the matter back. The the proposed appointment of a senior mistress to act isa q matron of the Secondary School. The matter appearedualifications of the three teachers were so similar to him to be a little bit outside the question of Educe- that the only alternative course' he could suggest was tion and he did not know whether the committee was that they should "toss up" for the appointment acting within its province in creating such an office. In (Laughter.) the absence of information he would move an amend- The CHAIRMAN said he was quite sure there had been merit that the recommendation be not confirmed, no favouritism. Mr. PLINSTON seconded on the ground that there were The amendment that the matter be referred two other mistresses whose claims to the appointment back for reconsideration was carried by 12 votes to 10. should be carefully considered. The MAYOR said he would like to know why:the Canon MORLEY STEVENSON explained that the recom- other proposed increases of salary should be given. mendation arose out of the visit paid by certain in I Canon M. STEVENSON said that the committee had had spectors to the secondary school, in which they met th lan interview with ths inspector though nothing appeared governors and gave them a very long report And laid on the minutes as the interview was private. In the down the law very strongly on certain points. The corn- , first place the inspector said they were paying their mittee had had that report under very careful considers- I headmaster far too little and pointed out that it would tion and one of the results was the recommendations be impossible for them to keep a good man at the salary now being considered. The first presents 4 the nec AI which they offered, and that they would not keep the sity and advisability of having in mixed present official unless they paid him a greater salary, school some lady among the teachers who should They thought they could only act upon that advice. be somewhat responsible for the care, health, The salary they were paying was much below that paid t• discipline, and manners of the girls. He did not thin ,in other towns. They had a most excellent headmaster it was necessary to labour that point,for they would all ' and one who was throwing himself energetically see thenecessity for such an appointment. There were , into the work of the school the organization three ladies in the schonl on one of whom the choice lof which was now perfect. He had in fact spared the must fall and the committee had considerable difficulty committee a very considerable amount of expense by in making that choice. After a full discussion it was , organizing the school and it would be a thousand pities decided all but unanimously—there was one dissentient if they allowed him to leave because his salary was not —to appoint Miss Frost. The difficulty would not occur adequate. The inspector also impressed upon them that again for when the post became vacant it would be , the scale for the assistant teachers was not adequate thrown open and advertised. I and that unless they could look forward to a maximum The Rev. JOHN YONGE, as the one dissentient at the I they would leave for anothpr school where they Committee meeting, was still strongly opposed to the I would get a few pounds mere. They had lost commendation. He supposed; he would be debarred Ino less than five assistant teachers which was by the stand'ng order from moving an amendment ,in itself against the interest of the school that the question be referred back for further I and was penny wise pound foolish." An arrange- so he suggested that Canon Morley merit had been made whereby £1.77 was saved in salaries should withdraw it with that understanding. which NM very satisfactory and would do to set against arrange- consideration, s Stevenson iderat i on, innttelide ocuotursthe that tf tfurtherhe wording discussion, o f t hMe r. standingH. ROBERTSo orderseE ;the increases given to the staff. They had decided to po I engage an additional master to teach engineering sub. would admit of an amendment in the terms mentioned ijects at a salary of .£l4O. The whole of that sum by Mr. Yong e, and it was decided by the proposer and would not be expended as from it would have to be to alter the amendment to that effect. seconder deducted the amounts received in grants and in school Mr. MAY said he heartily approved of the substance fees. Even then there would be a considerable saving of the two resolutions, but he knew nothing about the in salaries. relative merits of the ladies mentioned, He agreed with the appointment of a matron for the reason that he had received complaints from parents of children attending the school owing to circumstances which had arisen. it seemed to him entirely necessary that there should be such a mistless to supervise the manners and morals of the girls. Mr. R. T. FAIRCLOITGH said they had not heard any reason why the recommendation should be sent back. Was there any further information tq 'give to the committee? Councillor EvANS said he did not know anything about the details of the creation of this new office. The CHAIRMAN: The committee have endeavoured through the Chairman, to give that information. The MAYOR said he wanted to know the reason for the increasing of the salaries of secondary school teachers. Why were the teachers entitled to so much more now than they were before? The Education Committee seemed to do things in such a way that the Council would sooner or later have to put a check on the amount of money they were spending as compared with the results obtained. If the committee were going to earn the money and show the Town Council that they were getting value for it, the Council would be perfectly satisfied and willing to support them. Until they did that the Council must act with a certain amount of suspicion as to whether the committee were not going a little bit too fast fo: the amount of education that was being given. Mr. H. ROBERTS said it seemed to him that the reasons which had actuated the Committee in making the propesition were perfectly obvious. The care of the girls was the object of the passing of the resolution, and every member of the Committee who happened to be the father of girls would understand, without any discussion, the necessity for the appointment. The only difference there would be amongst them was as to who should be appointed. It appeared to him that if the work of sub-committees submitted to that committee for consideration was to be constantly made the subject for reference back again for reconsideration, the time wasted, to say nothing of the temper wasted, would be very considerable .The sub-committee, he thought,were entitled to the support and confidence of the committee in their proposition ; otherwise, it seemed that they were going to make that general committee meeting the fighting ground for dis- cussing in detail matters which wore in themselves of trifling importance, overlooking, at the same time, greater principles which were involved. Councillor Manusx said he presumed Miss Frost would only be engaged at school for the same number of hours as hitherto. If so, he would like to be told for what purpose she was to receive .£lO a year extra. He was sorry to find the increases of salary brought up at this stage. Originally they were told that salaries would only be considered in December ; then that</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-07-22_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <p>February should be the month. Now it seemed that intimated that the School Management Committee were every month of the year was the right date for increases. willing to discuss the scheme, but there must be the It wa§ not to this particular lady's salary alone that he broad principle that they should have control of the objected. He was opposed to the other recommended instruction. His olive branch, however, was rejected increases under the present conditions of trade. He with scorn and contempt. (Laughter). was told privately that the increases had been granted Mr. H. ROBERTS said he thought Alderman Tinnion on the recommendationS of the inspector. He had had no case. At his request, conveyed by Councillor always thought that it was the duty of the inapector to Evans, the committee at the last meeting withdrew a look after the education of the children and work of resolution on the matter on the distinct understanding the schools and not to dictate to the committee what that the Gymnasium Committee were prepared to dis- salaries they should pay. If for no other reason be cuss the matter. The responsibility for re-opening the would oppose the recommendations on the ground of negotiations therefore rested upon Alderman Tinnion at dictation from the inspector, r whose instigation the proposition was withdrawn. The CHAIRMAN: There is no dictation at all. ! Alderman TINNION: You ought to know I have had Councillor Maorms said he quite understood that it no opportunity. was necessary to have a matron but he failed to see that Mr. ROBERTS said a month had elapsed since the corn- the work was increased to the extent of £lO a year. mittee withdrew the proposition and the matter could Alderman TINNION said he did not see why any one not wait indefinitely. It did not appear to he clearly particular teacher should be appointed for this purpose. understooil that the alteration in the conditions of He took it that in a school like the Secondary School, employment of teachers put the responsibility of teach- with girls of that age,it was part of the duties of all the lug physical exercises quite as much as the teaching of teachers to attend to the health and care of the girls, anything else upon the qualified and paid teachers Why were the committee giving an increasa of salary to ' employed by the committee. a teacher for doing what was the bounden duty of every Alderman TINNION : his not usual. teacher who had charge of girls? Mr. ROBERTS : It does not matter whether it is Mr. JOLLEY : This is a mixed school, usual or not; it is the fact, and it has been particularly Alderman TINNION : If it is a mixed school, theladies emphasised by the Board of Education in its minutes will have charge of the girls. this year. Continuing he said he quite admitted that Mr. JOLLEY : Men have charge of some classes, everything that Alderman Tinnion claimed for the Alderman TINNION contended that it was unnecessary gymnasium he was fully entitled to claim, but it was to appoint anyone Specially to do the work. There was , a question as to whether it had not outlived a tendency on the part of the committee to lavishly i its necessity in regard to the physical instruction of increase salaries. They went as far as they could by the the school children, seeing that the particulars scale and it seemed to him that they had a delight in of the appointment of teachers required increasing the salaries of various teachers, that they should be qualified to give that The MAYOR: Some of them. instruction. All the Education Committee had asked Alderman TINNION : Others, of course, have a difft- for was that the children of three schools should have culty in getting their just rights, as we know, their exercises taught in the Gymnasium. They had Mr. J. D. RUCKTON did not see how it was possible both the material to teach and the places in which to for an assistant mistress who was teaching in one class teach in regard to the other schools. to look after the girls who were being taught by a Alderman TINNION : You have not the capability. master in another class. The committee were asked Jr.l ROBERTS: That again is a matter of opinion. to adopt the proposal solely on the recommendation of Continuing, he said the question really was whether the the inspectors. It had been said that it was extreme teachers should do the work or whether the committee to be always cavilling at the minutes of committees should pay for it twice over. So far as the schools in without just cause. Still the object of that meeting question were concerned, if the Gymnasium Committee was to confirm or otherwise the proceedings of the sub- were prepared to accept a reasonable payment to pro. committees, and if the committee were not to call them vide accommodation and to do what he thought was in question and amend them if necessary, it was useless incumbent upon the gymnasium if it was to supply a holding a meeting, public need—that was to assist the Education Committee Mr. UPSON said the only other solution of the in regard to the teachers who were not yet qualified to difficulty which faced the committee was the separation give the instruction,—he thought the School Manage- of the sexes and the establishment of two distinct ment Committee would be quite willing to fall in with schools. Unless they solved it in some such way as the arrangement. The question was merely one of they proposed to do, they were faced with the fact that finance and not of administration. It was all humbug they had a school in which there were girls from the for Alderman Tinnion to tell them that he was so age of twelve to nearly twenty and boys of similar ages, particularly jealous of the character of the physical and there were only two married mon on the staff, instruction given to the scholars. Difficulties were therefore created which he as the Alderman TINNION : I have made more sacrifices for father of girls attending the school had felt very keenly. it than you bade, by a great deal. A protest oirlht to be made against what Alderman Mr. H. ROBERTS: We are not discussing any. 9aes- - . -</p>
        <p>.. protest oug— ue made ...inst wha. Tinaion had said about the committee being anxious to lavishly increase salaries. He found the committee was very loath to do anything of the sort,—so far as some teachers were concerned, at any rate—and it was with very great difficulty that justice was obtained for some teachers. Alderman Tintrox : I qualified it and said some. Mr. UPSON, continuing, said the teacher who was appointed to the position of matron had added to her duties perhaps nothing longer in time, but she bad added to them a considerable amount of responsibility, and responsibility was the one thing that was paid for in this world. He contended that £lO a year would be a very small reward for the extra duties. With regard to the appointment itself, he thought that by discussing it in committee some arrangement satisfactory to all parties might be brought about. 0 Mr. A. J. JOLLEY said one point had not been men- tioned by any speaker with regard to the duties and re- sponsibilities which would devolve upon the matron,and that was to be in attendance on the sports flelds,which he thought was a very desirable thing. The Committee had not increased the salary by £lO a year but by £5, for accnrding to the scale in operation Miss Frost's salary would be automatically increased to £l2O. Mr. Buck- ton had said the appointment was made solely on the recommendation of the Inspector. That was quite erroneous. It was made after recommendation of the Inspector, but most assuredly not solely on account of it, nor was there in any sense an attempt to dictate to the Committee. A very little reflection would convince anyone that an appointment of that kind in an institu- tion where they were trying to carry on co-education economically, was very necessary. It should be borne in mind that an absolute saving was being effected by the re-organization of the Technical Institute Classes. Mr. H. ROBERTS said he never made use of the word " cavil " in alluding to the discussion, and he did not like to be accused of such language by Mr. l3uckton. What he referred to was unnecessary criticism. The MAYOR : You are splitting a hair. Mr. ROBERTS: It is snore than a hair.</p>
        <p>tion of personal sacrifice, but I am quite prePared (f the time comes to show that even I can claim to have made a few personal sacrifices in the interests of the people of this town.— Alderman TINNION : Not of physical instruction. Mr. ROBERTS : Probably with a longer record than my good friend Alderman Tinnion. Mr. A. J. JOLLEY thought that if the members of the Gymnasium Committee had shown the same amount of courtesy as the Mayor had shown when they met the Town Tall, Park and Baths Committee in regard to the question of free bathing, a satisfactory solution would have been arrived at months ago. The deliberations with the Gymnasium Committee had been made as difficult as possible (Alderman Tinnion " I deny that ") Alderman Tinnion's denial would not alter the factif the amendment were carried itlwould mean that the children would get no physical instruction at all. They contended that the instruction could be given properly on the school premises. He strongly repndiated any suggestion of influence by the inspector. The blame for the failure of the attempt to provide for the physical instruction of tho children at the gymnasium rested with the members of the Gymnasium Committee and not with the School Management Committee. Mr. UPSON said the inspector had refused to sanction the arrangement which had been made to take the school children to the baths duringschool hours, and the proba- bility was that if they arranged for the children to attend the gymnasium during schoolhours the Inspector would put his foot down and would net let them go. The MAYOR: The inspector is not dictator to the town. Mr. UPSON: The inspector has control of the grants which amounted to £30,000 per annum and if he is not to dictate he will not pay. He said the children must not go to the baths during school hours and they did not go. It is desirable if possible that the children should receive their physical instruction in their o tyn schools and play grounds. The Rev. N. WILSON: The Mayer said it did hot matter what the charge was because it was only putting the out of one pocket into another. In that</p>
        <p>ae money , case payment might be 'dispensed with and we might use the gymnasium as much as we like free of charge. The MAYOR: If you went the right way about it there would be no objection. Canon MORLEY STEVENSON said that when the question was adjourned at the last meeting of the committee it was upon the understanding that fresh negotiations should take place. They had heard nothing more since then. Their present arrangement with the Gymnasium Committee terminated at the end of the present month and as nothing had been done they had to do something with the children. The agreement with the Gymnasium Committee terminated and not being in a position to enter into a fresh one their hands were forced. The Gymnasium Committee refused to agree to any scheme which did not provide for the co-opera- tion and the assistance&amp; the committee and its officers. Alderman TINNION : The instruction could be given by your teachers but we should superintend. CANON STEVENSON: The great majority of our teachers are perfectly competent to give instruotion and I dent think they should be put in the position of being superintended by anyone else. They would not like it, being capable of drilling the children themselves. If the Gymnasium Committee is prepared to meet us we are prepared to meet them. We want the schools near to the gymnasium to be drilled in the building. The MAYOR: I don't see any. objection. Canon STEVENSON, continuing, said there were teachers who were older and who had not had the oppor- tunity of qualifying to give physical 'instruction. It would have been useful if the Gymnasium Committee bad taken them in hand and called formed so that they might be given the opportunity of qualifying. They were bound to take the steps. suggested in the resolu- tion as their agreement terminated. Alderman BENNETT thought the Education Committee had been reasonable, an I had all along been in the right. Alderman TINNION said he would withdraw his amend- ment, though he was still opposed to the principle. There was nothing to prevent some arrangement being entered into, provided the School Management Com- mittee did not want matters all their own way. ALTERATION IN CURRICULUM. The code of regulations for public elementary school and the regulations for evening schools, technical institutions, and schools of art, for the year 1903-6, were submitted, and it was resolved (a) that sewing be no longer taught to childreti under seven years of age in the infants schools; (b) that shorthand be no longer' taught in any of the public elementary schools; and (c) that the attention of the head teachers be calbd to the teaching of English and handwriting with a view to the standard of attainment in those subjects being improved. The minutes were approved. THE POSITION OF INFANTS. At a meeting of the School Attendance Committee the managers of the Grappenhall-road (infants') Council school recommended that, for the present, children under four years of age be not admitted to the school. The managers of the St. George's (infants') Council school recommended that, for the present, no children under five years of age be admitted to the school. It was resolved that the recommendations of the managers be approved. • Councillor Etrasox moved the adoption of the minutes. Mr. MAY referred to the death of Mr. Scarisbrick by which he said they had lost the services of an estim- able gentleman and one who was held in high regard. (Hear, hear.) He thought it was taking a retrograde step to exclude young children from the school as was proposed. It would be more reasonable to provide proper accommodation for those children than to thrust them on to the streets. Alderman SENNETT associated himself with Mr.' May's remarks with reference to the late Mr. Scarisbrick. The minutes were approved. . THE NEW SCHOOLS. At a meeting of the Sites, and Buildings Committee the Surveyor reported the result of the enquiries he had made, in accordance with the instructions of the Committee at their last meeting in regard to the material tO be usel for the play grounds of the new schools to be erected by the Committee, and it was resolved that the architeet for .the erection of the " Beamont " Council should be instructed to provide in his specification fortligi laying of tbe playgrounds in accordance with the veers of the Surveyor, at a cost not exceeding Ss. 3id. per square yard. The detailed specification prepared by the Architect for the "Beamont " Council school was submitted and was referred to the Surveyor and the Directer for approval. It was then resolved that the architect for the erection of the "Beamont" Council school be instructed to prepare the working drawings and the bifls of quantities; and that, when the same have been approved by the Surveyor and the Director, the neces- sary advertisements be issued inviting tenders for the erection of the school in accordance therewith. The tenders of Messrs. William Winstanley and Messrs. S. and F. Acton for painting and cleaning a number of schools were accepted. It was also resolved, that in accordance with a previous resolution, Mr. John Hesketh be appointed to supervise the carrying out of the annual cleaning and painting of the schools at a cost of Li. The tenders received for the proposed extension of the lavatory accommodation at the Secondary, school having been opened, and the lowest amount having been found to be above the estimated cost of carrying out the work, it was resolved that the Surveyor be instructed to submit an smended specification for this work to the two contractors whose tenders were the lowest.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-07-22_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>PHYSICAL INSTRUCTION TO BE GIVEN AT THE SCHOOLS BY THE TEACHERS.</head>
        <p>PHYSICAL INSTRUCTION TO BE GIVEN AT THE SCHOOLS BY THE TEACHERS.</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN reported to the School Management Committee that, at the last meeting of the Education Committee, he had, at the request of the Chairman of the Gymnasium Committee, withdrawn the resolution of the committee with reference to the proposed use of the gymnasium by school children. It was resolved that instructions be given to the managers and head teachers of the Trinity National (mixed), Warrington British (mixed), and Secondary schools that the necessary physical instruction must, after the 31st July next, be given at the schools by the teachers. Aldermen TIXXIOK moved an amendment that the minute be not confirmed. He said the decision of the School Management Committee was in keeping with the whole of the treatment the Gymnasium. Committee had received in the matter. When they asked a reasonable remuneration for their services they were dispensed with, and not in a very gentlwor kind spirit either. Having dealt with the early negotiations, Alderman Tinnion said the Education Com- mittee added insult to injury by offering the ridiculous sum of £3O for the use of the Gymnasium and services of the Instructor. He contended that it was impossible for the physical instruction to be properly given in the rooms or school yard at Holy Trinity and Warrington British schools. The Headmaster of the latter school had reported to the Managers that it would be a physical impossibility to give the instruction in anything like the way required, and the Headmistress of Holy Trinity school had said practically the same thing. The MAYOR seconded. Some of the teachers, he said, did not know how to give the instruction, because they themselves bad not been taught. When the Corporation had suitable buildings and all the necessary appliances, then ought to be used. The benefits that would accrue from instruction in the Gymnasium would be much greater than would result from exercises given in heated rooms by novices. Mr. C. E. PARKER supported the minutes. Alderman TINNION bad rather mistaken that attitude of the School Management Committee. If there had been any shutting of a door in a committee's face, it had been on the part of the Gymnasium Committee for when the use of the gymnasium was first suggested, that committee or the Town Council passed a resolution that they could not agree to any scheme which did not provide for the co-operation and assistance of the Gymnasium Committee and its officers. That was practically saying, "If you don't take our plan of teaching the children you shall have nothing." Be thought that if the Gymnasium Committee were wilting to re-discuss the question the School Management Committee would meet them. The CHAIRMAN said that at the Council meeting he</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-07-22_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <p>Alderman BENNET asked whether it was not falso. economy to keep the estimates down. The MAYOR, in reply, stated that it was found in some cases that they would have to match elaborate yellow tiling upon which money had been very lavishly expended, and which had made the school the dearest in the town it having cost nearly £lOO per head per scholar. If the Chairman would give them the money they would be pleased to do the work in the particular style which he desired. The minutes were approved. STOCK AND SHARE MARKETS</p>
