        <p>THE EXAMINER. JULY 22nd 1905.</p>
        <p>According to the Mayor, owing to money being "lavishly expended" in the past, the Teabsicat Institute has cost the town nearly £lOO per head for each scholar in attendance. The Committee are now earnestly economising on a new lavatory ! * * * "Mr. Balfour does not think there will be riots next winter. If lam alive then I am going to make the hungry feel the power they possess.' These pregnant words from Mr, Ben Tillett, coupled with Mr. Gavan Duffy's advice to "raid the shops" indicate in un- mistakable fashion that the workers mean to stand no more nonsense from the Government. Probably the speakers were carried away by the resentment they feel at the sacrifice of the Unemployed Bill, but the fact is plain that last winter's privations will not be again endured by the unemployed without some desperate effort being made to obtain a recognition of governmental and local responsibility for the care of the out-of-works and thsir families. Internal trouble is, just now, the canker at the beartof Eng- land's greatness,</p>
