        <p>Defrauding the Railway Company. At the Warrington Police Court yesterday (Friday), t Daniel Marsh, draper's assistant, Manchester, was fined 7s. and costs, for travelling from Manchester to Warrington on the Cheshire Lines Railatay - without having paid his fare.—lt appears that when asked for , his ticket Marsh said he had lost the return half. He gave his name but declined to give his address for private reason. I Music in the Park. lOn Thursday evening next the Leglstreet Bmss 1 Band will occupy the stand in the park and will 'render the following programme. Mr. Fred Pochnore ' conducting :—March, "Veterans," - Carter; overture, "Caliph ca.:Bagdad," Boieldieu ,• selection, "Soldiers chorus," Gounod; waltz, "Choristers," Phelps; selection, " Stradella," Flotow ; march, " Evertonian," Greenwood; "God save the King." Death of a Well-known Farmer. A noted Cheshire farmer in the person of Mr. Richard Clarke, Warburton Park Farm,,Warburton, died on Wednesday night, aged 72. 'Mr. Clarke had farmed on the Arley estate all his life, and only retired two years ago. As a breeder of dairy cattle and shorthorns he had a wide reputation,and won scores of prizes in the show ring. For 50 years he was a member of the .Cheshire Yeomanry, and three times won the cup as the best swordsman in the regiment. Accident to Dr. Smith. _ Owing to improvements in Bridge-street Messrs. Hodgkinson shop has been set back. The curbstones denoting the boundary of the old footpath have not however been removed and a somewhat serious acci- dent resulted from their present dangerous position Dr. Smith was riding down the street on a bicycle and when passing a cab collided with the curb, and fell heavily, being . rendered unconscious for a few minutes. He was taken home by Inspector Row- botham and P. S. Dean, apart'from the shock he was, happily, uninjured. New Pastor of Risley. On Thursday the Rev. Glover, who has been transferred from Whalley Range, Blackburn, was inducted to the pastorate of -Risley Presbyterian Church, rendered vacant by the sudden death of the Rev. W. Dunn. A largo congregation attended the service which was conducted by the Moderntor of the Manchester Presbytery. Risley Churah, it is interest- ing to note, was built under the Five Mile-Act which forbade " obstreperous " Nonconformist clergy to come within five miles of a town. Musical Successes. The following pupils of Mr. T.,Boothroyd 9, Suez- street, Warrington, have successfully passed the lecent examination of the International Union of Musicians, London. Miss Minnie Stanley has gained 'the silver medal of I.V.M. far Mandoline playing. Sbe lis also an associate of tho 1.V.M., Miss Stanley has ' now passed the final as there are na higher examina- tions in this subject. Mr. Thomas Blake has been elected an Associate of the I.V.M. for violin. Miss Lucie Law has gained the bronze medal for violin playing and Miss Elsie Dawson has passed the intermediate grade for mandoline. H. M. ASHTON'S LIFE TONIC takes away that tired feeling.—(Advt.) , , Theft of Newspapers. , At the Warrington Police Court. yesterday (Friday), a lad named Joseph Humphreys, 12 Philip-street, was charged with stealing a parcel of newspapers, the roperty of the Cheshire Lines Committee.—The evidence showed that four parcels of " Chronicles " arrived at Warrington and three were plrced on a truck, to he forwarded to other towns. Prisoner took one of the parcels and left the station. He was followed by a porter and when caught had opened the parcel and vraa selling the papers. Prisoner, who had been twice before the magistrates for selling papers Iwithout wearing his badge, was ordered to receive five strokes from the birch. Warrington Police Complimented. The following letter has been received by the Chief Constable (Mr. Luke Talbot) from the Chief Constable of Salford (Mr. J. W. Hallam) :—" I beg to express to you my sincere thanks for your kindness in furnishing me with 33 members of your force under your command on the occasion of the visit of their tfaiesties the King and Queen to open the new dock, and to unveil the war memorial in this borough on the 13th instant, and I desire to convey my highest ' appreciation of the admirable way in which Chief Inspector Phillips, officers, and constables under his command performed their duties. Their conduct was , praiseworthy, their appearance smart, and the service ;they rendered valuable. Oblige me by conveying to ' Chief Inspector Phillips and his detachment my high lappreciation of his services." Death of Mrs. J. C. Young. • . We record with regret the death of Mrs. Young, wife of Mr. J. Claikson Young, which took place at her residence, No. 1, Palmyra-square, on Tuesday. The deceased lady, who was in her 77th year, was widely known and highly respected, and her death will be generally and deeply regretted. The funeral took place at the Warrington Cemetery on Thursday, the officiating minister being the Rev. C. H. Cook. The chief mourners were: Mr. J. C. Yonne, Mr. and Mrs. J. Rymer Young, Mr. and Mrs. H. Young (sons and daughters-in-law) and Miss Taylor, and amongst those also present were Mr. J. F. Dutton, Mr. C. White, Miss White, Dr. Peacock, Mrs. J. L. Tunstall, Mrs. T. Tunstall, Mr. Whittle. Air. L. Greening, Mr. Hill, Mr. Wehs, Mr. and Mrs. J. Langland. Mr. P. Silcosk, Mrs. Cook, Mr. Peake (Earlestown), Mrs. Captain Deacon, Mrs. W. Hmghton (Penketh) and Miss Hunt. Floral tributes were sent by the family, Mr. J. F. Dutton, Mr. and Mrs. J. P. Hill, Mrs. T. Tunetall and family, Mr. F.W. Monks, Mr. and Mrs. John Fairclough, Mr. W. S. Dutton, "Nora," "Two old friends," "Annie and Emma," Miss Hunt, Mrs, William Houghton, Mr. and Mrs. T. B. Carter, Dr. and Mrs. Peacocke, Mr. James Wood and family, Mrs. J. L. Tunstall, Mary E. Greening, Sankey-street staff, Mr. and Mrs. A. Peake, Miss Houghton (Sankey), and the Rev. and Mrs. C. H. Cook. H. M. ASHTON'S LIFE Toxic takes away that tired feel ing.— (Ad v t.) Poor Children's Summer Camp. To-day (Saturday) 115 of Warringtoa'a Poorest girls leave by the 10.20 train for Preatatya where they will remain for a week. They will be under the charge of Mrs. Wilkinson who has obtained the services of a number of excel,lent assistants. On the following Saturday an equal number of girls will be sent to take the place of those who will return, and then the boys will have a turn for the succeeding fornight. At the last meeting of the Executive Com- mittee, held under the presidency of Mr. Gs W. Armi- tage, it was resolved that the best thanks of the com- mittee be accorded to Mr. Murray and his staff for the great assistance rendered by them to the committee for their investigations of suitable cases for camp. Arrangements were also made for the necessary medical examination, and the Hon. Secretary (Mr. Jenkins) having ieterviewed Mr. Charles White, that gentlersan consented to act as inspecting medical officer at any time that the Medical Officer was unable to act, owing to his official engagements. Mr. Frank Booth of Sankey-street, has sent a large box contain- ing different kinds of hats and caps for the children. Given fine weather, there is no doubt that the annual trip to s the seaside will be beneficial, and will be greatly enjoyed. Police Court and Prison Gate Mission Work. A meeting was held on Thursday afternoon on the lawn attached to Bruche Hall, in aid of the funds of the Police Court and Prison Gate Mission and the Juvenile Work of the Diocesan C.E.T.S. In the absence of the Rev. W. Pollock Hill, the Rector pre- sided, and there were also present : Captain W. Withington, Lieutenant-Colonel J. D. Buckton, Revs. T. A. G. Ridpath, J. Hirst, and G. C. Briggs, Mr. H. Taylor, P.C.M., and Mr. J. Critchley, —Captain Withington, in an interesting address. explained how the Mission had grown out of the Prisoners' Aid Society, and had for 23 years been carried on with great success and done a good work.—The Rev. J. Hirst explained that when first established the mission had one missioner. It now had 26 representatives who attended the different courts and petty sessions held in the diocese. At first the work was mainly directed against intemperance but it had now been extended and useful work was being done in many directions. tinfortun- , ately they were always behind financially and he earnestly appealed for greater support, in the work.— Mr. H. Taylor gave an interesting resume of the work of the Mission in Warrington. During the time he had been in the town 150 girls and women, and 100 boys and men had been given to his care by the magis- trates. He thanked the magistrates and the Chief Constable for their interest and assistance in the work.—Mr. Critchley also addressed the meeting.— Tea was served on the lawn and a collection was taken on. behalf of the funds of the Mission.</p>
