        <p>WEEKLY REPORT ON THE HEALTH OF WARRINGTON, WEEK ENDING JULY 15th, 1905. During the week, the following births and dent.h..t were registered :—Warrington Registration Distr% births, 45, deaths, 16. The excess of births over deatn was 29. The birth-rate was equal to 338. The death-rate from all causes was equal to 120 11 1,000 against 17'0 the average of the previous 4 weeks,llllo' against DI the corresponding period of last yea death rate from the seven chief zymotic diseases was I:Abe to 15 per 1,000, against 29 the average rate i•og previous 4 weeks and against 15 the corresponni period of 1905. The ages of mortality were: rot 0-1 1-5 5-15 15-25 25-65 65—up. 6022 1 2 3 'fro], The deaths included-1 from measles, !sr diarrhrea, 2 from phthisis, 2 from other tubeloo diseases, 3 from lung diseases, 1 from accident, 6 all other causes. been The following cases of infectious disease have ‘,„s ported during the wee:: :—Scarlet fever, 4 phthisis, 1 case. diag Total number of patients in hospital, week on I July 15th, 1905, 36. J. GUEST GOBNALL, ,tb, Medical Officer of Warrington.—Printed and publshed by JOHN &amp; Co., at The Examiner General Printiq, oral Bewsey-street, and published by them at tu'.a_eorP place, and also at The ExernainerGffices, Victor,l_rk ete WIDNES: 32, George-street, ST. HELENS: 3, I.ll'` j elf street, EARLESTOWN; Church-street RUNCOP' 22nd. 1905.</p>
